.man.registerFile ("backfill_state.q";"";".qbfstate";"backfill_state.q - the bounded worker lifecycle contract (.qbfstate). Implements requirements ETL-01 to ETL-05 of docs/reference/etl-framework-requirements.md. Note those IDs belong to the REQUIREMENTS document; the question bank uses an overlapping E-nn scheme for a different set of questions about source adapters. Where a question-bank answer is cited below it is named as such. The distinction this file exists to enforce (ETL-02): a BOUNDED worker takes an explicit [range_from;range_to) request, a resumable run specification, a private state file, and has a terminal completion path. That is an enforced contract, checked at init. a CONTINUOUS worker is a long-running poll-and-cursor loop. That is an established pattern with NO registry and no contract (ETL-03), and the asymmetry is deliberate - see docs issue ETL-22/ETL-24. \"A bounded worker must not silently become an unbounded tailer\" is the sentence the whole contract exists to make true.");
.man.registerFunc (".qbfstate.bounded_worker_methods";".qbfstate";"Every bounded worker must define these functions in its own namespace. Chosen to match the lifecycle the framework actually drives: acquire a request, turn a cursor into windows, fetch one window, publish it, then record the cursor that acknowledges it.";".qbfstate.bounded_worker_methods";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qbfstate.bounded_worker_globals";".qbfstate";"...and these globals. range_from/range_to make the bound explicit and inspectable rather than buried in a call; source_version is mandatory because coverage recorded under one source release says nothing about another (ETL-09), so a run that cannot name its release cannot record coverage either.";".qbfstate.bounded_worker_globals";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qbfstate.bounded_workers";".qbfstate";"worker name -> the namespace symbol holding its implementation.";".qbfstate.bounded_workers";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qbfstate.ns_names";".qbfstate";"Private: the names defined in a namespace, or an empty list if the namespace does not exist. Wrapped because `key` on an absent namespace throws rather than returning empty, and a missing namespace is a contract violation we want reported by name rather than as a bare error.";".qbfstate.ns_names";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qbfstate.register";".qbfstate";"Register a bounded worker. Does NOT validate - require_contract does that, at init, when the namespace is actually populated. Registering early and checking late is deliberate: a worker's methods are defined as its file loads, so validating at registration would force declaration order.";".qbfstate.register";".qbfstate.register[`markout_backfill;`.qmarkbf]");
.man.registerArg (".qbfstate.register";"param";"worker";"the worker's name, e.g. `markout_backfill");
.man.registerArg (".qbfstate.register";"param";"ns";"the namespace symbol holding its implementation, e.g. `.qmarkbf");
.man.registerArg (".qbfstate.register";"return";"";"the worker name");
.man.registerArg (".qbfstate.register";"eg";"";".qbfstate.register[`markout_backfill;`.qmarkbf]");
.man.registerFunc (".qbfstate.registered";".qbfstate";"Every registered bounded worker, for introspection and for tests.";".qbfstate.registered";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qbfstate.require_contract";".qbfstate";"Check that a registered worker implements the whole contract, and throw naming everything missing if it does not (question-bank C-01). Called during the worker's own initialisation, so an incomplete worker fails at startup rather than part-way through a backfill against a live source. It reports ALL missing names at once rather than the first, because fixing them one error at a time is the slow way to find out a worker was never wired up. This is what makes \"is this worker complete\" a deterministic test rather than a code-review question, which is what ETL-18 asks for.";".qbfstate.require_contract";".qbfstate.require_contract[`markout_backfill]");
.man.registerArg (".qbfstate.require_contract";"param";"worker";"a name previously passed to register");
.man.registerArg (".qbfstate.require_contract";"return";"";"the worker name, so it can be used inline in an init chain");
.man.registerArg (".qbfstate.require_contract";"throws";"";"error if the worker is unregistered, its namespace is absent, or");
.man.registerArg (".qbfstate.require_contract";"eg";"";".qbfstate.require_contract[`markout_backfill]");
.man.registerFunc (".qbfstate.lock_dir";".qbfstate";"Where lock files live. Shares the status directory, since .qpipe.status_dir already establishes one per deployment and a lock is the same kind of per-deployment runtime state.";".qbfstate.lock_dir";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qbfstate.acquire_lock";".qbfstate";"Take an exclusive single-instance lock, or refuse to start (question-bank C-09). One instance per worker is what lets ETL-06's checkpoint stay PRIVATE to that worker: no claim column, no shared cursor state, no claim-expiry logic for dead instances. Double-processing becomes impossible rather than unlikely, and the cost - no horizontal scaling of a single worker - costs nothing on a single-host deployment. Uses mkdir as the atomic primitive rather than a file existence check. `if[not exists; create]` is a race: two workers starting together can both see no lock and both proceed. mkdir either succeeds or fails atomically on every POSIX filesystem, so exactly one wins.";".qbfstate.acquire_lock";".qbfstate.acquire_lock[`markout_backfill]");
.man.registerArg (".qbfstate.acquire_lock";"param";"worker";"the worker's name");
.man.registerArg (".qbfstate.acquire_lock";"return";"";"the lock path");
.man.registerArg (".qbfstate.acquire_lock";"throws";"";"error if another instance already holds the lock");
.man.registerArg (".qbfstate.acquire_lock";"eg";"";".qbfstate.acquire_lock[`markout_backfill]");
.man.registerFunc (".qbfstate.release_lock";".qbfstate";"Release the lock. Safe to call when not held, so it can sit in a cleanup path that also runs on the failure branch.";".qbfstate.release_lock";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qbfstate.lock_held";".qbfstate";"Is the lock currently held? For diagnostics and tests, not for gating - gating on this would reintroduce the race acquire_lock avoids.";".qbfstate.lock_held";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qbfstate.checkpoint_path";".qbfstate";"Where a worker's private state file lives. PRIVATE is the operative word (ETL-06): one worker's checkpoint is never evidence that a dataset is complete, and no other worker may read it. Cross-process completeness has exactly one channel, the append-only etl_coverage ledger (ETL-07), and conflating the two is how a dataset gets declared complete because some unrelated worker happened to get far enough.";".qbfstate.checkpoint_path";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qbfstate.save_checkpoint";".qbfstate";"Save a cursor together with the FULL run specification that produced it (ETL-06). The specification is stored, not just the cursor, because a cursor is only meaningful relative to the run that produced it. A cursor from a [Sep 1; Sep 5) run at source_version v1 says nothing about a [Sep 1; Sep 30) run at v2, and resuming from it would skip most of the second run's range while reporting progress.";".qbfstate.save_checkpoint";"");
.man.registerArg (".qbfstate.save_checkpoint";"param";"worker";"the worker's name");
.man.registerArg (".qbfstate.save_checkpoint";"param";"spec";"the run specification - source_version, range_from, range_to");
.man.registerArg (".qbfstate.save_checkpoint";"param";"cursor";"how far the run has got");
.man.registerArg (".qbfstate.save_checkpoint";"return";"";"the path written");
.man.registerFunc (".qbfstate.load_checkpoint";".qbfstate";"Load a cursor, but only if it belongs to THIS run specification (ETL-06). Returns the saved cursor when the specification matches, and a null timestamp when there is no checkpoint or the specification differs - \"discard saved state when the current specification differs\", which is the requirement's own wording. Discarding is the safe direction: re-running a window that was already done is idempotent under ETL-13's retry-safe publication, whereas resuming from a foreign cursor silently skips data.";".qbfstate.load_checkpoint";"");
.man.registerArg (".qbfstate.load_checkpoint";"param";"worker";"the worker's name");
.man.registerArg (".qbfstate.load_checkpoint";"param";"spec";"the CURRENT run specification");
.man.registerArg (".qbfstate.load_checkpoint";"return";"";"the cursor to resume from, or 0Np to start from range_from");
.man.registerFunc (".qbfstate.clear_checkpoint";".qbfstate";"Remove a worker's checkpoint, for a deliberate restart from the beginning.";".qbfstate.clear_checkpoint";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qbfstate.fail";".qbfstate";"Convert a thrown error into a terminal failed status (question-bank M-01). The convention is: deterministic code throws with a message prefixed by its own name, and the worker SHELL catches once and converts. That follows ETL-04's existing pure/impure split rather than adding a second axis to remember - the code that throws is exactly the code ETL-04 already calls unit-testable, and the code that converts is exactly the shell. Note an empty result is NOT an error and must not come through here: \"ran, found no work\" is an `idle status, a success. Conflating the two is what C-07 warns against, because an orchestrator that cannot tell them apart retries a successful no-op forever.";".qbfstate.fail";"");
.man.registerArg (".qbfstate.fail";"param";"worker";"the worker's name");
.man.registerArg (".qbfstate.fail";"param";"spec";"the run specification, for the status record");
.man.registerArg (".qbfstate.fail";"param";"progress";"what had been done when it failed - partial progress is");
.man.registerArg (".qbfstate.fail";"param";"err";"the caught error string");
.man.registerArg (".qbfstate.fail";"return";"";"the status file path written");
.man.registerFunc (".qbfstate.run_pass";".qbfstate";"Run a worker's pass under the shell contract: trap, convert, release. `@[f;::;handler]` rather than `.[f;();handler]` - the latter fires its handler even when f SUCCEEDS on this build, discarding the real result. That is documented at length in scripts/torq_pipeline.q's safe_timer, and it is the same trap here.";".qbfstate.run_pass";"");
.man.registerArg (".qbfstate.run_pass";"param";"worker";"the worker's name");
.man.registerArg (".qbfstate.run_pass";"param";"spec";"the run specification");
.man.registerArg (".qbfstate.run_pass";"param";"pass";"a niladic function performing one bounded pass, returning a");
.man.registerArg (".qbfstate.run_pass";"return";"";"the progress dict on success; on failure, writes a failed status");

.man.registerFile ("bounded_worker.q";"";".qbw";"bounded_worker.q - the generic bounded-worker shell (.qbw). Issue #124. demo_deals_backfill.q was 238 lines of which FOUR were worker-specific - worker_name, source_name, dataset, width - and the other 234 were the same glue any bounded worker needs, because that is exactly what .qbfstate / .qwrt / .qcov / .qsrc already define. A second worker would have duplicated those 234 lines to change four, and then needed keeping in step by hand: the framework moved four times in one day while #46 was being built, and a duplicate would have fallen behind on two of them. So the glue lives here once, parameterised, and a worker is a declaration. WHY STATE STILL LIVES IN THE WORKER'S OWN NAMESPACE The obvious design is for this file to hold the state. It cannot: ETL-01's contract, enforced by .qbfstate.require_contract, requires source_version/range_from/range_to to be names in the WORKER's namespace, so that \"is this worker complete\" stays a deterministic check rather than a code-review question. Moving them here would make every worker pass the contract vacuously - the check would be inspecting the shell, not the worker. So the shell reads and writes through the worker's namespace (`get`/`put` below), and the worker declares the globals itself. The cost is two indirection helpers; the benefit is that require_contract still means something. WHAT A WORKER MAY STILL OVERRIDE Everything. The worker's own `fetch`, `publish` and so on are ordinary names in its namespace that happen to delegate here, so a worker with a genuinely different publish path defines its own and nothing in this file objects. The shell is a default, not a framework that owns the worker.");
.man.registerFunc (".qbw.cfgs";".qbw";"worker -> its configuration. `ns` is the worker's namespace symbol, `source` its registered .qsrc source, `dataset` the name coverage is recorded under, `width` its window size.";".qbw.cfgs";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qbw.optional_cfg";".qbw";"The keys a worker MAY declare. Absent ones are filled with (::) at registration, which is not tidiness - it is load-bearing. `config` is a dictionary of dictionaries, and q coerces same-keyed dicts into a TABLE. That coercion was happening by accident: with two workers declaring different keys, `value config` became a table anyway and every worker silently acquired every other worker's keys - demo_events_backfill reported a `check` it had never declared. Worse, a worker declaring a key no earlier worker had made registration fail outright with 'mismatch, because a table cannot gain a column that way. Normalising every config to the same key set makes the table shape INTENDED rather than emergent: registration order stops mattering, a new optional key is one entry here, and no worker is handed a key it did not ask for with a value it did not choose.";".qbw.optional_cfg";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qbw.define";".qbw";"Declare a worker's configuration. Validated here rather than at first use, because a declaration is one literal with nothing to defer - the same reasoning .qsrc.register follows and the opposite of .qbfstate.register, whose methods appear as a file loads.";".qbw.define";"");
.man.registerArg (".qbw.define";"param";"worker";"the worker's name");
.man.registerArg (".qbw.define";"param";"cfg";"dict of ns, source, dataset, width");
.man.registerArg (".qbw.define";"throws";"";"error naming every missing or malformed field at once");
.man.registerFunc (".qbw.normalised";".qbw";"Private: a config carrying every optional key, absent ones as (::).";".qbw.normalised";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qbw.declaration";".qbw";"One worker's configuration, or a refusal naming it. Throws rather than returning a null for the same reason .qsrc.declaration does: a caller handed an empty dict fails later and somewhere else.";".qbw.declaration";".qbw.declaration `demo_deals_backfill");
.man.registerArg (".qbw.declaration";"param";"worker";"the defined worker's name, as a symbol");
.man.registerArg (".qbw.declaration";"return";"";"the config dict (ns, source, dataset, width)");
.man.registerArg (".qbw.declaration";"throws";"";"error naming the worker when define was never called for it");
.man.registerArg (".qbw.declaration";"eg";"";".qbw.declaration `demo_deals_backfill");
.man.registerFunc (".qbw.read_state";".qbw";"Private: read and write a global in the WORKER's namespace. See the header for why the state cannot live here. Named read_state/write_state, not get/put: `get` is a q BUILTIN (the counterpart of `set`), so defining it in a namespace throws `assign at load time and aborts the rest of the file - leaving .qbw half-populated while the enclosing script carries on. Seventh reserved-name collision in this repository, after desc, tables, sv, load, var and save.";".qbw.read_state";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qbw.write_state";".qbw";"Private: read and write a global in the WORKER's namespace. See the header for why the state cannot live here. Named read_state/write_state, not get/put: `get` is a q BUILTIN (the counterpart of `set`), so defining it in a namespace throws `assign at load time and aborts the rest of the file - leaving .qbw half-populated while the enclosing script carries on. Seventh reserved-name collision in this repository, after desc, tables, sv, load, var and save.";".qbw.write_state";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qbw.spec";".qbw";"The worker's current run specification.";".qbw.spec";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qbw.init";".qbw";"Resolve everything that can fail BEFORE any work happens (ETL-16). Order is deliberate, cheapest and most-likely-misconfigured first: configuration, then the contract, then the source and its fixture, then the coverage ledger's shape, then the lock, then the connection. A worker that takes a lock and then finds its configuration wrong has to release it again; one that connects and then fails the contract has burned a connection for nothing.";".qbw.init";"");
.man.registerArg (".qbw.init";"param";"worker";"the worker's name");
.man.registerArg (".qbw.init";"param";"run_spec";"dict of source_version, range_from, range_to");
.man.registerArg (".qbw.init";"return";"";"the run specification, as stored");
.man.registerFunc (".qbw.connect";".qbw";"Private: open the live connection. Separate from init so the failure is attributable, and so a test can exercise init without one.";".qbw.connect";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qbw.plan";".qbw";"Turn a cursor into the windows still to do (ETL-13, ETL-18). Two narrowings, in this order, and the order matters: 1. the CURSOR narrows the range to what this run has not yet reached. 2. COVERAGE narrows it further to what is not already published, at this source_version. A retry after a partial run then redoes only the gaps. Coverage first and cursor second would re-plan windows this run had already passed, which is merely wasteful. Cursor only would re-fetch windows a PREVIOUS run published, which is what ETL-13 exists to avoid.";".qbw.plan";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qbw.fetch";".qbw";"Fetch one window under the retry policy (M-04). Transport failures retry; data failures do not, because retrying a schema mismatch produces the same mismatch more slowly. with_retry returns a dict rather than throwing, so the caller decides what a terminal failure means - here, per M-05, the window fails and the run moves on. Every fetched window is validated against the source contract (ETL-12). Not belt-and-braces: a source that dropped a column returns rows where the missing column reads as a NULL in most q code, so without this the worker publishes nulls and records the window as covered.";".qbw.fetch";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qbw.publish";".qbw";"Publish a window's rows into the source's declared target. Returns the row count, which finish_window records as rows_published. Zero is legal and meaningful (ETL-07): an empty window is positive evidence the range was examined and held nothing.";".qbw.publish";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qbw.checkpoint";".qbw";"Save the cursor. Present because the contract requires it (ETL-01); the write goes through .qbfstate so ETL-06's spec-binding is not re-implemented.";".qbw.checkpoint";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qbw.run";".qbw";"Run one bounded pass to completion. Per M-05 a window that exhausts its retries is TERMINAL for that window and the run continues: failing the whole pass would throw away the windows that did succeed, and their coverage is what makes the retry cheap. Failed windows stay uncovered, so the next run plans them again.";".qbw.run";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qbw.run_check";".qbw";"Private: one window, end to end. Accumulates into the worker's own `progress` rather than returning, because a q lambda does not close over an enclosing local and `each` over windows needs somewhere to put the totals. Private: run a worker's declared data-quality check over one batch. Returns the FAILURES, so an empty result means the batch passed - the same convention .qdqc.summarize_checks already uses, and reusing it means a worker can hand that function's output straight back with no adapter. A worker that declares no check returns no failures. That is deliberate: the framework does not force a check, because a check written to satisfy a requirement rather than to catch something is worse than none - it reads as protection while asserting nothing. What the framework does guarantee is that a check, once declared, is unskippable. The check runs in DRY RUN too. It reads the batch and publishes nothing, so suppressing it would only hide the one signal a dry run could give about the data - and \"would this have published garbage\" is exactly what a dry run is for.";".qbw.run_check";"");
.man.registerArg (".qbw.run_check";"param";"worker";"the worker's name");
.man.registerArg (".qbw.run_check";"param";"batch";"the fetched rows, before publication");
.man.registerArg (".qbw.run_check";"return";"";"a table of failures, empty when the batch is acceptable");
.man.registerArg (".qbw.run_check";"throws";"";"error when a declared check is not callable, or returns a");
.man.registerFunc (".qbw.no_failures";".qbw";"Private: the empty failure table, so every path returns one shape.";".qbw.no_failures";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qbw.do_window";".qbw";"Private: one window, end to end - fetch, check, publish, record. Accumulates into the worker's own `progress` rather than returning, because a q lambda does not close over an enclosing local and `each` over windows needs somewhere to put the totals.";".qbw.do_window";"");
.man.registerArg (".qbw.do_window";"param";"worker";"the worker's name");
.man.registerArg (".qbw.do_window";"param";"w";"a row carrying range_from and range_to");
.man.registerArg (".qbw.do_window";"return";"";"1b when the window completed, 0b when it failed and the run");
.man.registerFunc (".qbw.advanced_to";".qbw";"Private: the new cursor, refusing any move that is not strictly forward. D-09 asked whether backfill is strictly oldest-first, and whether the order matters to correctness or only to observability. It is oldest-first by construction - windows[] builds starts as from_ts+width*til n, and remaining hands back ascending sub-ranges - and the order matters to CORRECTNESS, because plan[] uses this cursor as its LOWER bound. A window processed out of order would push the cursor past windows that are still uncovered, and the next run would plan from there and never come back for them. Coverage would still show them as gaps, so nothing gets wrongly reported complete; they simply never get filled while that checkpoint stands. So the ordering was load-bearing and unenforced. .qcont.advance already refuses a non-strictly-forward continuous cursor for a closely related reason; this is the same invariant on the bounded path, which had a plain assignment. One comparison, and the asymmetry is gone.";".qbw.advanced_to";"");
.man.registerArg (".qbw.advanced_to";"throws";"";"error when the cursor would stand still or move backwards");
.man.registerFunc (".qbw.publish_pending";".qbw";"Private: a NILADIC publisher for one worker's pending batch. A projection with its last argument still missing, so finish_window's dry-run gate can choose not to call it at all. `unused`, not `_`: an underscore parameter makes the application throw `'match`, because `_` is q's DROP/CUT operator rather than an ordinary name. It parses and even projects without complaint, then fails only when applied - so the first draft of this file loaded cleanly, planned windows correctly, and failed on the first publish with a bare `'match` naming nothing. Eighth reserved-name class here, and the first that is punctuation rather than a word.";".qbw.publish_pending";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qbw.cleanup";".qbw";"Release everything the worker holds. Safe on the failure branch too, since release_lock is a no-op when not held.";".qbw.cleanup";"");

.man.registerFile ("coercion.q";"";".qcoer";"coercion.q - the shared text-to-type layer for external sources (.qcoer). Implements bank question E-05 (issue #73), answered there: sources return text that q must coerce, and there IS one shared coercion layer rather than a per-source cast. Three trap classes were named as having actually bitten the real sources, and each is handled explicitly below rather than left to q's own casts: floats      - decimal separators, exponents, and EMPTY-AS-ZERO timestamps  - format, and a date where a timestamp was expected symbols     - trailing whitespace and case WHY A SHARED LAYER AND NOT A CAST AT EACH SITE Every one of these traps returns a plausible value rather than an error, so a per-source cast is a per-source opportunity to get it wrong quietly. The requirements' own phrasing - \"is there one shared coercion layer\" - is the answer: one place, tested once, named in the source contract. WHAT I MEASURED, INCLUDING WHERE I WAS WRONG Two things I expected to be traps are NOT, on this KDB-X build, and the comments below say so rather than repeating folklore: \"F\"$\"\" is already 0n, not 0f. So q does not silently turn a missing number into a zero, and the empty-to-null mapping here agrees with q rather than correcting it. `$\"EURUSD \" TRIMS: the resulting symbol is 6 characters and matches `EURUSD under both = and ~, and a join on it returns its row. So trailing whitespace on a symbol is not a q-level trap at all. The trim below is kept because it is free and a source may pad in ways `$ does not handle, but it is not fixing a q behaviour. Three traps ARE real and measured, and they are why this file exists: \"P\"$\"2026.09.15\" silently yields midnight. A date where a timestamp was expected collapses every row in a day onto 00:00, destroying intraday ordering - so every markout at a positive horizon reads the wrong quote. Nothing errors; the numbers are simply wrong. THIS IS THE WORST ONE. \"F\"$\"1,0842\" silently yields 0n. A European decimal comma does not truncate, it DELETES the value - and a null rate then propagates as \"no data\" rather than as a parse failure anyone would investigate. `$\"eurusd\" does not match `EURUSD. Unlike whitespace, case is not folded by `$, so a lower-case source symbol misses every join and returns an empty table that reads as \"no data for that pair\".");
.man.registerFunc (".qcoer.numeric_chars";".qcoer";"Characters that are legitimately part of a number, once a decimal comma has been normalised. Anything else means the field is not a number at all rather than a number needing cleanup.";".qcoer.numeric_chars";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qcoer.to_float";".qcoer";"Coerce text to a float, normalising a European decimal comma. The comma is the real trap, measured: \"F\"$\"1,0842\" is 0n, so the value is not truncated, it is DELETED. A null rate then reads downstream as \"no data for this row\" rather than as a parse failure someone would investigate - and for an FX rate, quietly having no price is materially different from having a wrong one. Empty also maps to null, which AGREES with q (\"F\"$\"\" is already 0n) rather than correcting it. It is stated explicitly here anyway, because a reader should not have to know that to trust the function, and because a future build changing it would otherwise change this function's meaning silently.";".qcoer.to_float";".qcoer.to_float[\"1.0842\"]  ->  1.0842");
.man.registerArg (".qcoer.to_float";"param";"str";"the text to coerce");
.man.registerArg (".qcoer.to_float";"return";"";"the float, or 0n when the text is empty or not numeric");
.man.registerArg (".qcoer.to_float";"eg";"";".qcoer.to_float[\"1.0842\"]  ->  1.0842");
.man.registerArg (".qcoer.to_float";"eg";"";".qcoer.to_float[\"1,0842\"]  ->  1.0842");
.man.registerArg (".qcoer.to_float";"eg";"";".qcoer.to_float[\"\"]        ->  0n");
.man.registerFunc (".qcoer.to_long";".qcoer";"Coerce text to a long, mapping empty to null rather than zero.";".qcoer.to_long";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qcoer.to_timestamp";".qcoer";"Coerce text to a timestamp, accepting both ISO and q spellings. A source emitting \"2026-09-15T09:30:00\" is not malformed; q's own \"P\"$ handles several forms, but the DATE-ONLY case is the trap. \"2026-09-15\" coerces happily to 2026.09.15D00:00:00.000000000 - so every row in a day collapses onto midnight, every intraday ordering is destroyed, and every markout at a positive horizon reads from the wrong quote. Nothing errors; the numbers are simply wrong. So a date-only value is REFUSED here rather than widened. A caller that genuinely wants midnight can say so with `to_date_as_midnight`, which is explicit and greppable.";".qcoer.to_timestamp";".qcoer.to_timestamp[\"2026-09-15T09:30:00\"]  ->  2026.09.15D09:30:00");
.man.registerArg (".qcoer.to_timestamp";"return";"";"the timestamp, or 0Np when empty, malformed, or date-only");
.man.registerArg (".qcoer.to_timestamp";"eg";"";".qcoer.to_timestamp[\"2026-09-15T09:30:00\"]  ->  2026.09.15D09:30:00");
.man.registerArg (".qcoer.to_timestamp";"eg";"";".qcoer.to_timestamp[\"2026-09-15\"]           ->  0Np");
.man.registerFunc (".qcoer.is_date_only";".qcoer";"Is this text a date with no time part? Exported because \"did the source give me a date where I asked for a timestamp\" is a question worth answering explicitly, and because to_timestamp's refusal is otherwise indistinguishable from a parse failure.";".qcoer.is_date_only";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qcoer.to_date_as_midnight";".qcoer";"Widen a date-only value to midnight, DELIBERATELY and visibly. Separate from to_timestamp so that \"this column is a date and midnight is correct for it\" is a statement in the source declaration rather than an accident of q's casting rules. If intraday ordering matters for the dataset, this is the wrong function and the source needs a real timestamp.";".qcoer.to_date_as_midnight";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qcoer.to_symbol";".qcoer";"Coerce text to a symbol, trimming and upper-casing. CASE is the real trap: `$\"eurusd\" does not match `EURUSD, and in q that is not an error - every join returns zero rows and the result is an empty table that reads as \"no data for that pair\" rather than as a data bug. This library's convention is upper-case six-letter pairs throughout, and .qccy.normalize_ccy_pair names the same trap for user input. The TRIM is defensive rather than corrective, and it is worth being honest about which: measured on this build, `$\"EURUSD \" already yields a 6- character symbol that matches `EURUSD and joins correctly, so q handles trailing whitespace itself. The trim stays because it costs nothing and a source may pad with characters `$ does not strip, but it is not fixing a q behaviour and should not be cited as doing so.";".qcoer.to_symbol";".qcoer.to_symbol[\"EURUSD \"]  ->  `EURUSD");
.man.registerArg (".qcoer.to_symbol";"eg";"";".qcoer.to_symbol[\"EURUSD \"]  ->  `EURUSD");
.man.registerArg (".qcoer.to_symbol";"eg";"";".qcoer.to_symbol[\" eurusd\"]  ->  `EURUSD");
.man.registerFunc (".qcoer.to_symbol_cased";".qcoer";"Coerce text to a symbol WITHOUT case folding, for identifiers where case is meaningful - an exchange order id, say. Still trims, because trailing whitespace is never meaningful and always silent.";".qcoer.to_symbol_cased";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qcoer.require_present";".qcoer";"Refuse a null where a value is mandatory. The coercions above map bad input to null rather than throwing, so a single bad row does not abort a whole window. But some columns cannot be null and still mean anything - a trade with no rate, a row with no timestamp - and for those, publishing the null is worse than failing the window: it puts a hole in the data that every downstream consumer must then remember to check for.";".qcoer.require_present";"");
.man.registerArg (".qcoer.require_present";"param";"field";"the column name, for the message");
.man.registerArg (".qcoer.require_present";"param";"v";"the coerced value");
.man.registerArg (".qcoer.require_present";"throws";"";"error when v is null");
.man.registerFunc (".qcoer.coerce_column";".qcoer";"Coerce a whole column and report how many values failed. Returns the count rather than throwing, so a caller can decide: a handful of bad rows in a million might be tolerable and worth logging, while a column that failed entirely means the format changed and the window should not be published at all. That judgement belongs to the worker, not here.";".qcoer.coerce_column";"");
.man.registerArg (".qcoer.coerce_column";"param";"f";"one of the to_* functions");
.man.registerArg (".qcoer.coerce_column";"param";"col";"a list of text values");
.man.registerArg (".qcoer.coerce_column";"return";"";"dict of values and the failure count");

.man.registerFile ("continuous_state.q";"";".qcont";"continuous_state.q - the continuous-worker poll-and-cursor pattern (.qcont). Implements requirement ETL-03: \"implement continuous workers as long-running poll loops: load a local cursor at startup, publish a transformed page, then advance and persist the cursor. Do not publish a resume-completion claim merely because a continuous cursor advanced.\" THE ASYMMETRY WITH BOUNDED WORKERS IS DELIBERATE There is no registry here and no enforced contract, unlike .qbfstate.bounded_workers. ETL-01/ETL-02 give bounded workers both because the thing being prevented is specific: \"a bounded worker must not silently become an unbounded tailer\". A continuous worker is already unbounded, so there is no such failure to prevent, and a registry with nothing to enforce is ceremony. ETL-22/ETL-24 (issue #62) ask whether that should change; this file deliberately does not pre-empt that answer by inventing a contract. THE SENTENCE THIS FILE EXISTS TO ENFORCE \"Do not publish a resume-completion claim merely because a continuous cursor advanced.\" A continuous cursor moving forward means \"I have seen up to here\", NOT \"everything up to here is published and complete\". Those are different claims, and conflating them is how a dataset gets declared complete because a tailer happened to get far enough. So this file has NO path to .qcov.stage_completion, and `advance` refuses a cursor that would go backwards - the one way a tailer can silently re-publish. Freshness is reported instead, which is the honest statement a consumer of continuous output can actually use. That it is not a cross-worker contract is ETL-22's open question, and `freshness` says so at the point of use.");
.man.registerFunc (".qcont.cursor_path";".qcont";"Where a continuous worker's cursor lives. Shares the status directory with bounded workers' checkpoints, but the FILENAME differs (.cursor, not .checkpoint) so the two can never be read for each other - a bounded resume reading a tailer's cursor would skip history it never published.";".qcont.cursor_path";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qcont.load_cursor";".qcont";"Load the cursor at startup (ETL-03). Returns 0Np when there is none, which a worker reads as \"start from whatever its own policy says\" - typically now, or a configured lookback. Deliberately NOT an error: a first run has no cursor, and treating that as a failure would make every fresh deployment need manual seeding. Unlike .qbfstate.load_checkpoint this takes no run specification, because a continuous worker has no bounded run to compare against. That is the whole structural difference between the two kinds of state, and it is why they are separate functions rather than one with a flag.";".qcont.load_cursor";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qcont.save_cursor";".qcont";"Persist the cursor AFTER the page it acknowledges has been published (ETL-03, ETL-05). The ordering is the requirement. Persisting first and publishing second means a crash between them loses the page for good: the cursor says it was handled and nothing will ever fetch it again. Publishing first risks re-publishing the page on restart, which retry-safe publication tolerates. Under-claim over over-claim, exactly as bounded coverage does it.";".qcont.save_cursor";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qcont.clear_cursor";".qcont";"Forget a feeder's saved cursor, so its next run starts from the source's own beginning. Deliberately separate from advance: losing a cursor is a decision, not a side effect of moving one. Continuous output has no coverage ledger to notice re-published pages, so this is destructive in a way the bounded path's checkpoint is not.";".qcont.clear_cursor";".qcont.clear_cursor `fx_feed_2");
.man.registerArg (".qcont.clear_cursor";"param";"worker";"the feeder's name, as a symbol");
.man.registerArg (".qcont.clear_cursor";"return";"";"the worker's name");
.man.registerArg (".qcont.clear_cursor";"eg";"";".qcont.clear_cursor `fx_feed_2");
.man.registerFunc (".qcont.advance";".qcont";"Advance a cursor, refusing to move it backwards. A cursor going backwards is the one way a tailer silently re-publishes: it re-reads a page it has already handled, and because continuous output has no coverage ledger, nothing anywhere records that it happened twice. A bounded worker is protected from the same mistake by ETL-13's coverage precheck; a continuous worker has only this. Equal is also refused. A page that does not move the cursor means the poll found nothing new, and calling advance for it would write the file on every idle tick - turning a quiet source into constant disk churn, and making `saved_at` useless as a signal of when progress last actually happened.";".qcont.advance";"");
.man.registerArg (".qcont.advance";"throws";"";"error when the new cursor is not strictly ahead");
.man.registerFunc (".qcont.freshness";".qcont";"How current is this worker's output? This is the honest statement a consumer of continuous output can make, and it is deliberately NOT a completeness claim (ETL-03). \"I have seen up to 09:41\" says nothing about whether everything before 09:41 is published - only that the tailer has passed it. ETL-22 asks whether continuous workers need a framework-level public freshness contract. They do not have one: this is per-worker, there is no cross-worker aggregate, and nothing consuming continuous output has a sanctioned way to ask \"is this fresh enough\". Reporting the gap in the return value beats leaving a caller to assume there is a guarantee.";".qcont.freshness";"");
.man.registerArg (".qcont.freshness";"return";"";"dict of cursor, lag (now minus cursor) and a stated caveat");
.man.registerFunc (".qcont.feeds";".qcont";"worker -> the dataset it feeds (ETL-22, decided on #62). The one dict ETL-22 needed. It is the only registry continuous workers have, and it is deliberately NOT a contract: registering says which dataset a tailer feeds, nothing about how it must behave. ETL-24's \"no enforced lifecycle for continuous workers\" stands.";".qcont.feeds";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qcont.register_feeder";".qcont";"Declare which dataset a continuous worker feeds.";".qcont.register_feeder";".qcont.register_feeder[`fx_feed_2;`quotes]");
.man.registerArg (".qcont.register_feeder";"eg";"";".qcont.register_feeder[`fx_feed_2;`quotes]");
.man.registerFunc (".qcont.dataset_freshness";".qcont";"How current is a DATASET, across every worker that feeds it (ETL-22)? A dataset is only as fresh as its SLOWEST feeder. Three tailers with cursors at 09:41, 09:41 and 09:12 mean the dataset is current to 09:12, not 09:41 - anything after 09:12 may be missing the third feed's rows. So the cursor reported is the MINIMUM over feeders and the lag the MAXIMUM, and the laggard is NAMED, because \"the dataset is 29 minutes behind\" is a symptom and \"fx_feed_2 is 29 minutes behind\" is a diagnosis. A feeder with no cursor at all makes the dataset not-fresh, for the same reason a single never-run worker is not fresh: a missing feed is the worst possible lag, not a feed to ignore. Its cursor is reported as null and it is the laggard. Still not a completeness claim, and the payload says so. This aggregates \"seen up to here\" across feeders; it does not say everything before that point is published. That distinction is ETL-03's, and it survives aggregation unchanged.";".qcont.dataset_freshness";"");
.man.registerArg (".qcont.dataset_freshness";"return";"";"dict of dataset, cursor (min), lag (max), laggard, feeders, and");
.man.registerArg (".qcont.dataset_freshness";"throws";"";"error when no worker is registered as feeding the dataset - a");
.man.registerFunc (".qcont.dataset_is_fresh";".qcont";"Is a dataset within `tolerance` of now, across all its feeders?";".qcont.dataset_is_fresh";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qcont.is_fresh";".qcont";"Is this worker's output within `tolerance` of now? A missing cursor is NOT fresh, and that matters: a worker that has never run has no cursor, and defaulting the answer to true would report a never-started tailer as up to date.";".qcont.is_fresh";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qcont.poll_once";".qcont";"One poll iteration: fetch a page, publish it, advance (ETL-03). Returns a dict rather than looping, so the LOOP belongs to the worker's own timer and this function stays testable without one. That follows ETL-04's split: the page's transformation and the cursor arithmetic are deterministic and unit-testable, while the timer that calls this lives in the shell. An empty page is a SUCCESS with state `idle, not a failure and not an error. A tailer on a quiet source is working correctly, and an orchestrator that cannot tell \"nothing new\" from \"broken\" alerts all night on a healthy process (C-07).";".qcont.poll_once";"");
.man.registerArg (".qcont.poll_once";"param";"worker";"the worker's name");
.man.registerArg (".qcont.poll_once";"param";"fetch_page";"a unary function taking the current cursor and returning");
.man.registerArg (".qcont.poll_once";"param";"publish_page";"a unary function taking the page and returning a row");
.man.registerArg (".qcont.poll_once";"param";"next_cursor";"a unary function taking the page and returning the");
.man.registerArg (".qcont.poll_once";"return";"";"dict of state (`idle or `published), rows and cursor");

.man.registerFile ("coverage.q";"";".qcov";"coverage.q - the append-only completeness ledger and its interval arithmetic (.qcov). Implements requirements ETL-07 to ETL-11 of docs/reference/etl-framework-requirements.md. Those IDs are the REQUIREMENTS document's; the question bank uses an overlapping E-nn scheme for source-adapter questions, so question-bank answers are named as such below. The two things most easily got wrong here, both counter-intuitive and both stated explicitly in the canonical document: 1. Coverage is RECORDED, not derived (ETL-07). It is not computed from the target data; a completion event is staged for every completed bounded window, INCLUDING AN EMPTY ONE. The empty-window rule is what makes \"we ran and there was nothing\" distinguishable from \"we never ran\" - and a derived ledger cannot express that difference at all. 2. source_version is not optional and not advisory (ETL-09, ETL-10). Coverage recorded under one source release says nothing about another, so every read filters on it and intervals from different versions are NEVER merged to satisfy a dependency.");
.man.registerFunc (".qcov.schema";".qcov";"DECIDED, and this tree is the authority (issue #60, closed 2026-09-16). This block used to read \"ASSUMED, NOT VERIFIED\", because etl_coverage existed only in a canonical tree that could not be reached from here. That premise is gone: A-03 made this repository the primary lineage and A-02 froze canonical, so there is no other schema to verify against. The shape below IS the schema, and `scripts/verify_coverage_schema.q -local 1` confirms the code and the table agree. ON THE PARTITION KEY, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH READING The frontend requirements describe coverage \"by dataset, partition key, and time range\", and there is no partition key here. That was the open worry on #60 - if a real ledger carried one, a query filtering on dataset and version alone would aggregate ACROSS partitions and report a range covered in one partition as covered everywhere. It is deliberately absent, because coverage in this tree has no partition dimension to record. There is exactly one caller of stage_completion - .qwrt.finish_window - and it takes (dataset; source_version; range_from; range_to; rows). A bounded worker covers a WHOLE dataset for a window; nothing backfills one partition at a time. A partition-key column would hold one value per dataset and widen every signature below for nothing. WHAT WOULD CHANGE THAT ANSWER A worker that backfills per partition - per sym, per venue, per region. Then coverage genuinely needs the fourth dimension, and it must be a REQUIRED parameter for the same reason source_version is (ETL-09): an optional filter is one a caller forgets, and forgetting this one reports a gap-ridden range as complete. That is not left to memory. .qbw.define refuses two workers declaring the same dataset, which is the concrete shape the problem takes - two things filling one dataset with no way to tell their coverage apart. A future per-partition worker trips that guard and has to make the decision deliberately. Deliberately one constant in one place: changing it should be an edit here plus the writer's column list, not a hunt through the file.";".qcov.schema";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qcov.still_current";".qcov";"Create the ledger if absent. Append-only by contract (ETL-07) - nothing in this file updates or deletes a row, and a reader should treat any such mutation elsewhere as a bug. The table lives at the ROOT, not in .qcov, because it is a published database table like quotes/trades/position - it flows through the tickerplant to rdb/hdb and is read by processes that know nothing about this namespace. That has a consequence worth stating, because it is a silent-wrong-answer trap: inside `\\d .qcov` a bare `etl_coverage` resolves to `.qcov.etl_coverage`, NOT the root table. Backtick forms (`etl_coverage set / insert) are absolute and hit the root; bare reads are not. Every read below therefore goes through ledger[] rather than naming the table directly. Sentinel for \"this claim has not been superseded\". 0Wp, not 0Np. A null would make the validity test need a special case - `as_of<0Np` is false in q, so every current row would drop out of an as-of read and the ledger would report a fully published range as empty. Infinity makes `as_of<0Wp` true by arithmetic, so the current rows need no branch at all. That is the classic bitemporal encoding and it is chosen here specifically because this repository keeps being bitten by null comparisons that return a plausible answer instead of erroring.";".qcov.still_current";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qcov.init_ledger";".qcov";"Create the root ledger table if it is absent, and return its name. Inside \\d .qcov a bare `etl_coverage` resolves to `.qcov.etl_coverage`, NOT the root table. Backtick forms (`etl_coverage set / insert) are absolute and hit the root; bare reads are not. Every read below therefore goes through ledger[] rather than naming the table directly.";".qcov.init_ledger";".qcov.init_ledger[]");
.man.registerArg (".qcov.init_ledger";"return";"";"the ledger table name");
.man.registerArg (".qcov.init_ledger";"eg";"";".qcov.init_ledger[]");
.man.registerFunc (".qcov.ledger";".qcov";"The root ledger table. Exists so no read below names `etl_coverage` bare - see init_ledger's note on namespace resolution.";".qcov.ledger";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qcov.attach";".qcov";"Attach to the ledger, validating its shape only if it already existed. This is the function a worker's init should call, and it exists because `require_schema` alone was the wrong shape for a caller: a worker cannot tell whether it is the process that created the table, so it either validated a table it had just built (trivially true, and therefore pointless) or skipped the check entirely. Making that distinction here rather than at every call site means no worker has to get it right. The asymmetry is the whole point: ledger absent   -> we create it, so it matches by construction. Nothing to check, and checking would only ever confirm itself. ledger present  -> someone else created it, so its shape is EVIDENCE rather than assumption, and it must be validated before a single read is trusted. That second case is why this exists. A ledger another process created may have a shape this file does not expect - a partition key, say - and then every read here aggregates across whatever that column distinguishes, so a range covered for one value of it reports as COMPLETE for all of them, with no error, because every row found is valid.";".qcov.attach";".qcov.attach[]");
.man.registerArg (".qcov.attach";"return";"";"the ledger table name");
.man.registerArg (".qcov.attach";"throws";"";"error, via require_schema, when an existing ledger has a");
.man.registerArg (".qcov.attach";"eg";"";".qcov.attach[]");
.man.registerFunc (".qcov.require_schema";".qcov";"Refuse to trust a ledger whose shape is not the one this file declares. Converts a SILENT wrong answer into a loud refusal. A ledger carrying an extra column that distinguishes rows - a partition key is the obvious one - makes every read here aggregate across it, so a range covered for one value reports as COMPLETE for all of them, and nothing errors because every row found is valid. A consumer then reads a gap-ridden range believing it whole. With #60 closed this is no longer a guard against an unknown canonical shape; it is a drift guard. The shape is decided (see the header), and this catches a ledger some other process built to a different one. Call it from a worker's init when attaching to a ledger this process did not create - .qbw.init does, via attach. NOT called from init_ledger: a table this file just built trivially matches, so checking there would only ever confirm itself. `scripts/verify_coverage_schema.q` is the same check as a standalone command, against a local table or a remote handle.";".qcov.require_schema";"");
.man.registerArg (".qcov.require_schema";"return";"";"1b when the live shape matches");
.man.registerArg (".qcov.require_schema";"throws";"";"error naming the difference, and what it would silently do");
.man.registerFunc (".qcov.require_interval";".qcov";"Validate a half-open [from;to) interval (ETL-08). Rejecting empty and reversed intervals at construction is what stops a zero-width backfill reporting success: a window where from=to covers nothing, so recording it as coverage would claim completeness for no data.";".qcov.require_interval";"");
.man.registerArg (".qcov.require_interval";"throws";"";"error if the interval is empty or reversed");
.man.registerFunc (".qcov.compose";".qcov";"Merge overlapping and boundary-adjacent intervals, leaving real gaps (ETL-08). \"Only at their common boundary\" is the load-bearing phrase. With half-open intervals [Mon;Tue) and [Tue;Wed) are contiguous with nothing between them, so they compose; [Mon;Tue) and [Wed;Thu) leave Tuesday uncovered and MUST NOT be merged. A naive \"merge anything close\" implementation silently reports a missing day as covered, which is the failure this rule exists to prevent.";".qcov.compose";".qcov.compose[([] range_from:...; range_to:...)]");
.man.registerArg (".qcov.compose";"param";"intervals";"a table with range_from and range_to columns");
.man.registerArg (".qcov.compose";"return";"";"a table of composed intervals, ordered by range_from");
.man.registerArg (".qcov.compose";"eg";"";".qcov.compose[([] range_from:...; range_to:...)]");
.man.registerFunc (".qcov.gaps";".qcov";"The sub-ranges of [range_from;range_to) that `covered` does not cover. An empty result means fully covered.";".qcov.gaps";"");
.man.registerArg (".qcov.gaps";"param";"range_from";"start of the requested range");
.man.registerArg (".qcov.gaps";"param";"range_to";"end of the requested range, exclusive");
.man.registerArg (".qcov.gaps";"param";"covered";"a table of intervals, need not be composed");
.man.registerArg (".qcov.gaps";"param";"from_ts";"start of the requested range");
.man.registerArg (".qcov.gaps";"param";"to_ts";"end of the requested range, exclusive");
.man.registerArg (".qcov.gaps";"param";"covered";"a table of intervals, need not be composed");
.man.registerArg (".qcov.gaps";"return";"";"a table of uncovered sub-ranges; empty means fully covered");
.man.registerFunc (".qcov.stage_completion";".qcov";"Stage a completion event for one completed bounded window (ETL-07). Called for EVERY completed window, including one that published no rows. That is not an oversight to optimise away: an empty window is positive evidence that the range was examined and held nothing, and without it a reader cannot distinguish that from a range never attempted. Recording rows_published=0 is the whole point. Only ever called AFTER the underlying work is complete (ETL-07), which is the same publish-before-acknowledge ordering ETL-05 requires of cursors.";".qcov.stage_completion";".qcov.stage_completion[`markouts;`v1;2026.09.13D00:00;2026.09.14D00:00;1234]");
.man.registerArg (".qcov.stage_completion";"param";"dataset";"the dataset completed, e.g. `markouts");
.man.registerArg (".qcov.stage_completion";"param";"source_version";"the immutable source-release label (ETL-09)");
.man.registerArg (".qcov.stage_completion";"param";"range_from";"window start");
.man.registerArg (".qcov.stage_completion";"param";"range_to";"window end, exclusive");
.man.registerArg (".qcov.stage_completion";"param";"rows_published";"how many rows the window published; 0 is legal and");
.man.registerArg (".qcov.stage_completion";"return";"";"the number of rows now in the ledger");
.man.registerArg (".qcov.stage_completion";"throws";"";"error if source_version is null, or the interval is empty/reversed");
.man.registerArg (".qcov.stage_completion";"eg";"";".qcov.stage_completion[`markouts;`v1;2026.09.13D00:00;2026.09.14D00:00;1234]");
.man.registerFunc (".qcov.valid_at";".qcov";"Every coverage interval for one dataset at one source release. source_version is a required parameter rather than an optional filter, because ETL-09 requires consumers to filter on it and an optional filter is one a caller forgets. Intervals from other versions are not returned, so they cannot be merged into this answer (ETL-10).";".qcov.valid_at";"");
.man.registerArg (".qcov.valid_at";"param";"dataset";"the dataset to report on");
.man.registerArg (".qcov.valid_at";"param";"source_version";"the release to report for");
.man.registerArg (".qcov.valid_at";"return";"";"a table of composed intervals");
.man.registerFunc (".qcov.intervals";".qcov";"The composed intervals covered for a dataset and source_version, as understood at `as_of` (D-11).";".qcov.intervals";".qcov.intervals[`demo_deals;`v1;.z.p]");
.man.registerArg (".qcov.intervals";"param";"as_of";"the instant to answer as of; .z.p for \"now\"");
.man.registerArg (".qcov.intervals";"eg";"";".qcov.intervals[`demo_deals;`v1;.z.p]");
.man.registerFunc (".qcov.is_covered";".qcov";"Is [range_from;range_to) fully covered for this dataset and release?";".qcov.is_covered";"");
.man.registerArg (".qcov.is_covered";"return";"";"1b when there are no gaps");
.man.registerFunc (".qcov.missing";".qcov";"The uncovered sub-ranges of a requested range, for a caller that wants to narrow its request rather than be refused outright.";".qcov.missing";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qcov.require_covered";".qcov";"Admission check: refuse the caller unless the range is fully covered. ETL-11 requires that local historical reads, INCLUDING this check, go through a gateway addressing both `rdb and `hdb. That matters because completion data moves after EOD: a check bound to an rdb-only handle starts returning false gaps the morning after, for data that is present and simply no longer in memory. Callers must therefore route this read, not run it against a single tier.";".qcov.require_covered";"");
.man.registerArg (".qcov.require_covered";"throws";"";"error naming the missing ranges when not fully covered");
.man.registerFunc (".qcov.supersede";".qcov";"Withdraw the coverage claims overlapping [from_ts;to_ts), as of now. D-11, option A: a restatement does not DELETE the old claim, it closes it. The row stays in the ledger with `superseded_at` set, so a read at an earlier as_of still sees it - which is the whole point of recording a restatement rather than overwriting one. \"What did we believe on the 12th\" remains answerable after the 13th says otherwise. Append-only is preserved in the sense that matters: no row is removed and no historical answer changes. What changes is the answer to questions asked from now on, which is exactly what a restatement means. OVERLAP, not containment: a claim covering [09-01;09-10) is withdrawn by a restatement of [09-05;09-06), because after that restatement the original claim is no longer wholly true and leaving it standing would report the restated days as still covered by the old belief. The caller republishes whatever is still correct; this only withdraws.";".qcov.supersede";".qcov.supersede[`demo_deals;`v1;2026.09.12D00:00;2026.09.13D00:00]");
.man.registerArg (".qcov.supersede";"param";"ds";"the dataset");
.man.registerArg (".qcov.supersede";"param";"version";"the source_version whose claims are being withdrawn");
.man.registerArg (".qcov.supersede";"param";"from_ts";"inclusive lower bound of the restated range");
.man.registerArg (".qcov.supersede";"param";"to_ts";"exclusive upper bound");
.man.registerArg (".qcov.supersede";"return";"";"the number of claims withdrawn");
.man.registerArg (".qcov.supersede";"throws";"";"error when the interval is not a proper half-open range");
.man.registerArg (".qcov.supersede";"eg";"";".qcov.supersede[`demo_deals;`v1;2026.09.12D00:00;2026.09.13D00:00]");
.man.registerFunc (".qcov.history";".qcov";"Every claim ever made for a dataset and source_version, withdrawn or not. The audit view. `intervals` answers \"what is true\", this answers \"what was ever said\", which is the question a restatement makes worth asking.";".qcov.history";".qcov.history[`demo_deals;`v1]");
.man.registerArg (".qcov.history";"return";"";"the ledger rows for this dataset/version, in record order");
.man.registerArg (".qcov.history";"eg";"";".qcov.history[`demo_deals;`v1]");

.man.registerFile ("dag.q";"";".qdag";"dag.q - the job graph: every data engineering job's inputs and outputs, in q, so a DAG can be generated and drawn without leaving the interpreter (.qdag). Jobs declare what they READ and what they WRITE; the edges are derived by matching one job's outputs against another's inputs. Nothing declares an edge directly, because an edge stated by hand is a third place for the graph to be wrong - and this repository already has H-01 as its standing lesson about process facts living in more than one file. DERIVE, NEVER RE-DECLARE. Three registries already know their own inputs and outputs, so none of them is asked to restate anything: .qbw.cfgs     bounded workers. Input is the source's remote `table`, output is its `target` - both already on the .qsrc declaration, reachable from the worker's `source`. .qcont.feeds    continuous feeders. Output is the dataset they feed; their input is external by definition (a live feed). PIPELINES       the nine TorQ streaming processes. H-05 already decided the Python Pipeline registry is the source of truth, so this side is GENERATED into q rather than re-declared - the same shape as generate_operational_docs.py, and for the same reason. The point of having it in q: `topological[]` gives a runnable order and `mermaid[]`/`to_json[]` give a drawing, with no Python in the path. A q process can therefore schedule and render its own graph.");
.man.registerFunc (".qdag.jobs";".qdag";"job -> spec dict. A dictionary rather than a table so a spec can hold variable-length symbol vectors without nesting rules getting in the way.";".qdag.jobs";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qdag.required_spec";".qdag";"The keys every spec must carry. `kind` is descriptive rather than behavioural - nothing branches on it - but it is what lets a viz tool colour a streaming process differently from a backfill.";".qdag.required_spec";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qdag.kinds";".qdag";"The kinds a job may declare. Closed on purpose: a typo like `streaming` for `stream` would otherwise silently create a new category that every consumer has to learn about.";".qdag.kinds";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qdag.register";".qdag";"Register a job's inputs and outputs. Re-registering the same job REPLACES its spec rather than erroring, so reloading a file during development is not a failure - the same posture .qbw.define takes for redeclaring a worker.";".qdag.register";".qdag.register[`cross1;`kind`inputs`outputs!(`stream;`quotes;`symbol$())]");
.man.registerArg (".qdag.register";"param";"job";"symbol naming the job, e.g. `posbook1 or `demo_deals_backfill");
.man.registerArg (".qdag.register";"param";"spec";"dict of kind, inputs, outputs");
.man.registerArg (".qdag.register";"throws";"";"error when a required key is missing, or the kind is not known");
.man.registerArg (".qdag.register";"eg";"";".qdag.register[`cross1;`kind`inputs`outputs!(`stream;`quotes;`symbol$())]");
.man.registerFunc (".qdag.declaration";".qdag";"A job's spec, or a refusal naming it.";".qdag.declaration";"");
.man.registerArg (".qdag.declaration";"throws";"";"error when the job was never registered");
.man.registerFunc (".qdag.reset";".qdag";"Forget every registration. For tests, and for rebuilding the graph after the underlying registries change.";".qdag.reset";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qdag.registry";".qdag";"The registry as a table, for reading rather than for lookup.";".qdag.registry";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qdag.producers";".qdag";"Which jobs write this table? Empty means nothing here produces it, which makes it an external input rather than an error.";".qdag.producers";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qdag.consumers";".qdag";"Which jobs read this table?";".qdag.consumers";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qdag.no_edges";".qdag";"Private: the empty edge table, so every return path has one shape.";".qdag.no_edges";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qdag.edges";".qdag";"Every edge in the graph, one row per (producer; table; consumer). An input nothing produces still gets a row, with a null upstream - so a drawing shows where data ENTERS the system rather than silently omitting it. Dropping those rows would make an unconnected job look like a root.";".qdag.edges";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qdag.external_inputs";".qdag";"Tables read by some job and written by none - where data enters.";".qdag.external_inputs";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qdag.sinks";".qdag";"Tables written by some job and read by none - where data comes to rest.";".qdag.sinks";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qdag.job_edges";".qdag";"Private: job-level dependency pairs, with external entry points dropped - a null upstream is not a job and cannot be waited on.";".qdag.job_edges";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qdag.topological";".qdag";"A runnable order: every job appears after everything it reads from. Kahn's algorithm, one layer at a time. The layers matter beyond ordering - jobs in the same layer have no dependency between them, so a scheduler can run a layer in parallel, which is the question a DAG is usually asked. Refuses on a cycle and NAMES the jobs still unplaced, because \"there is a cycle\" without saying where leaves the reader tracing edges by hand.";".qdag.topological";"");
.man.registerArg (".qdag.topological";"throws";"";"error naming every job in the cycle");
.man.registerFunc (".qdag.layers";".qdag";"The same order, grouped into parallel-safe layers.";".qdag.layers";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qdag.id_chars";".qdag";"Private: the characters a mermaid node id may contain.";".qdag.id_chars";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qdag.safe_id";".qdag";"Private: a mermaid-safe node id - every other character becomes \"_\". An allow-list rather than a list of replacements, because the first version replaced only dots and spaces and then met `demo_deals@demo_deals`, whose `@` is invalid in a mermaid id and produced a diagram that silently failed to render. Enumerating what is permitted cannot be outgrown that way; enumerating what is forbidden always can.";".qdag.safe_id";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qdag.mermaid";".qdag";"A mermaid flowchart of the whole graph, as a newline-joined string. Mermaid rather than an image format: GitHub renders it, a plain clone reads it as text, and a reviewer sees a diagram change as a readable diff. That is J-03's answer applied here.";".qdag.mermaid";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qdag.to_json";".qdag";"The graph as JSON, for a viz tool that would rather not parse mermaid.";".qdag.to_json";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qdag.external_ref";".qdag";"Register every bounded worker from .qbw.cfgs, deriving its inputs and outputs from the source declaration it already names. A worker reads the source's remote `table` and writes its `target`, so neither has to be restated on the worker - which is the whole point: a worker that declared its own inputs could disagree with the source it actually reads. A node naming a table that lives on an EXTERNAL source, as \"table@source\". A table's identity in this graph is (location, name), not name - and the shipped sources make that unavoidable rather than theoretical. Both of them declare `table` and `target` as the SAME symbol: demo_deals reads a remote `demo_deals` and writes a local `demo_deals`. Keyed on the bare name, a worker therefore consumed exactly what it produced, and the first run of adopt_all[] reported a cycle among both workers - correctly, given what it had been told. Qualifying the remote side fixes the model rather than the symptom. The same trap in a different costume as `quote` versus `quotes` (N-03): two different things one character - here, zero characters - apart. Parseable on purpose, so a viz tool can split it back into source and table rather than having to treat the node as opaque.";".qdag.external_ref";".qdag.external_ref[`demo_deals;`demo_deals]  ->  `demo_deals@demo_deals");
.man.registerArg (".qdag.external_ref";"eg";"";".qdag.external_ref[`demo_deals;`demo_deals]  ->  `demo_deals@demo_deals");
.man.registerFunc (".qdag.adopt_workers";".qdag";"Register every bounded worker into the job graph, from .qbw's own registry.";".qdag.adopt_workers";".qdag.adopt_workers[]");
.man.registerArg (".qdag.adopt_workers";"return";"";"the worker names registered, empty when .qbw is not loaded");
.man.registerArg (".qdag.adopt_workers";"eg";"";".qdag.adopt_workers[]");
.man.registerFunc (".qdag.adopt_feeders";".qdag";"Register every continuous feeder from .qcont.feeds. A feeder's input is external by definition - it tails a live feed, which is why there is no coverage ledger on that path (ETL-22) - so it declares no inputs and appears as a root.";".qdag.adopt_feeders";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qdag.adopt_pipelines";".qdag";"Register the nine TorQ streaming processes, if the generated bridge has been loaded. src/etl/generated/pipeline_dag.q defines register_pipelines; it is GENERATED from the Python Pipeline registry, because H-05 made that the source of truth and a hand-written q copy would be a second place for the same edges to be wrong. Absent - a bare ETL process that never loads it - this returns empty rather than throwing, so the graph is simply smaller.";".qdag.adopt_pipelines";".qdag.adopt_pipelines[]");
.man.registerArg (".qdag.adopt_pipelines";"return";"";"the process names registered, empty when the generated bridge is");
.man.registerArg (".qdag.adopt_pipelines";"eg";"";".qdag.adopt_pipelines[]");
.man.registerFunc (".qdag.adopt_all";".qdag";"Rebuild the whole graph from every registry that declares one. Resets first, so calling it twice gives the same graph rather than an accumulation - and every registration below is reproducible from a registry, so nothing is lost by clearing.";".qdag.adopt_all";"");

.man.registerFile ("heartbeat.q";"";".qhb";"heartbeat.q - a per-worker liveness table monitoring can poll (.qhb). Answers bank question K-04, decided by the maintainer: yes, a heartbeat table. WHAT IT ADDS OVER THE STATUS FILES. A status file records what a run is doing, and .qpipe writes one per lifecycle transition. That is enough to see a run that finished or failed, and it is what /ops/backfill and the Airflow sensor read. It is NOT enough to see a run that stopped making progress: a worker wedged inside a window - a source that accepted the connection and never answered, a lock never released - leaves a status file that says `running` and keeps saying it indefinitely. Nothing in that file ages. A heartbeat ages. beat[] is called per window, so `last_seen` moves while work is happening and stops moving when it is not, and `stale` turns that into an answer instead of a judgement. THE LIMITATION, STATED RATHER THAN DISCOVERED. This table lives inside the worker's own process, so it cannot report that its process died - the table dies with it. That is not a gap to patch here, it is a division of labour: is the process alive?      the monitor's query either connects or does not is it making progress?     THIS table's last_seen what did the last run do?  the status file (FE-06) is the data complete?      the coverage ledger (ETL-07) Four questions, four answers, no overlap. Trying to make any one of them answer another is how a monitor ends up confidently wrong - and a heartbeat's particular temptation is to read a missing row as \"dead\" when it means \"never started\", which `stale` keeps separate below. Staleness is computed at READ time and never stored. A persisted is_stale flag is wrong the instant after it is written, and this repository has already deleted one such flag (shape_is_assumed) that was set by a single call site and read by nothing.");
.man.registerFunc (".qhb.columns";".qhb";"The table's declared shape. Named so require_schema can check it rather than assume it - the mistake .qcov.require_schema exists to prevent.";".qhb.columns";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qhb.init_table";".qhb";"Create the root table if it is absent, and return its name. Keyed on `worker`: monitoring wants each worker's CURRENT state, not a history. History already exists twice over - the status files per transition, and the coverage ledger per publication - and a third append-only log of the same runs would be a third thing to reconcile. Backtick form (`worker_heartbeat set), not a bare name: inside \\d .qhb a bare `worker_heartbeat` resolves to .qhb.worker_heartbeat, NOT the root table. Same trap coverage.q documents at length.";".qhb.init_table";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qhb.ledger";".qhb";"The root table. Exists so no read below names it bare.";".qhb.ledger";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qhb.require_schema";".qhb";"Refuse if the table is not the declared shape (ETL-16's posture).";".qhb.require_schema";"");
.man.registerArg (".qhb.require_schema";"throws";"";"error naming the missing or unexpected column(s)");
.man.registerFunc (".qhb.attach";".qhb";"Create the table and verify it, returning its name. Called from a worker's init. Present because .qcov.require_schema spent a while defined, tested, and reached from no live path - a check that cannot fire protects nothing, and its existence reads as protection.";".qhb.attach";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qhb.beat";".qhb";"Record that `worker` is alive and in `state`, stamping the time HERE. The worker stamps its own beat. A reader stamping arrival time would measure the reader's clock and the network, and would keep advancing for a worker that had stopped - which is the one thing this table exists to notice.";".qhb.beat";".qhb.beat[`demo_deals_backfill;`running] `seen`, not `prior`: prior is a q BUILTIN (in key `.q), so assigning it as a lambda local throws at LOAD time and aborts the rest of the file - the eighth reserved-name collision in this repository, after desc, tables, sv, load, var, save, get and the `_` parameter. check_q_traps did not have it.");
.man.registerArg (".qhb.beat";"param";"worker";"the worker's name");
.man.registerArg (".qhb.beat";"param";"state";"a lifecycle state symbol, e.g. `running or `idle");
.man.registerArg (".qhb.beat";"return";"";"the worker's name");
.man.registerArg (".qhb.beat";"eg";"";".qhb.beat[`demo_deals_backfill;`running] `seen`, not `prior`: prior is a q BUILTIN (in key `.q), so assigning it as a lambda local throws at LOAD time and aborts the rest of the file - the eighth reserved-name collision in this repository, after desc, tables, sv, load, var, save, get and the `_` parameter. check_q_traps did not have it.");
.man.registerFunc (".qhb.beat_window";".qhb";"Record a beat AND count a completed window. Separate from beat so the count means \"windows finished\", not \"beats recorded\". Conflating them would make the number grow while a worker sat wedged, which is exactly the signal being destroyed.";".qhb.beat_window";".qhb.beat_window[`demo_deals_backfill]");
.man.registerArg (".qhb.beat_window";"eg";"";".qhb.beat_window[`demo_deals_backfill]");
.man.registerFunc (".qhb.report";".qhb";"Every worker's beat, with its age derived AT READ TIME.";".qhb.report";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qhb.stale";".qhb";"Workers whose last beat is older than `max_age`. Returns a TABLE rather than a list of names, because \"which workers are stale\" is never the whole question - how stale, and in what state, is what decides whether to page someone.";".qhb.stale";".qhb.stale[0D00:05]");
.man.registerArg (".qhb.stale";"param";"max_age";"a timespan, e.g. 0D00:05");
.man.registerArg (".qhb.stale";"eg";"";".qhb.stale[0D00:05]");
.man.registerFunc (".qhb.has_beaten";".qhb";"Has this worker ever beaten at all? The distinction the table would otherwise lose. A worker with no row has NEVER STARTED; a worker with an old row started and stopped. Both are \"not beating\", and they need different people woken up, so `stale` above deliberately cannot report the first - it has no age to compare.";".qhb.has_beaten";".qhb.has_beaten[`demo_deals_backfill]");
.man.registerArg (".qhb.has_beaten";"eg";"";".qhb.has_beaten[`demo_deals_backfill]");
.man.registerFunc (".qhb.never_started";".qhb";"Registered workers that have never beaten, from .qbw's registry. Derived from the worker registry rather than from a second list, so a worker cannot be missing from monitoring by being forgotten here - the same reason .qdag adopts rather than asking anyone to re-declare.";".qhb.never_started";"");

.man.registerFile ("io_manager.q";"";".qio";"io_manager.q - where a worker's output goes, as a declaration rather than a hardcoded table insert (.qio). Gap 2.1 of docs/architecture/pipeline-framework-gaps.md. Before this, .qbw.publish was four lines and they decided everything: t:.qsrc.declaration[(declaration worker)`source]`target; if[not t in tables `.; t set 0#batch]; t insert batch; Every worker wrote to an in-process table named by its source declaration, and that was the only thing it could do. There was no way to capture a worker's output in a test without touching a table, to write the same asset somewhere else, or to change where an asset lands without editing the shell. Compute and storage change for different reasons and at different times, which is the whole argument for separating them. WHAT A MANAGER IS. A dictionary carrying a `write` function - the same shape .qsrc already uses for a source, and for the same reason: a dict of functions is a first-class value in q, so a pluggable component needs no new language mechanism, only a second instance of a pattern already shipped here. WHY THERE IS NO `read` OR `exists`. A general IO manager has both. Nothing in this framework reads a target back through an abstraction - downstream workers read the q table directly, in-process - so a `read` here would be a capability reached from no live path. This repository has found four of those in as many days (docs/man.q, .qcov.require_schema, .qwcfg.set_layers, .qdqc), and each read as protection it was not providing. They go in when something calls them.");
.man.registerFunc (".qio.required";".qio";"The keys a manager must carry. One, for now, and the list exists so that adding a second is a change to this line rather than to every validator.";".qio.required";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qio.require_manager";".qio";"Refuse a manager that is not one, naming what is wrong. Checked at DECLARATION rather than at first write, because a worker with a malformed io manager should fail at define time - not halfway through a backfill, having already fetched a window it is now unable to store.";".qio.require_manager";"");
.man.registerArg (".qio.require_manager";"param";"mgr";"the candidate manager");
.man.registerArg (".qio.require_manager";"return";"";"1b when acceptable");
.man.registerArg (".qio.require_manager";"throws";"";"error naming the missing key, or the key whose value is not a function");
.man.registerFunc (".qio.write_memory";".qio";"Private: append a batch to a root table, creating it from the batch's own shape when absent. Backtick form (`target set / insert), because inside \\d .qio a bare name resolves to .qio.<name> rather than the root table - the trap coverage.q documents at length, and the reason every write here is explicit.";".qio.write_memory";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qio.memory";".qio";"The default: an in-process table, which is exactly what every worker did before this file existed. Named so a worker can declare it explicitly and read the same as one that says nothing. (enlist `write), not `write: a symbol ATOM keying a one-element list is a type error in q, and the two forms look identical at a glance. Same trap the shipped sources hit with row_key, and it fails loudly rather than building a dictionary of the wrong shape.";".qio.memory";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qio.write_discard";".qio";"Private: count the rows and write nothing.";".qio.write_discard";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qio.discard";".qio";"Writes nothing, reports what it would have written. Distinct from the dry-run gate, and the difference matters: dry run suppresses publication, coverage AND the checkpoint together, so a dry run makes no progress. This suppresses only the STORE, so a pipeline still plans, fetches, checks, records coverage and advances its cursor. That is what makes it useful for exercising a pipeline end to end, or for measuring fetch cost without paying storage cost - neither of which a dry run can do. Named `discard`, not `null` or `noop`: `null` is a q builtin, and a manager called noop reads as \"does nothing\", which would be a fair description of the dry-run gate and a wrong one for this.";".qio.discard";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qio.for_cfg";".qio";"The manager a worker's declaration asks for, defaulting to `memory`. Defaulting rather than requiring keeps every existing worker working unchanged, which is what makes this seam safe to introduce: a declaration that says nothing about io gets exactly the behaviour it had before.";".qio.for_cfg";".qio.for_cfg[`ns`source`dataset`width!(`.qddbf;`s;`d;1D)]  ->  .qio.memory");
.man.registerArg (".qio.for_cfg";"param";"cfg";"a worker's configuration dictionary");
.man.registerArg (".qio.for_cfg";"return";"";"the manager dict");
.man.registerArg (".qio.for_cfg";"throws";"";"error, via require_manager, when a declared manager is malformed");
.man.registerArg (".qio.for_cfg";"eg";"";".qio.for_cfg[`ns`source`dataset`width!(`.qddbf;`s;`d;1D)]  ->  .qio.memory");
.man.registerFunc (".qio.write";".qio";"Write one batch through a manager.";".qio.write";".qio.write[.qio.memory;`demo_deals;batch]");
.man.registerArg (".qio.write";"param";"mgr";"the manager");
.man.registerArg (".qio.write";"param";"target";"the table symbol the source declaration names");
.man.registerArg (".qio.write";"param";"batch";"the rows to store");
.man.registerArg (".qio.write";"return";"";"the number of rows written, as the manager reports them");
.man.registerArg (".qio.write";"eg";"";".qio.write[.qio.memory;`demo_deals;batch]");

.man.registerFile ("log.q";"";".qlog";"log.q - the logging contract for ETL workers (.qlog). Answers question-bank K-01: \"bare .lg.o/.lg.e, or a levelled layer on top?\" A levelled layer, and a thin one - four functions over TorQ's own .lg, adding exactly the two things TorQ lacks and nothing else. WHAT TORQ ALREADY PROVIDES, AND IS NOT DUPLICATED TorQ's .lg.l takes [level;proctype;proc;id;message;dict], formats to pipe-delimited or JSON (-jsonlogs), routes to stdout/stderr through .lg.outmap, and publishes through .lg.pubmap. That is a complete transport and this file does not reimplement any of it. Every message here ends up in .lg.l, so `-jsonlogs`, log rolling and publication all keep working. THE TWO THINGS TORQ LACKS 1. A DEBUG level. .lg.outmap knows ERROR/ERR/INF/WARN only, and `debug_level`, `verbose` and `log_verbose` in the requirements imply a fourth. Without one, diagnostic detail is either always on (and a million-row backfill floods its log with per-window lines) or never on (and a stuck worker gives you nothing). DBG is registered in outmap at 0 - off - and switched on per process with .qlog.debug[]. 2. Structure. A worker logging \"window done\" is useless in aggregate; a worker logging window=[from;to) rows=1234 is greppable across a fleet. Every function here takes a DICT of fields and renders it k=v, so the field names are the same in every worker and a log line from one is comparable with a log line from another. That is what \"every worker needs the same answer or logs become unreadable in aggregate\" means in practice. WHY THIS WORKS WITHOUT TORQ LOADED The ETL core is unit-tested standalone, where .lg does not exist. So the transport is resolved AT CALL TIME: .lg.l when present, a plain -1 to stdout when not. Resolving at load time would bind the fallback forever in any process that loads this file before torq.q - and q's late binding of names is precisely what makes the call-time check cheap.");
.man.registerFunc (".qlog.levels";".qlog";"Level names, in severity order. DBG is this file's addition; the other three are TorQ's own, kept identical so outmap and pubmap apply unchanged.";".qlog.levels";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qlog.register";".qlog";"Register DBG with TorQ's routing tables if TorQ is loaded and has not heard of it. Off by default (0): nothing changes for an existing process until it opts in. Idempotent, so it is safe to call from every worker's init rather than exactly once.";".qlog.register";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qlog.debug";".qlog";"Switch debug output on (or off) for THIS process. Per process, not global, because the case for debug is one misbehaving worker - turning it on fleet-wide is how you lose the signal in the noise you switched it on to find.";".qlog.debug";"");
.man.registerArg (".qlog.debug";"param";"on";"1b to emit DBG lines, 0b to suppress them");
.man.registerFunc (".qlog.render";".qlog";"Private: render a field dict as space-separated k=v, values via .Q.s1 so a symbol, a timestamp and a string all render unambiguously and a list does not spread across the line. Field ORDER is preserved, so a worker that always logs (worker;window;rows) produces columns a human can scan.";".qlog.render";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qlog.torq_loaded";".qlog";"Private: is TorQ's logging loaded? `l in key `.lg, NOT `lg in key `. - the latter is always false, because key `. lists the ROOT namespace's own names and namespaces are not among them. The first draft used it, so this layer took the stdout fallback in every process including ones with TorQ fully loaded: right level gating, wrong transport, and no error to say so. Caught only by running the layer against TorQ's real .lg definitions and noticing the line had three fields where TorQ's format has six.";".qlog.torq_loaded";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qlog.emit";".qlog";"Private: the transport. TorQ's .lg.l when loaded, stdout otherwise. Level gating outside TorQ mirrors TorQ's own default: DBG is suppressed unless debug[] was called, everything else prints. That way a test that asserts \"this DBG line was not emitted\" gets the same answer whether or not torq.q happens to be loaded.";".qlog.emit";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qlog.enabled";".qlog";"Private: is this level going to be emitted at all? Split out so `line` can check BEFORE rendering. Mirrors the transport's own gating: TorQ's outmap when it is loaded, the debug switch when it is not.";".qlog.enabled";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qlog.line";".qlog";"Private: assemble and emit one line. The suppression check comes FIRST, before the fields are rendered (bank question K-02). Rendering a message nobody will read is pure waste, and the waste is concentrated exactly where it hurts: DBG is off by default and is the level a worker emits per WINDOW, so a million-row backfill with debug off would otherwise render and discard one message per window. q has no lazy arguments, so a caller that wants to avoid building an expensive FIELD VALUE still has to check `enabled` itself - this only avoids the rendering, not the caller's own work. That is the honest limit of the fix, and it is why `enabled` is not private. Written as `if[enabled ...; emit ...]` rather than an early return: the first draft used `if[not enabled level; :::]` and `:::` does NOT parse as \"return generic null\" - the if body was a no-op, execution fell through, and the rendering happened anyway. The gate read as correct and did nothing. Wrapping the emit has no such ambiguity.";".qlog.line";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qlog.dbg";".qlog";"The four levels. `id` is the worker or component name - it becomes TorQ's `id` column, so `select from logmsg where id=`demo_deals_backfill` works on a published log.";".qlog.dbg";".qlog.info[`demo_deals_backfill;\"window published\"; `range_from`range_to`rows!(2026.09.11D00:00;2026.09.12D00:00;1234)]");
.man.registerArg (".qlog.dbg";"param";"id";"the worker or component, as a symbol");
.man.registerArg (".qlog.dbg";"param";"text";"a short fixed message - what happened, not the values");
.man.registerArg (".qlog.dbg";"param";"fields";"a dict of the values, rendered k=v after the text");
.man.registerArg (".qlog.dbg";"eg";"";".qlog.info[`demo_deals_backfill;\"window published\"; `range_from`range_to`rows!(2026.09.11D00:00;2026.09.12D00:00;1234)]");
.man.registerFunc (".qlog.info";".qlog";"The four levels. `id` is the worker or component name - it becomes TorQ's `id` column, so `select from logmsg where id=`demo_deals_backfill` works on a published log.";".qlog.info";".qlog.info[`demo_deals_backfill;\"window published\"; `range_from`range_to`rows!(2026.09.11D00:00;2026.09.12D00:00;1234)]");
.man.registerArg (".qlog.info";"param";"id";"the worker or component, as a symbol");
.man.registerArg (".qlog.info";"param";"text";"a short fixed message - what happened, not the values");
.man.registerArg (".qlog.info";"param";"fields";"a dict of the values, rendered k=v after the text");
.man.registerArg (".qlog.info";"eg";"";".qlog.info[`demo_deals_backfill;\"window published\"; `range_from`range_to`rows!(2026.09.11D00:00;2026.09.12D00:00;1234)]");
.man.registerFunc (".qlog.warn";".qlog";"The four levels. `id` is the worker or component name - it becomes TorQ's `id` column, so `select from logmsg where id=`demo_deals_backfill` works on a published log.";".qlog.warn";".qlog.info[`demo_deals_backfill;\"window published\"; `range_from`range_to`rows!(2026.09.11D00:00;2026.09.12D00:00;1234)]");
.man.registerArg (".qlog.warn";"param";"id";"the worker or component, as a symbol");
.man.registerArg (".qlog.warn";"param";"text";"a short fixed message - what happened, not the values");
.man.registerArg (".qlog.warn";"param";"fields";"a dict of the values, rendered k=v after the text");
.man.registerArg (".qlog.warn";"eg";"";".qlog.info[`demo_deals_backfill;\"window published\"; `range_from`range_to`rows!(2026.09.11D00:00;2026.09.12D00:00;1234)]");
.man.registerFunc (".qlog.err";".qlog";"Log an error. Does NOT throw and does NOT exit - TorQ's .lg.e does both depending on .proc state, which is right for an init failure and wrong for a worker recording that one window failed and moving on (M-05). A worker that wants to abort throws itself; this only records.";".qlog.err";"");

.man.registerFile ("source_contract.q";"";".qsrc";"source_contract.q - the centralised external-source contract (.qsrc). Implements requirement ETL-12: \"register every external source table, target mapping, required field and required type in the centralised source contract. Validate both generated fixtures and live external metadata against that same contract.\" The phrase doing the work is \"that SAME contract\". One declaration validates both a local fixture and a live source, so a fixture cannot drift from the thing it stands in for. Two separate declarations would pass a suite while the real source had changed - and a fixture that no longer resembles its source is worse than no fixture, because it manufactures confidence. Four ETL decisions are recorded here rather than left implicit, and the time-and-timezone block after them likewise. They follow from answers already given, and are stated so nobody has to re-derive them: bank E-04 (answered via A-04 + FE-22/FE-23) - the external driver is NOT a hard dependency. A public single-host demo cannot require a licensed ODBC driver, or the whole backfill path is undemonstrable. Every source declares a fixture, so the path is exercisable with no driver at all. bank E-07 (answered via A-04 + the FE-20 precedent) - credentials come from the ENVIRONMENT only. Nothing secret lives in this tree, and the YAML layer must never carry one. `require_credentials` enforces that rather than documenting it. bank E-08 (answered via FE-14) - source queries are PARAMETERISED q lambdas, never built by string concatenation. FE-14's guarantee is that no caller input reaches query text; uqf_frontend/queries.py already honours it, and a source adapter is the same problem with a less friendly input. bank E-09 (answered via A-04) - bank-internal business logic is not reimplementable here. What gets built is a generic ANALOGUE with the same shape and none of the logic. So this contract describes shapes, and deliberately carries no business semantics. TIME AND TIMEZONE (issue #80: L-03, L-05, L-06) L-06 - \"do external sources return local times, and who converts?\" WHAT THE BANK'S SOURCES ACTUALLY RETURN IS NOT KNOWABLE FROM HERE. The canonical tree is unreachable and A-04 forbids its schemas appearing here, so nobody in this repository can answer that half. What IS decidable is the policy, and the policy is what changes the code: - internally, everything is UTC. That is not new: python/uqf_frontend already enforces it at the HTTP edge, where queries.coerce rejects a naive datetime outright. It cites that rule as \"ETL-08/R9.1\" in the FRONTEND's numbering - a different ETL-08 from this file's, which is the parameterised-query decision above. Same rule, two numbering schemes; this file's timezone handling is the q-side half of it. - the zone is therefore a PER-SOURCE property, because only the source knows it, and it is DECLARED rather than defaulted. An omitted zone is the exact shape of the bug: it reads as \"UTC\" to every later reader while the source was handing over wall-clock local time all along, which is silent and off by one offset. - the FRAMEWORK converts, once, at fetch, in fetch_window below. Not the worker (each would do it slightly differently, and a worker that forgot would be indistinguishable from a UTC source), and not the consumer (by then the zone is gone). L-03 - the `z->p` cast bug class. THE CANONICAL BUG ITSELF IS NOT RECOVERABLE from this tree; what follows is the class, measured here under KDB-X, and the guards that stop it recurring. q's `datetime` (type 15h, `z`) is a FLOAT count of days; `timestamp` (12h, `p`) is a long count of nanoseconds. Going z->p is therefore a float-to-long rounding, and it is quiet: - measured: of 1000 timestamps one nanosecond apart, 999 do not survive a p->z->p round trip; the largest error seen was 629ns, and up to 447ns of it BACKWARDS, i.e. to an earlier instant. - whole seconds DO survive (0 error across a full day of them), so the bug passes every hand-check built from round numbers and only shows up on real trade timestamps. - `=` says a z and a p at the same instant are equal (1b) while `~` says they do not match (0b), and `distinct` keeps a value and its own round trip as TWO values - so a dedupe on (key;time) silently stops recognising rows it has already published. - filtering a z column with p window bounds does not even warn: q promotes, the window comes back plausible, and the wrongness is carried in the data rather than raised. Three things prevent recurrence, all of them enforcement rather than documentation: (1) register below requires the time_field to be one of the declared fields AND to be declared `p`, so a z time column is a registration failure; (2) validate/validate_live compare declared type characters against `meta`, so a source that silently changes a column from p to z fails on both the fixture and the live path; (3) scripts/check_q_traps.py forbids the q datetime type in src/ outright - the cast direction is not statically decidable, but the type's PRESENCE is, and this tree has no legitimate use for it. L-05 - DST in windowed backfills. Windows are cut in UTC by .qwrt.windows, so a \"daily\" window is always exactly 24h of elapsed time: never short, never long, and the coverage ledger keeps tiling exactly across a transition. The variable thing is the LOCAL span, and that is handled here rather than by warping window widths - see source_bounds and local_to_utc for the two traps that produces.");
.man.registerFunc (".qsrc.required_declarations";".qsrc";"What every registered source must declare. Named as data so a test can assert the set rather than trusting a code review. `time_zone` is REQUIRED, with no default (L-06). A defaulted zone is the bug: it reads as a decision downstream while nobody ever made one. Stating `UTC` costs one symbol and makes \"this source hands over UTC\" a claim somebody wrote, which validate_live can then be run against.";".qsrc.required_declarations";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qsrc.sources";".qsrc";"source -> its declaration dict.";".qsrc.sources";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qsrc.register";".qsrc";"Register an external source table (ETL-12). Registration VALIDATES immediately, unlike .qbfstate.register which deliberately defers. The asymmetry is deliberate and worth stating: a bounded worker's methods appear as its file loads, so validating early would force declaration order. A source declaration is a single literal with no such ordering problem, so the earliest possible failure is the best one.";".qsrc.register";"");
.man.registerArg (".qsrc.register";"param";"source";"a name for this source, e.g. `deals_analogue");
.man.registerArg (".qsrc.register";"param";"decl";"a dict carrying every name in required_declarations:");
.man.registerArg (".qsrc.register";"return";"";"the source name");
.man.registerArg (".qsrc.register";"throws";"";"error naming every missing or malformed declaration at once");
.man.registerFunc (".qsrc.registered";".qsrc";"Every registered source's name. The registry IS the list - there is no second declaration of which sources exist, which is what makes bank E-02 true: adding a source is a file plus a registration, with no core change.";".qsrc.registered";".qsrc.registered[]");
.man.registerArg (".qsrc.registered";"return";"";"a symbol vector, empty when nothing has registered yet");
.man.registerArg (".qsrc.registered";"eg";"";".qsrc.registered[]");
.man.registerFunc (".qsrc.row_key";".qsrc";"The column(s) identifying a row uniquely, always as a vector. Exported so a future dedupe or restatement path has one place to ask, rather than each caller reaching into the declaration and deciding for itself whether a single symbol needs enlisting. Stored normalised by `register`, so this is already a vector - the `(),` is belt-and-braces for a declaration written directly into `sources` by a test rather than through register.";".qsrc.row_key";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qsrc.declaration";".qsrc";"One source's full declaration, or a refusal naming it. The single read path every other module uses to reach a source's contract - five files call it - so it throws rather than returning a null: a caller that got an empty dict back would fail later, somewhere else, on a missing key.";".qsrc.declaration";".qsrc.declaration `demo_deals");
.man.registerArg (".qsrc.declaration";"param";"source";"the registered source's name, as a symbol");
.man.registerArg (".qsrc.declaration";"return";"";"the declaration dict (source, table, target, time_field, row_key,");
.man.registerArg (".qsrc.declaration";"throws";"";"error naming the source when it was never registered");
.man.registerArg (".qsrc.declaration";"eg";"";".qsrc.declaration `demo_deals");
.man.registerFunc (".qsrc.type_chars";".qsrc";"Private: the q type character for each column of a table. `meta`'s `t` column is exactly this, and using it rather than `type each` means an empty table validates the same way a populated one does - which matters because a fixture may legitimately be empty, and a source may legitimately return no rows for a window.";".qsrc.type_chars";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qsrc.validate";".qsrc";"Validate a table against a source's declaration (ETL-12). This is the single function both the fixture check and the live metadata check go through, which is what makes \"that same contract\" true rather than aspirational. Extra columns are ALLOWED, declared fields are not optional. A source growing a column is routine and breaking on it would make every upstream addition an outage; a source LOSING a declared column, or changing its type, is precisely the silent breakage worth failing on - a missing column reads as a null in most q code rather than as an error.";".qsrc.validate";"");
.man.registerArg (".qsrc.validate";"param";"source";"a registered source name");
.man.registerArg (".qsrc.validate";"param";"tbl";"a table to check");
.man.registerArg (".qsrc.validate";"return";"";"1b when the table satisfies the declaration");
.man.registerArg (".qsrc.validate";"throws";"";"error naming every missing field and every type mismatch at once");
.man.registerFunc (".qsrc.validate_fixture";".qsrc";"Validate a source's own fixture (ETL-12's \"generated fixtures\"). Runs in the deterministic suite, with no connection anywhere. A fixture that does not satisfy the contract is a broken test double, and finding that out from a failing worker test is a much longer path.";".qsrc.validate_fixture";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qsrc.validate_live";".qsrc";"Validate LIVE external metadata against the same declaration (ETL-12). Separate from validate_fixture only in where the table comes from - the contract and the checking are identical, which is the requirement. Belongs to the `smoke` lane, not the deterministic suite (ETL-20): the deterministic suite proves local behaviour, not that a configured external service is reachable or compatible.";".qsrc.validate_live";"");
.man.registerArg (".qsrc.validate_live";"param";"source";"a registered source name");
.man.registerArg (".qsrc.validate_live";"param";"h";"an open handle to the external source");
.man.registerFunc (".qsrc.credential_var";".qsrc";"The environment variable holding a source's credential (ETL-07). Mechanical from the source name, so an operator can guess it. Deliberately a separate prefix from .qwcfg's UQF_: a credential is not configuration, and keeping the namespaces apart means a credential can never arrive through the YAML or overrides layer by accident.";".qsrc.credential_var";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qsrc.require_credentials";".qsrc";"Read a source's credential, or refuse (ETL-07). Environment ONLY. There is no file fallback and no vault, on purpose: nothing secret can live in this tree, and a file fallback is how a credential ends up committed. The error names the variable, since \"which environment variable\" is the only thing the operator needs to know.";".qsrc.require_credentials";"");
.man.registerArg (".qsrc.require_credentials";"throws";"";"error when the variable is unset");
.man.registerFunc (".qsrc.has_credentials";".qsrc";"Is a credential available? For deciding between the live and fixture paths without throwing.";".qsrc.has_credentials";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qsrc.zone_table";".qsrc";"The zone table: timezoneID, gmtDateTime, adjustment (L-06). Empty until an operator loads one, and that is deliberate. q has no built-in tz database - `ltime`/`gtime` only ever speak the PROCESS's own TZ, which is a property of whoever started the process and therefore the least trustworthy input available. So conversion needs a real table, and a source that declares a non-UTC zone without one is refused rather than approximated (see require_zone_table). Not vendored a second time: TorQ already ships a tzdata-derived table at lib/torq/config/tzinfo (631 zones, 70381 transitions), in exactly this shape. src/ deliberately does not reach into lib/, so the PATH is the caller's to supply - tests and processes pass it in.";".qsrc.zone_table";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qsrc.zone_names";".qsrc";"Cached so require_zone_table is not a 70k-row scan per fetch.";".qsrc.zone_names";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qsrc.load_zone_table";".qsrc";"Load a zone table from a serialised q table (L-06).";".qsrc.load_zone_table";"");
.man.registerArg (".qsrc.load_zone_table";"param";"path";"a file path, e.g. \"lib/torq/config/tzinfo\"");
.man.registerArg (".qsrc.load_zone_table";"return";"";"the number of transitions loaded");
.man.registerArg (".qsrc.load_zone_table";"throws";"";"error when the file does not hold a table of the expected shape");
.man.registerFunc (".qsrc.require_zone_table";".qsrc";"Refuse to convert without a table, or for a zone it does not know (L-06). There is deliberately no fallback to a fixed offset. A fixed offset is correct for part of the year and an hour wrong for the rest, which puts rows in the wrong window without ever failing - and a coverage ledger then records both windows as complete.";".qsrc.require_zone_table";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qsrc.offsets_for";".qsrc";"Private: the offsets this zone has ever used. At most 8 in tzdata, so the candidate search in local_to_utc is cheap and fully vectorised.";".qsrc.offsets_for";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qsrc.offset_at";".qsrc";"Private: the offset in effect at each UTC instant. Unambiguous by construction - every UTC instant has exactly one offset.";".qsrc.offset_at";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qsrc.utc_to_local";".qsrc";"UTC -> the source's local wall clock (L-06). Shape-preserving: an atom in, an atom out, so a caller converting window bounds does not have to enlist and unwrap.";".qsrc.utc_to_local";"");
.man.registerArg (".qsrc.utc_to_local";"throws";"";"error when an instant predates the zone table's coverage - a null");
.man.registerFunc (".qsrc.local_to_utc";".qsrc";"The source's local wall clock -> UTC (L-06), and the answer to L-05. This direction is the hard one, and it is the whole reason a fixed offset will not do. A local wall-clock reading is not a unique instant: - on a spring-forward day an hour of local times NEVER HAPPENED (Europe/London 2026.03.29, local 01:00-01:59). - on an autumn day an hour of local times happens TWICE (Europe/London 2026.10.25, local 01:00-01:59 - once as BST, once as GMT). So the method is candidate-and-verify rather than a lookup: every offset the zone has ever used gives one candidate UTC instant, and a candidate is real only if converting it back (the unambiguous direction) reproduces the local reading. The count of survivors is the answer: 1 survivor - the normal case, converted. 0 survivors - the local time never existed. THROW. 2 survivors - the local time is ambiguous. THROW. Throwing on both is the deliberate pick L-05 asks for, and it is the only one that cannot lie. Picking either candidate silently assigns the row a UTC instant that may be an hour off, which moves it into a neighbouring backfill window - and since the ledger records windows rather than rows, both windows then read as complete while one holds an hour of the other's data. A hard failure at fetch, naming the row, is recoverable; a misplaced hour discovered months later is not. The operator's fix is to have the source hand over UTC, which is why `UTC is the recommended declaration and the only one needing no table at all.";".qsrc.local_to_utc";"");
.man.registerArg (".qsrc.local_to_utc";"param";"zone";"a zone in the loaded table");
.man.registerArg (".qsrc.local_to_utc";"param";"ts";"local wall-clock timestamp(s)");
.man.registerArg (".qsrc.local_to_utc";"return";"";"the corresponding UTC timestamp(s), same shape as ts");
.man.registerArg (".qsrc.local_to_utc";"throws";"";"error on a nonexistent or an ambiguous local time");
.man.registerFunc (".qsrc.local_candidates";".qsrc";"Private: every UTC instant a local reading could denote, per row. Candidate-and-verify: every offset the zone has ever used gives one candidate, kept only if converting it back reproduces the reading. One vectorised round trip per offset (at most 8 in tzdata), not one lookup per row - a backfill window can hold millions.";".qsrc.local_candidates";"");
.man.registerArg (".qsrc.local_candidates";"return";"";"a list, one ragged entry per input row: 1 instant normally, 0 in a");
.man.registerFunc (".qsrc.nonexistent_message";".qsrc";"Private: the two error texts, shared by local_to_utc and narrow_to_utc so the two paths cannot explain the same failure differently.";".qsrc.nonexistent_message";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qsrc.coercers";".qsrc";"The coercion function for each declared q type character (ETL-05). Declared here rather than left to each adapter, because ETL-05's question was \"is there ONE shared coercion layer\" and the answer is only true if every source reaches it by default. A source that casts its own text is a source that can get the decimal comma or the date-only timestamp wrong privately. `p` deliberately maps to to_timestamp, which REFUSES a date-only value rather than widening it to midnight. A source whose column really is a date, and for which midnight is correct, has to say so by coercing with .qcoer.to_date_as_midnight explicitly - which is greppable, unlike an accident of q's casting rules. A KNOWN LIMITATION, recorded rather than shipped silently: this maps every `s` column to to_symbol, which UPPER-CASES. That is right for currency pairs, which is this library's convention throughout - but it is a guess for enum-like columns. A text source returning \"buy\" yields `BUY, while demo_deals' own fixture uses `buy, so the two paths disagree on case for that one column. It does not bite today: the fixture returns typed data, so `coerce` is never applied to it. It WOULD bite the first time a real text source lands a side or venue column, and the fix is a per-field coercer override in the declaration rather than a cleverer default - there is no case rule that is right for both `EURUSD and `buy. Left undone deliberately because inventing the override mechanism before a source needs it would be guessing at its shape.";".qsrc.coercers";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qsrc.coerce";".qsrc";"Coerce a table of TEXT columns into the declared types (ETL-05). For a source that returns text - which is what ETL-05 is about - this is the step between fetch and validate. Returns the coerced table plus a per- column failure count, so the worker can decide: a few bad rows in a million might be tolerable and worth logging, while a column that failed entirely means the format changed and the window must not be published. That judgement is the worker's, not this layer's.";".qsrc.coerce";"");
.man.registerArg (".qsrc.coerce";"param";"source";"a registered source name");
.man.registerArg (".qsrc.coerce";"param";"tbl";"a table whose declared fields hold text");
.man.registerArg (".qsrc.coerce";"return";"";"dict of `table (coerced) and `failures (field -> count)");
.man.registerArg (".qsrc.coerce";"throws";"";"error when a declared type has no coercer");
.man.registerFunc (".qsrc.fetch_window";".qsrc";"Fetch one window, from the live source or from the fixture (ETL-04). The fixture is not a fallback for a FAILED connection - that would turn an outage into silently synthetic data, which is the worst possible outcome for a coverage ledger that then records the window as complete. It is the path taken when no credential is configured at all, which is an explicit statement that this is a demo. So: credential present means live, and a failure there is a failure. Credential absent means fixture, and that is announced in the return value rather than inferred.";".qsrc.fetch_window";"");
.man.registerArg (".qsrc.fetch_window";"param";"source";"a registered source name");
.man.registerArg (".qsrc.fetch_window";"param";"h";"an open handle, or 0Ni when running on the fixture");
.man.registerArg (".qsrc.fetch_window";"param";"range_from";"window start");
.man.registerArg (".qsrc.fetch_window";"param";"range_to";"window end, exclusive");
.man.registerArg (".qsrc.fetch_window";"return";"";"(`live or `fixture; the table)");
.man.registerFunc (".qsrc.bound_padding";".qsrc";"How far to widen a non-UTC source's window, in its own clock. One day, which is far more than any offset change in tzdata (the largest is the date-line jump, 24h). It buys correctness at the cost of over-fetching, and the exact narrowing in narrow_to_utc throws the excess away - so the only cost is bandwidth on sources that are not UTC.";".qsrc.bound_padding";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qsrc.source_bounds";".qsrc";"Private: the window bounds to hand the source, in the SOURCE's clock. For `UTC this is the identity, and that is the path every source in this tree takes today. For a zoned source the bounds are deliberately WIDE rather than exact, and this is the trap worth stating because the obvious implementation is silently wrong. Converting each bound with the offset in effect AT THAT BOUND is not monotonic across an autumn transition: measured for Europe/London, the UTC window [2026.10.25D00:30; 2026.10.25D01:30) converts to local [01:30; 01:30) - an EMPTY range. The query returns no rows, nothing errors, and the coverage ledger records an hour of missing trades as a complete window. So: pad the bounds, fetch a superset, and narrow exactly in UTC afterwards where the arithmetic is unambiguous.";".qsrc.source_bounds";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qsrc.narrow_to_utc";".qsrc";"Private: convert a fetched page's time_field to UTC and narrow it to the requested half-open range. For `UTC this is the identity: the query (or window_fixture) has already applied [range_from;range_to) and re-filtering would be dead code that could only ever disagree. For a zoned source the narrowing is NOT optional - source_bounds deliberately over-fetched, so without this every window would publish up to a day of its neighbours' rows while coverage recorded the narrow range. It does NOT simply call local_to_utc on the column, and the reason is the padding above. local_to_utc refuses an ambiguous reading outright, which is right when a caller asks about one instant - but here the over-fetch has pulled in up to a day of a NEIGHBOUR's rows, and failing this window because of an ambiguous row that belongs to the next one would make every window on an autumn transition day unbackfillable, not just the affected hour. So the rule is range-scoped: - an ambiguous row is refused only if one of its candidate instants actually lands in [range_from;range_to). Otherwise it is dropped: it is not this window's row, and the window that owns it will refuse it. - a NONEXISTENT reading is refused unconditionally, range or no range. There is no instant to compare against a range, and a source emitting a wall-clock time its own calendar never had means the declared zone is wrong - which is a contract breach, not a windowing question.";".qsrc.narrow_to_utc";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qsrc.window_fixture";".qsrc";"Private: apply the window to a fixture, on its declared time_field. Functional select (`?[t;where;0b;()]`) rather than qSQL, because the column name is a variable: `select from t where time_field>=from_ts` would compare the literal symbol, not the column it names.";".qsrc.window_fixture";"");

.man.registerFile ("worker_config.q";"";".qwcfg";"worker_config.q - typed, validated worker configuration with a stated precedence (.qwcfg). Implements the configuration half of requirement ETL-16, and the precedence decision recorded on issue #71 (question-bank C-02): env  >  process_overrides.csv  >  config/backfill.yaml  >  code default ONLY THE FIRST LAYER IS WIRED TODAY, and saying so here matters more than the order does. `set_layers` below is what populates the lower three, and its only callers are tests - no production path calls it, so in a running worker override_values, yaml_values and default_values stay empty and `env` supplies everything. `config/` does not exist in this tree at all. The precedence is therefore a decided contract with one supplier, not a description of four live layers. It is left standing rather than deleted because C-02 settled the ORDER and that answer does not expire - but a reader who concluded they could drop a key into a YAML file and have a worker pick it up would be wrong, and the previous version of this comment invited exactly that. Wiring the rest needs a YAML hand-off from the Python orchestrator (which already owns that parsing) plus a decision about where `config/` lives, which is bank question H-07. Most specific and most immediate wins, so an operator can override anything from the environment without editing tracked config - which is what you want when debugging a running process. Two obligations came with that answer and both are met here rather than left as intent: the order is documented at the point of use, and test_worker_config.q asserts it by setting the same key in two sources and checking which one wins. An unstated precedence's failure mode is \"works on my machine\", which this repository already hit once with H-01's three process-definition files. Errors accumulate rather than failing on the first problem (ETL-16, and the shape canonical's own worker_config.q uses): a worker started with three bad settings should be told about three, not discover them over three restarts.");
.man.registerFunc (".qwcfg.sources";".qwcfg";"The sources, most significant first. Named rather than positional so a diagnostic can say WHERE a value came from, which is the question an operator actually asks.";".qwcfg.sources";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qwcfg.env_values";".qwcfg";"Private: per-source lookup tables, populated by `set_layers`. Kept separate rather than merged eagerly so `explain` below can report provenance.";".qwcfg.env_values";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qwcfg.override_values";".qwcfg";"Private: per-source lookup tables, populated by `set_layers`. Kept separate rather than merged eagerly so `explain` below can report provenance.";".qwcfg.override_values";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qwcfg.yaml_values";".qwcfg";"Private: per-source lookup tables, populated by `set_layers`. Kept separate rather than merged eagerly so `explain` below can report provenance.";".qwcfg.yaml_values";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qwcfg.default_values";".qwcfg";"Private: per-source lookup tables, populated by `set_layers`. Kept separate rather than merged eagerly so `explain` below can report provenance.";".qwcfg.default_values";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qwcfg.set_layers";".qwcfg";"Register the non-environment layers. Called by a worker's init, typically from whatever parsed the YAML and the overrides CSV - this file does not parse either format, because the Python orchestrator already owns those and duplicating a parser is how the two drift.";".qwcfg.set_layers";"");
.man.registerArg (".qwcfg.set_layers";"param";"overrides";"dict of key -> string value from process_overrides.csv");
.man.registerArg (".qwcfg.set_layers";"param";"yaml";"dict of key -> string value from config/backfill.yaml");
.man.registerArg (".qwcfg.set_layers";"param";"defaults";"dict of key -> string value, the code's own fallbacks");
.man.registerFunc (".qwcfg.env_name";".qwcfg";"Private: the environment variable name for a config key. UQF_BACKFILL_FROM for `backfill_from, so the mapping is mechanical and an operator can guess it without reading this file.";".qwcfg.env_name";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qwcfg.raw_from";".qwcfg";"Private: raw string value for a key from one named source, or \"\" when the source does not carry it.";".qwcfg.raw_from";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qwcfg.explain";".qwcfg";"Which source supplies a key, and its raw value. Returns (`none;\"\") when no source has it. Exported rather than private because \"where did this value come from\" is the question that actually gets asked when a worker misbehaves, and answering it should not require reading the precedence order off a comment.";".qwcfg.explain";".qwcfg.explain[`dry_run]  ->  (`none;\"\") That is the honest UNSET answer, and it is what the example asserts. Once UQF_DRY_RUN=true is exported the same call returns (`env;\"true\"). The previous example claimed (`env;\"2026.09.01\") for `backfill_from, which was wrong twice over: nothing in this repository sets UQF_BACKFILL_FROM (a worker's window comes from read_state, not from config - see C-04), and an example whose documented value depends on the caller's ambient environment cannot be verified by anything. `dry_run` is the one key production really reads, via .qwrt.is_dry_run.");
.man.registerArg (".qwcfg.explain";"eg";"";".qwcfg.explain[`dry_run]  ->  (`none;\"\") That is the honest UNSET answer, and it is what the example asserts. Once UQF_DRY_RUN=true is exported the same call returns (`env;\"true\"). The previous example claimed (`env;\"2026.09.01\") for `backfill_from, which was wrong twice over: nothing in this repository sets UQF_BACKFILL_FROM (a worker's window comes from read_state, not from config - see C-04), and an example whose documented value depends on the caller's ambient environment cannot be verified by anything. `dry_run` is the one key production really reads, via .qwrt.is_dry_run.");
.man.registerFunc (".qwcfg.raw";".qwcfg";"Raw string value for a key, honouring the precedence order. `(),` is load-bearing, not decoration. A single character is an ATOM in q, so a layer holding a one-character value (\"1\", \"7\", \"y\") yields an atom rather than a string, and every comparison against a list of string literals then fails silently - get_flag returned FALSE for a flag configured as \"1\". Normalising once here means no consumer has to remember.";".qwcfg.raw";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qwcfg.errors";".qwcfg";"Errors accumulated by the typed getters below, so one startup reports every bad setting rather than the first.";".qwcfg.errors";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qwcfg.note";".qwcfg";"Private: record a problem and return a null of the right type.";".qwcfg.note";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qwcfg.get_timestamp";".qwcfg";"A required timestamp, e.g. a backfill window bound.";".qwcfg.get_timestamp";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qwcfg.get_positive";".qwcfg";"A required positive long, e.g. a row cap.";".qwcfg.get_positive";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qwcfg.get_symbol";".qwcfg";"A required symbol, e.g. a source_version.";".qwcfg.get_symbol";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qwcfg.truthy";".qwcfg";"The accepted spellings. Note `enlist \"1\"`, not `\"1\"`: a single character is an ATOM in q, so the plain literal would make these mixed atom-and-vector general lists - and `\"true\" in (\"1\";\"true\")` is then a TYPE error, not a false. Match with ~/: rather than `in` for the same reason: comparing a string with `in` against a list of strings is a trap, and `in` on two strings is per-character, which is the related trap one line further on.";".qwcfg.truthy";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qwcfg.falsy";".qwcfg";"The accepted spellings. Note `enlist \"1\"`, not `\"1\"`: a single character is an ATOM in q, so the plain literal would make these mixed atom-and-vector general lists - and `\"true\" in (\"1\";\"true\")` is then a TYPE error, not a false. Match with ~/: rather than `in` for the same reason: comparing a string with `in` against a list of strings is a trap, and `in` on two strings is per-character, which is the related trap one line further on.";".qwcfg.falsy";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qwcfg.get_flag";".qwcfg";"A boolean flag. Absent means false, so a flag is opt-in - which matters for dry_run, where defaulting to true would make a worker silently do nothing.";".qwcfg.get_flag";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qwcfg.reset";".qwcfg";"Reset the accumulated errors, before a fresh resolution.";".qwcfg.reset";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qwcfg.require_valid";".qwcfg";"Throw if anything went wrong, naming every problem at once (ETL-16). Refusing to start beats starting with defaults silently substituted: a worker that begins with a wrong window backfills the wrong data and reports success, which is worse than not starting.";".qwcfg.require_valid";"");
.man.registerArg (".qwcfg.require_valid";"throws";"";"error listing every accumulated problem");

.man.registerFile ("worker_runtime.q";"";".qwrt";"worker_runtime.q - retry classification, dry-run, and the authority split (.qwrt). Implements requirements ETL-13 to ETL-17 of docs/reference/etl-framework-requirements.md, and the retry decisions recorded on issues #71/#72 (question-bank M-04, M-05). The guarantee this file provides is deliberately WEAKER than the one people assume. ETL-13 says so in as many words: \"do not assume exactly-once processing - the framework establishes retry-safe publication and coverage skipping, which is a weaker and more honest guarantee.\" So a window may be fetched twice; what must not happen is that a window is published twice without the ledger knowing, or skipped while reporting success. Three things here are easy to get backwards, so each is enforced rather than commented: 1. Data failures do NOT retry (M-04). Retrying a schema mismatch produces the same mismatch more slowly and buries the real error under N identical ones. Only transport failures retry. 2. Unclassifiable errors are treated as DATA, i.e. terminal. Defaulting the other way means a genuine bug retries until the backoff cap and then fails anyway, having hidden itself for the duration. 3. Dry-run must suppress THREE side effects, not one (ETL-14). Suppressing only the row publication leaves coverage claiming the window is complete - the exact lie the ledger exists to prevent.");
.man.registerFunc (".qwrt.q_owned";".qwrt";"ETL-15's split, written down as data so a diagnostic can cite it and a test can assert nobody quietly moved a concern across the line.";".qwrt.q_owned";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qwrt.airflow_owned";".qwrt";"ETL-15's split, written down as data so a diagnostic can cite it and a test can assert nobody quietly moved a concern across the line.";".qwrt.airflow_owned";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qwrt.retry_boundary";".qwrt";"ETL-15 assigns RETRIES to Airflow, and M-04 has this file retrying transport errors in-process. That reads like a contradiction and is not, so state the resolution plainly rather than leaving the next reader to reconcile it: - in-process retry is WITHIN one task attempt, for a transient blip on a connection this process already holds. Airflow cannot help there; by the time a task fails and is rescheduled the cheap fix is long gone. - task-level retry - \"run this task again\" - is Airflow's, and nothing here reschedules itself, re-enqueues work, or decides how many task attempts there should be. The line is therefore: bounded, in-attempt, transport-only here; everything about whether and when the TASK runs again is Airflow's.";".qwrt.retry_boundary";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qwrt.owner";".qwrt";"Which layer owns a concern, for a diagnostic that would otherwise guess.";".qwrt.owner";".qwrt.owner[`scheduling]  ->  `airflow");
.man.registerArg (".qwrt.owner";"throws";"";"error if the concern belongs to neither layer");
.man.registerArg (".qwrt.owner";"eg";"";".qwrt.owner[`scheduling]  ->  `airflow");
.man.registerFunc (".qwrt.transport_patterns";".qwrt";"Transport failures: the connection, the host, the socket. Retryable, because the next attempt genuinely may differ (M-04).";".qwrt.transport_patterns";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qwrt.data_patterns";".qwrt";"Data failures: the payload is wrong. Terminal, because the next attempt is identical (M-04).";".qwrt.data_patterns";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qwrt.classify";".qwrt";"Classify a caught error string as `transport or `data. Data patterns are tested FIRST, because the overlap is real and the safe direction is terminal: \"schema mismatch on connection to rdb\" mentions a connection but retrying it is pointless. Getting this order wrong turns one clear failure into max_attempts identical ones. An unrecognised error is `data, i.e. terminal - see the header's point 2.";".qwrt.classify";".qwrt.classify[\"connection refused\"]  ->  `transport");
.man.registerArg (".qwrt.classify";"param";"err";"the caught error string");
.man.registerArg (".qwrt.classify";"return";"";"`transport or `data");
.man.registerArg (".qwrt.classify";"eg";"";".qwrt.classify[\"connection refused\"]  ->  `transport");
.man.registerFunc (".qwrt.retryable";".qwrt";"Is this error worth another attempt in this process?";".qwrt.retryable";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qwrt.default_policy";".qwrt";"Bounded backoff, config-driven (M-04). Defaults are deliberately small: this is an in-attempt blip, not a scheduling policy - a worker sitting in backoff for minutes is Airflow's job to time out, not this file's job to wait through.";".qwrt.default_policy";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qwrt.policy";".qwrt";"Resolve the policy from configuration, falling back to the defaults. Read through .qwcfg so the precedence is the one C-02 fixed, rather than a second ad-hoc lookup order that drifts from it.";".qwrt.policy";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qwrt.backoff_ms";".qwrt";"Exponential backoff for attempt n (1-based), capped.";".qwrt.backoff_ms";".qwrt.backoff_ms[.qwrt.default_policy;3]  ->  1000");
.man.registerArg (".qwrt.backoff_ms";"eg";"";".qwrt.backoff_ms[.qwrt.default_policy;3]  ->  1000");
.man.registerFunc (".qwrt.sleep_ms";".qwrt";"Private: wait, in milliseconds. Zero and negative waits do nothing, which is what lets a test set base_delay_ms to 0 and run at full speed rather than sleeping through its own retry assertions.";".qwrt.sleep_ms";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qwrt.with_retry";".qwrt";"Run a niladic function under the retry policy. Returns a dict rather than throwing, because the CALLER decides what a terminal failure means: under M-05 a worker's failed window is terminal and the worker moves on, which is a decision about the run, not about this function.";".qwrt.with_retry";".qwrt.with_retry[.qwrt.default_policy;{42}]`result  ->  42");
.man.registerArg (".qwrt.with_retry";"param";"pol";"a policy dict as returned by `policy`");
.man.registerArg (".qwrt.with_retry";"param";"f";"a niladic function performing the attempt");
.man.registerArg (".qwrt.with_retry";"return";"";"dict of state (`ok or `failed), kind (`none/`transport/`data),");
.man.registerArg (".qwrt.with_retry";"eg";"";".qwrt.with_retry[.qwrt.default_policy;{42}]`result  ->  42");
.man.registerFunc (".qwrt.suppressed_in_dry_run";".qwrt";"The three side effects ETL-14 suppresses, named once so they cannot drift apart. Suppressing a subset is the dangerous case: rows withheld while coverage is still published leaves the ledger asserting a window is complete when nothing was written.";".qwrt.suppressed_in_dry_run";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qwrt.is_dry_run";".qwrt";"Is this run diagnostic-only? Opt-in via .qwcfg.get_flag, which defaults absent-to-false - so a misconfigured worker does real work rather than silently doing none.";".qwrt.is_dry_run";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qwrt.commit";".qwrt";"Perform one named side effect, or record that dry-run withheld it. Every ETL-14-suppressed effect goes through here rather than being guarded inline at its call site, so \"what does dry-run skip\" has one answer that a test can enumerate. Takes the action and its arguments SEPARATELY, applying them with `.` only on the non-dry branch. That is the whole point: a fully-applied projection like f[a;b;c] is not a deferred call in q, it is a CALL - so writing commit[dry;`publish_rows;publish[a;b]] would perform the publication while building the argument, and dry-run would suppress only the recording of an effect that had already happened. The bug would be invisible in the return value, which would correctly say `skipped.";".qwrt.commit";"");
.man.registerArg (".qwrt.commit";"param";"dry";"whether this is a dry run");
.man.registerArg (".qwrt.commit";"param";"effect";"one of suppressed_in_dry_run");
.man.registerArg (".qwrt.commit";"param";"action";"the function performing the effect");
.man.registerArg (".qwrt.commit";"param";"args";"a list of arguments for it; () for a niladic action, which is");
.man.registerArg (".qwrt.commit";"return";"";"(`skipped;effect) or (`done;effect;the action's result)");
.man.registerArg (".qwrt.commit";"throws";"";"error on an unrecognised effect name");
.man.registerFunc (".qwrt.windows";".qwrt";"Split [from_ts;to_ts) into consecutive half-open windows of `width`. ETL-18 names \"window boundaries\" as one of six lifecycle decision points where a wrong answer is silent, and this is where that answer lives. Two properties make it right, and both are tested: - the windows TILE the range: each one's range_to is the next one's range_from, with no overlap and no gap. Overlap double-publishes; a gap leaves data unfetched while coverage composes cleanly over the whole range and reports it complete. - the LAST window is clipped to to_ts, never extended past it. An over-running final window records coverage for a range that was never requested, which a later run then skips. WHAT A \"DAY\" IS HERE (issue #80's L-04, and L-05 with it) A 1D window is 24h of ELAPSED UTC TIME measured from from_ts. It is not a calendar day, not a business date, and not aligned to any venue's session: a range starting at 17:00 produces windows starting at 17:00, and a five-day range over a weekend is five windows, not five trading days. WHAT DEFINES A TRADING DAY FOR THE CANONICAL WORKERS IS NOT KNOWABLE FROM THIS TREE - that answer lived with the bank's calendars, which A-04 keeps out of a public repository. So the assumption is written down instead of guessed at, and tests/q/test_time_zone.q pins it: whoever adds a venue calendar has to change a failing test rather than a comment. Nothing else here has a business-date notion either - .qcov composes half-open intervals and .qdcf counts actual calendar days - and the only day roll this tree knows about is TorQ's EOD reload, which is an operational state rather than a business date. Cutting in UTC is also what makes DST harmless (L-05): a daily window is exactly 24h across a transition, never the 23h or 25h a local calendar day becomes, so coverage keeps tiling exactly. The variable local span is handled where it belongs, in .qsrc's per-source zone conversion.";".qwrt.windows";".qwrt.windows[2026.09.01D00:00;2026.09.04D00:00;1D]  -> 3 daily windows");
.man.registerArg (".qwrt.windows";"param";"from_ts";"range start");
.man.registerArg (".qwrt.windows";"param";"to_ts";"range end, exclusive");
.man.registerArg (".qwrt.windows";"param";"width";"a timespan, e.g. 1D");
.man.registerArg (".qwrt.windows";"return";"";"a table of range_from/range_to");
.man.registerArg (".qwrt.windows";"eg";"";".qwrt.windows[2026.09.01D00:00;2026.09.04D00:00;1D]  -> 3 daily windows");
.man.registerFunc (".qwrt.needs_fetch";".qwrt";"Should this window be fetched, or is it already published (ETL-13)? Version-specific by construction: is_covered takes source_version as a required parameter, so a window covered at v1 does NOT suppress a fetch at v2. That is ETL-10, and it is the direction that matters - the alternative skips a re-extraction the version bump exists to force.";".qwrt.needs_fetch";"");
.man.registerArg (".qwrt.needs_fetch";"return";"";"1b when the window still needs fetching");
.man.registerFunc (".qwrt.remaining";".qwrt";"Narrow a requested range to the parts not yet published (ETL-13). Returns the gaps rather than a yes/no, so a retry after a partial run re-fetches only what is missing instead of the whole range. An empty result means there is nothing to do - which under C-07 is an `idle success, not a failure.";".qwrt.remaining";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qwrt.finish_window";".qwrt";"Complete one window: publish rows, record coverage, save the checkpoint - in that order, and all three behind the dry-run gate. The ORDER is the requirement, not an implementation detail. Coverage is staged only after the publication it describes has happened (ETL-07), and the checkpoint only after coverage, so every possible interruption point leaves an under-claim rather than an over-claim: a re-run redoes work, which retry-safe publication tolerates, instead of skipping work the ledger wrongly believes is done.";".qwrt.finish_window";"");
.man.registerArg (".qwrt.finish_window";"param";"worker";"the worker's name");
.man.registerArg (".qwrt.finish_window";"param";"ds";"the dataset");
.man.registerArg (".qwrt.finish_window";"param";"spec";"the run specification (source_version, range_from, range_to)");
.man.registerArg (".qwrt.finish_window";"param";"from_ts";"window start");
.man.registerArg (".qwrt.finish_window";"param";"to_ts";"window end, exclusive");
.man.registerArg (".qwrt.finish_window";"param";"publish";"a niladic function publishing the window, returning a row");
.man.registerArg (".qwrt.finish_window";"return";"";"dict of the three effects' outcomes plus the row count");
.man.registerFunc (".qwrt.declared_dependencies";".qwrt";"worker -> the TorQ process types it needs a connection to (ETL-16).";".qwrt.declared_dependencies";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qwrt.declare_dependencies";".qwrt";"Declare what a worker needs before it can run.";".qwrt.declare_dependencies";".qwrt.declare_dependencies[`markout_backfill;`tickerplant`hdb]");
.man.registerArg (".qwrt.declare_dependencies";"eg";"";".qwrt.declare_dependencies[`markout_backfill;`tickerplant`hdb]");
.man.registerFunc (".qwrt.connected_override";".qwrt";"Private: which process types currently have a live connection. Reads TorQ's .servers.SERVERS when it is loaded, and returns empty otherwise. Wrapped because this file is unit-tested outside a TorQ process, where .servers does not exist at all - and an unwrapped read would make every test here depend on a running stack. Overridable via connected_override so a test can present a fleet without one.";".qwrt.connected_override";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qwrt.connected";".qwrt";"The proctypes currently reachable in the fleet. Empty when TorQ is absent, which makes require_dependencies report every declared dependency as missing and refuse to start - the direction a total function has to pick, so that a broken probe stops a worker at init rather than letting it run blind.";".qwrt.connected";".qwrt.connected[]");
.man.registerArg (".qwrt.connected";"return";"";"a symbol vector of proctypes, empty outside a TorQ process");
.man.registerArg (".qwrt.connected";"eg";"";".qwrt.connected[]");
.man.registerFunc (".qwrt.require_dependencies";".qwrt";"Fail at INITIALISATION when a declared dependency is unavailable (ETL-16). At init, not at first use: a worker that starts, runs for twenty minutes and then discovers the hdb was never reachable has already published a partial window and burned the operator's time. Reports every missing dependency at once, for the same reason require_contract does.";".qwrt.require_dependencies";"");
.man.registerArg (".qwrt.require_dependencies";"throws";"";"error naming every unavailable dependency");
.man.registerFunc (".qwrt.require_upstream";".qwrt";"Fail at initialisation when an upstream dataset is not published for the requested range (ETL-16's \"coverage precondition\"). Distinct from needs_fetch above, which asks about THIS worker's own output. This asks about its INPUT: a markout backfill over a range whose trades are not yet published would compute markouts against missing trades and record coverage saying it had done so.";".qwrt.require_upstream";"");
.man.registerArg (".qwrt.require_upstream";"throws";"";"error naming the missing upstream ranges");

.man.registerFile ("pipeline_dag.q";"";".qdag";"GENERATED BY scripts/generate_operational_docs.py - DO NOT EDIT. Run `python3 scripts/generate_operational_docs.py` to regenerate; CI runs it with --check, so an edit here fails the build (J-01/J-02). The nine TorQ streaming processes, as .qdag job registrations, so the job graph is complete inside q and a DAG can be ordered or drawn with no Python in the path. GENERATED rather than hand-written because H-05 already decided the Python Pipeline registry is the source of truth. Re-declaring these edges in q by hand would make a second place for them to be wrong, which is H-01's lesson; deriving them the other way would contradict H-05. A generated bridge is the only option that keeps one authority. A FUNCTION rather than bare statements at load time: .qdag.reset[] would otherwise wipe these with no way to get them back, and a test that resets the registry could not restore the real graph.");
.man.registerFunc (".qdag.register_pipelines";".qdag";"Register the nine TorQ streaming processes into the job graph.";".qdag.register_pipelines";".qdag.register_pipelines[]");
.man.registerArg (".qdag.register_pipelines";"return";"";"the process names registered");
.man.registerArg (".qdag.register_pipelines";"eg";"";".qdag.register_pipelines[]");

.man.registerFile ("demo_deals.q";"";".qsdemo";"demo_deals.q - a generic analogue of an external relational deal source (.qsdemo). WHY THIS IS AN ANALOGUE AND NOT A PORT (bank question E-09, answered via A-04) The canonical tree has workers over `marketwarehouse_deals` and `piggybank_general_ledger`. Those names, their schemas, and the business logic over them are bank-internal and must not appear in a public repository - that was decided in A-04 and it is not a technical limitation to be worked around. So this file reproduces the SHAPE of such a source and none of its content: an external relational table of dealt trades, read in bounded windows by a timestamp, landing in a local table. Every column here is one any FX deal capture system would have, and the fixture's values are synthetic. Nothing about the bank's actual schema, naming or logic is recoverable from it. Two consequences worth stating plainly, rather than discovering later: 1. This CANNOT validate the real source contract. ETL-12 asks that live external metadata be validated against the same declaration as the fixture, and the machinery for that is built - but the declaration itself is a guess at a shape nobody here can see. Running `.qsrc.validate_live` against the real source is the only thing that would settle it, and that has to happen on the work machine. 2. A worker built on this is a real worker with a synthetic source, not a demo of a worker. The lifecycle, coverage and retry behaviour it exercises are the production ones.");
.man.registerFunc (".qsdemo.fields";".qsdemo";"The columns this adapter reads, and their q types. Deliberately a small subset of what such a table would have: ETL-12 asks for the required fields, not every field, and declaring columns the worker does not read would make an upstream change to an unused column break the run.";".qsdemo.fields";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qsdemo.types";".qsdemo";"j=long, p=timestamp, s=symbol, s=symbol, f=float, f=float";".qsdemo.types";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qsdemo.target";".qsdemo";"The local table this lands in.";".qsdemo.target";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qsdemo.time_field";".qsdemo";"The column the bounded window is taken on. Declared rather than assumed so .qsrc can window the fixture exactly as the live query windows the source.";".qsdemo.time_field";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qsdemo.row_key";".qsdemo";"What identifies a row uniquely (D-11). `deal_id` is the natural key for a deal-shaped source and is obviously right for this synthetic one, where the fixture generates distinct ids. Worth stating that a natural key is NOT obviously right for a real source: it is only correct if the source guarantees uniqueness, and a source that reuses ids after a purge would silently merge unrelated rows. That choice is per-source, which is why the key is declared here rather than inferred - see docs/architecture/restatement-design.md §2.1.";".qsdemo.row_key";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qsdemo.time_zone";".qsdemo";"The zone deal_time is expressed in (L-06). Stated rather than left to a default, because an unstated zone is exactly the shape of the bug: every later reader assumes UTC while the source may have been handing over wall-clock local time all along, and the two differ by an offset that changes twice a year. `UTC` here is a claim about this source that .qsrc.validate_live can be run against - not an absence of information. It is also the only value that needs no zone table at all, which is why a real integration should push the conversion upstream rather than declare a zone: see .qsrc.local_to_utc for the hour of local timestamps that is irrecoverable in any other arrangement.";".qsdemo.time_zone";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qsdemo.query";".qsdemo";"A PARAMETERISED lambda, never string concatenation (bank question E-08, answered via FE-14). The window bounds are arguments to a functional select evaluated on the remote side, so no caller value is ever spliced into query text. The alternative - building \"select from t where time>=\" ,string range_from - is how a symbol name or a crafted timestamp string becomes an injection, and it is also how a type coercion bug becomes a silent wrong answer rather than an error. Half-open [range_from;range_to) throughout, per ETL-08: >= on the lower bound and < on the upper. Getting that wrong by one operator double-publishes every boundary row, which then appears as a duplicate nobody can explain.";".qsdemo.query";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qsdemo.fixture";".qsdemo";"A synthetic table of the same shape (bank question E-04, answered via A-04 + FE-22/FE-23). Deterministic - fixed values, no .z.p, no random - because a fixture that changes between runs makes a failing assertion impossible to attribute. The one thing it must be is contract-satisfying, and test_source_contract.q asserts exactly that.";".qsdemo.fixture";"");

.man.registerFile ("demo_events.q";"";".qsevt";"demo_events.q - a generic analogue of a venue order/trade event tape (.qsevt). The ingestion half of issue #46. Shape and rationale in docs/architecture/event-tape.md; this file is the ETL-12 source declaration for it. SYNTHETIC BY DESIGN (A-04) Every column is one any venue's tape would carry and every value is invented. Nothing about a real venue's schema, a bank's data, or any business logic is recoverable from it. That is the same standing as demo_deals.q and for the same reason. WHY THIS IS THE MICROSTRUCTURE TAPE AND NOT AN OMS TAPE The seven ROADMAP features blocked on #46 need `action` in {add,cancel, trade}, an AGGRESSOR side, and a per-event size - flow-toxicity inputs. An OMS lifecycle tape (new/ack/fill/cancel/reject over our own orders) unblocks execution-quality metrics instead, which is a different set and largely already served by hit_ratio_by and markout_at_horizons. See docs/architecture/event-tape.md for the comparison.");
.man.registerFunc (".qsevt.fields";".qsevt";"The columns this adapter reads, and their q types. p=timestamp, s=symbol, j=long, f=float. Note `j` for a long, not `l`: that is what `meta` reports, and validate compares against meta - the first draft wrote \"l\" and was refused at registration, correctly.";".qsevt.fields";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qsevt.types";".qsevt";"The columns this adapter reads, and their q types. p=timestamp, s=symbol, j=long, f=float. Note `j` for a long, not `l`: that is what `meta` reports, and validate compares against meta - the first draft wrote \"l\" and was refused at registration, correctly.";".qsevt.types";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qsevt.time_field";".qsevt";"The window is taken on event time.";".qsevt.time_field";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qsevt.row_key";".qsevt";"An event is identified by its order and its action: one order_id produces an add and then exactly one terminal event (a cancel or a trade), so the pair is unique where order_id alone is not. Worth stating because it is the first COMPOSITE key in this tree, and it is what D-11's restatement path will key on if a venue ever corrects an event.";".qsevt.row_key";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qsevt.query";".qsevt";"Parameterised, never concatenated (ETL-08 / FE-14). Half-open [range_from;range_to) per ETL-08 - >= on the lower bound and < on the upper, so a boundary event is published exactly once.";".qsevt.query";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qsevt.fixture";".qsevt";"A deterministic synthetic tape, ordered by time (the sortedness contract in docs/architecture/event-tape.md). Deliberately shaped so the two implemented features have something to measure rather than degenerate: - both sides trade, with a buy-side skew, so signed_trade_flow is nonzero and signed - cancels outnumber trades, as they do on any real venue, so cancel_to_trade_ratio is > 1 rather than a coin flip - one order_id adds then cancels, another adds then trades, so the composite row_key has both terminal shapes present";".qsevt.fixture";"");

.man.registerFile ("demo_deals_backfill.q";"";".qddbf";"demo_deals_backfill.q - the demo deals bounded worker (.qddbf). A declaration over the generic shell in src/etl/core/bounded_worker.q (#124). This file was 238 lines of which four were worker-specific; the other 234 were glue every bounded worker needs, and are now in .qbw once. WHAT IT IS AND IS NOT A real worker with a synthetic source, not a demonstration of a worker. The lifecycle, coverage and retry behaviour are the production ones; only the rows are invented (A-04). Its tests say nothing about the real source's schema, which only .qsrc.validate_live on the work machine can settle. WHY THE GLOBALS ARE STILL DECLARED HERE ETL-01's contract, enforced by .qbfstate.require_contract, requires source_version/range_from/range_to to be names in THIS namespace. Moving them into the shell would make the contract check inspect the shell rather than the worker, and every worker would pass it vacuously. So the shell reads and writes them here - see bounded_worker.q's header.");
.man.registerFunc (".qddbf.source_version";".qddbf";"Explicit and inspectable rather than buried in a call, which is what ETL-02 asks for: a bounded worker must make its bound visible. Written by .qbw.init.";".qddbf.source_version";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qddbf.range_from";".qddbf";"Explicit and inspectable rather than buried in a call, which is what ETL-02 asks for: a bounded worker must make its bound visible. Written by .qbw.init.";".qddbf.range_from";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qddbf.range_to";".qddbf";"Explicit and inspectable rather than buried in a call, which is what ETL-02 asks for: a bounded worker must make its bound visible. Written by .qbw.init.";".qddbf.range_to";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qddbf.handle";".qddbf";"The live handle, or 0Ni when running on the fixture. Resolved at init.";".qddbf.handle";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qddbf.progress";".qddbf";"Run accumulators, written by the shell. Here rather than in .qbw because a q lambda does not close over an enclosing local, so `each` over windows needs a named place to put the running totals - and one per worker, or two workers running in one process would share them.";".qddbf.progress";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qddbf.last_batch";".qddbf";"Run accumulators, written by the shell. Here rather than in .qbw because a q lambda does not close over an enclosing local, so `each` over windows needs a named place to put the running totals - and one per worker, or two workers running in one process would share them.";".qddbf.last_batch";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qddbf.spec";".qddbf";"Ordinary names in this namespace that happen to delegate. A worker needing a genuinely different publish path defines its own here and the shell does not object - .qbw is a default, not an owner.";".qddbf.spec";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qddbf.init";".qddbf";"Ordinary names in this namespace that happen to delegate. A worker needing a genuinely different publish path defines its own here and the shell does not object - .qbw is a default, not an owner.";".qddbf.init";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qddbf.plan";".qddbf";"Ordinary names in this namespace that happen to delegate. A worker needing a genuinely different publish path defines its own here and the shell does not object - .qbw is a default, not an owner.";".qddbf.plan";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qddbf.fetch";".qddbf";"Ordinary names in this namespace that happen to delegate. A worker needing a genuinely different publish path defines its own here and the shell does not object - .qbw is a default, not an owner.";".qddbf.fetch";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qddbf.publish";".qddbf";"Ordinary names in this namespace that happen to delegate. A worker needing a genuinely different publish path defines its own here and the shell does not object - .qbw is a default, not an owner.";".qddbf.publish";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qddbf.checkpoint";".qddbf";"Ordinary names in this namespace that happen to delegate. A worker needing a genuinely different publish path defines its own here and the shell does not object - .qbw is a default, not an owner.";".qddbf.checkpoint";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qddbf.run";".qddbf";"Ordinary names in this namespace that happen to delegate. A worker needing a genuinely different publish path defines its own here and the shell does not object - .qbw is a default, not an owner.";".qddbf.run";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qddbf.cleanup";".qddbf";"Ordinary names in this namespace that happen to delegate. A worker needing a genuinely different publish path defines its own here and the shell does not object - .qbw is a default, not an owner.";".qddbf.cleanup";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qddbf.quality_check";".qddbf";"Refuse a batch that is shaped correctly but cannot be true. Runs between fetch and publish, so a batch that fails is never published and its window is never recorded as covered - the next run plans it again. Before this gate existed the ledger would have recorded a window of nulls or of zero rates as complete, and read as published forever. The three conditions are deliberately ones no correct deal can meet, rather than statistical outlier detection: a non-positive rate or notional is arithmetically impossible for a trade, and a null deal_id cannot be reconciled against anything. A check that fires on merely UNUSUAL data would train its reader to ignore it, and an ignored check is worse than none because it still reads as protection. Returns FAILURES, so an empty table means the batch passed - the shape .qdqc.summarize_checks already uses.";".qddbf.quality_check";".qddbf.quality_check[.qsdemo.fixture[]]  ->  an empty table");
.man.registerArg (".qddbf.quality_check";"param";"batch";"the fetched rows, before publication");
.man.registerArg (".qddbf.quality_check";"return";"";"a table check/status/detail, one row per offending row");
.man.registerArg (".qddbf.quality_check";"eg";"";".qddbf.quality_check[.qsdemo.fixture[]]  ->  an empty table");

.man.registerFile ("demo_events_backfill.q";"";".qevbf";"demo_events_backfill.q - the event-tape bounded worker (.qevbf). A declaration over the generic shell (#124). This is the file that made #124 worth doing: written against the old copy-the-glue pattern it would have been 238 lines, 234 of them duplicated from demo_deals_backfill.q and needing hand-maintenance every time the framework moved. Window width is 0D01:00:00, not demo_deals' 1D, because an event tape is far denser than a deal feed - an hour of events is a comparable amount of work to a day of deals, and a window that takes an hour of wall-clock to fetch is a window whose failure costs an hour.");
.man.registerFunc (".qevbf.source_version";".qevbf";"Declared here, not in the shell: require_contract checks for them in THIS namespace, and moving them would make the check inspect the shell and pass vacuously for every worker. Written by .qbw.init.";".qevbf.source_version";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qevbf.range_from";".qevbf";"Declared here, not in the shell: require_contract checks for them in THIS namespace, and moving them would make the check inspect the shell and pass vacuously for every worker. Written by .qbw.init.";".qevbf.range_from";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qevbf.range_to";".qevbf";"Declared here, not in the shell: require_contract checks for them in THIS namespace, and moving them would make the check inspect the shell and pass vacuously for every worker. Written by .qbw.init.";".qevbf.range_to";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qevbf.progress";".qevbf";"Per-worker accumulators - one set each, or two workers in one process would share their running totals.";".qevbf.progress";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qevbf.last_batch";".qevbf";"Per-worker accumulators - one set each, or two workers in one process would share their running totals.";".qevbf.last_batch";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qevbf.spec";".qevbf";"Ordinary names in this namespace that happen to delegate. A worker needing a genuinely different publish path defines its own here and the shell does not object - .qbw is a default, not an owner.";".qevbf.spec";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qevbf.init";".qevbf";"Ordinary names in this namespace that happen to delegate. A worker needing a genuinely different publish path defines its own here and the shell does not object - .qbw is a default, not an owner.";".qevbf.init";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qevbf.plan";".qevbf";"Ordinary names in this namespace that happen to delegate. A worker needing a genuinely different publish path defines its own here and the shell does not object - .qbw is a default, not an owner.";".qevbf.plan";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qevbf.fetch";".qevbf";"Ordinary names in this namespace that happen to delegate. A worker needing a genuinely different publish path defines its own here and the shell does not object - .qbw is a default, not an owner.";".qevbf.fetch";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qevbf.publish";".qevbf";"Ordinary names in this namespace that happen to delegate. A worker needing a genuinely different publish path defines its own here and the shell does not object - .qbw is a default, not an owner.";".qevbf.publish";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qevbf.checkpoint";".qevbf";"Ordinary names in this namespace that happen to delegate. A worker needing a genuinely different publish path defines its own here and the shell does not object - .qbw is a default, not an owner.";".qevbf.checkpoint";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qevbf.run";".qevbf";"Ordinary names in this namespace that happen to delegate. A worker needing a genuinely different publish path defines its own here and the shell does not object - .qbw is a default, not an owner.";".qevbf.run";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qevbf.cleanup";".qevbf";"Ordinary names in this namespace that happen to delegate. A worker needing a genuinely different publish path defines its own here and the shell does not object - .qbw is a default, not an owner.";".qevbf.cleanup";"");

.man.registerFile ("example_defaults.q";"";".qexdef";"example_defaults.q - shared scaling constants used by scripts/*.q to build synthetic order-book/tick data for worked examples. Unlike forwards.q's ts_col/col_precedence (genuine library output-shape config, read by src/*.q functions), nothing in src/*.q consumes these - they exist so every example script that builds a synthetic book (level prices, level sizes) starts from the same baseline instead of each redefining its own copy of the same numbers. A script may still override locally (e.g. scaling pip_size per symbol) without touching these defaults.");
.man.registerFunc (".qexdef.pip_size";".qexdef";"One pip, in rate terms - the increment example scripts use between adjacent synthetic order book levels (e.g. spot, spot-pip_size, spot-2*pip_size, ...). Not the same thing as pip_factor (10000 for most pairs, 100 for JPY crosses), which is the real library's caller- supplied scaling parameter for markout/eff_spread/slippage/etc - this is 1%10000 in the same units pip_factor scales, purely for building demo data.";".qexdef.pip_size";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qexdef.size_unit";".qexdef";"One synthetic order-book level's baseline notional, in example scripts' synthetic books - a round, readable size (1 million) that scripts scale up per level to build a depth ladder.";".qexdef.size_unit";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qexdef.size_row_drift";".qexdef";"Jitter amount (same units as size_unit) example scripts add per row so consecutive synthetic ticks' sizes aren't identical.";".qexdef.size_row_drift";"");

.man.registerFile ("execution.q";"";".qexec";"execution.q - eFX execution-quality analytics: markouts, effective spread, slippage and quoting/fill statistics. Sign convention used throughout: side is 1 for a client/algo buy (long base currency) and -1 for a sell. Every *cost* style metric (eff_spread, slippage) is positive when it went against the side taking the trade; markout is positive when the market moved in that side's favour after the trade (i.e. positive markout received by a client is \"toxic flow\" from a liquidity provider's point of view - flip side to view it from the LP's side of the same trade).");
.man.registerFunc (".qexec.markout";".qexec";"Post-trade price movement: how far the reference/mid price has moved, in pips, from the trade price by the time ref_price was observed. ref_price may be a single price or a vector of prices at several horizons (t+1s, t+10s, t+60s, ...) - the function vectorises naturally.";".qexec.markout";".qexec.markout[1;1.1000;1.1010;10000]  -> 10f");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.markout";"param";"side";"1 for a buy, -1 for a sell");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.markout";"param";"trade_price";"the execution price");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.markout";"param";"ref_price";"the reference/mid price at the markout horizon (atom or vector)");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.markout";"param";"pip_factor";"10000 for most pairs, 100 for JPY crosses");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.markout";"return";"";"the markout, in pips");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.markout";"eg";"";".qexec.markout[1;1.1000;1.1010;10000]  -> 10f");
.man.registerFunc (".qexec.markout_at_horizons";".qexec";"Markout at one or more time horizons after each trade, looking up the reference mid itself via an as-of join against a quote table - the table-native companion to markout, for when you have trades and quotes as tables rather than an already-aligned ref_price vector. For each trade and each horizon, finds the most recent quote at or before trade_time+horizon (same semantics as kdb+'s aj) and computes markout against its mid. A trade/horizon pair with no quote at or before the target time gets a null ref_price and a null markout_pips - filter with `where not null markout_pips` if you want to drop those rather than see them.";".qexec.markout_at_horizons";".qexec.markout_at_horizons[trades;quotes;0D00:00:01 0D00:00:10]");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.markout_at_horizons";"param";"trades";"table with columns `sym`time`side`trade_price`pip_factor");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.markout_at_horizons";"param";"quotes";"table with columns `sym`time`mid - need not be");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.markout_at_horizons";"param";"horizons";"a timespan, or list of timespans, to look ahead from");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.markout_at_horizons";"return";"";"a table with one row per (trade, horizon), columns reordered");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.markout_at_horizons";"throws";"";"error naming every column missing from trades (`sym`time`side`trade_price`pip_factor)");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.markout_at_horizons";"eg";"";".qexec.markout_at_horizons[trades;quotes;0D00:00:01 0D00:00:10]");
.man.registerFunc (".qexec.eff_spread";".qexec";"Effective spread paid/received relative to the prevailing mid at the moment of execution, in pips. Positive = cost to the side that traded.";".qexec.eff_spread";".qexec.eff_spread[1;1.1002;1.1000;10000]  -> 4f");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.eff_spread";"param";"side";"1 for a buy, -1 for a sell");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.eff_spread";"param";"trade_price";"the execution price");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.eff_spread";"param";"mid_at_trade";"the mid price at the moment of execution");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.eff_spread";"param";"pip_factor";"10000 for most pairs, 100 for JPY crosses");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.eff_spread";"return";"";"the effective spread, in pips");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.eff_spread";"eg";"";".qexec.eff_spread[1;1.1002;1.1000;10000]  -> 4f");
.man.registerFunc (".qexec.slippage";".qexec";"Slippage between a decision/arrival price and the actual execution price, in pips. Positive = cost to the side that traded.";".qexec.slippage";".qexec.slippage[1;1.1000;1.1003;10000]  -> 3f");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.slippage";"param";"side";"1 for a buy, -1 for a sell");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.slippage";"param";"arrival_price";"the decision/arrival price");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.slippage";"param";"exec_price";"the actual execution price");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.slippage";"param";"pip_factor";"10000 for most pairs, 100 for JPY crosses");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.slippage";"return";"";"the slippage, in pips");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.slippage";"eg";"";".qexec.slippage[1;1.1000;1.1003;10000]  -> 3f");
.man.registerFunc (".qexec.fill_ratio";".qexec";"Fraction of quotes/orders that resulted in a fill.";".qexec.fill_ratio";".qexec.fill_ratio[73;100]  -> 0.73");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.fill_ratio";"param";"num_fills";"number of filled orders");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.fill_ratio";"param";"num_quotes";"number of quotes/orders sent");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.fill_ratio";"return";"";"the fill ratio, in [0,1]");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.fill_ratio";"eg";"";".qexec.fill_ratio[73;100]  -> 0.73");
.man.registerFunc (".qexec.reject_ratio";".qexec";"Fraction of trade requests rejected (e.g. under last look).";".qexec.reject_ratio";".qexec.reject_ratio[4;100]  -> 0.04");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.reject_ratio";"param";"num_rejects";"number of rejected requests");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.reject_ratio";"param";"num_requests";"total number of requests");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.reject_ratio";"return";"";"the reject ratio, in [0,1]");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.reject_ratio";"eg";"";".qexec.reject_ratio[4;100]  -> 0.04");
.man.registerFunc (".qexec.hit_ratio_by";".qexec";"Hit ratio (fraction of requests that resulted in a fill/hit), windowed by time, optionally time-bucketed (hourly, daily, ...), and grouped by arbitrary columns - the table-native, group-by-aware companion to the simple fill_ratio above. Named hit_ratio_by rather than get_hit_ratio to match this file's existing fill_ratio/reject_ratio naming (no function in this library uses a get_ prefix) while still reading clearly as \"hit ratio, grouped by ...\". Two modes: `count (unweighted - number of hits / number of requests) and `amount (size-weighted - sum of hit size / sum of total size, so one large hit counts more than many small misses, and one huge miss can swamp the ratio the way it wouldn't in `count mode).";".qexec.hit_ratio_by";".qexec.hit_ratio_by[requests;start_ts;end_ts;0D01:00:00;enlist `sym;`amount]");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.hit_ratio_by";"param";"requests";"table with at least `ts`hit`size, plus whatever columns group_cols names");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.hit_ratio_by";"param";"start_ts";"only consider requests at or after this time");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.hit_ratio_by";"param";"end_ts";"only consider requests at or before this time");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.hit_ratio_by";"param";"bucket_size";"a timespan to floor ts into buckets by (xbar) and");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.hit_ratio_by";"param";"group_cols";"column names to group by in addition to any time");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.hit_ratio_by";"param";"mode";"`count (hit ratio by number of requests) or `amount (hit ratio weighted by size)");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.hit_ratio_by";"return";"";"a table, ts (if bucket_size isn't null) then group_cols columns");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.hit_ratio_by";"throws";"";"error if requests is missing a required column (ts, hit, size,");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.hit_ratio_by";"eg";"";".qexec.hit_ratio_by[requests;start_ts;end_ts;0D01:00:00;enlist `sym;`amount]");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.hit_ratio_by";"eg";"";".qexec.hit_ratio_by[requests;start_ts;end_ts;0Nn;`symbol$();`count]  -> one overall count-mode ratio, no time-bucketing or grouping");
.man.registerFunc (".qexec.reject_ratio_by";".qexec";"Fraction of trade requests rejected, windowed by time, optionally time-bucketed and grouped by arbitrary columns - the table-native, group-by-aware companion to the flat reject_ratio above, and the reject-side mirror of hit_ratio_by. Exists because a flat reject ratio hides exactly what an LP needs to see: whether rejects cluster in the minutes after a spread widening, or on one counterparty, or on one pair. Same two modes as hit_ratio_by - `count (number rejected / number of requests) and `amount (size-weighted, so one large reject counts for more than several small ones).";".qexec.reject_ratio_by";".qexec.reject_ratio_by[requests;start_ts;end_ts;0D01:00:00;enlist `sym;`amount]");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.reject_ratio_by";"param";"requests";"table with at least `ts`reject`size, plus whatever columns group_cols names");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.reject_ratio_by";"param";"start_ts";"only consider requests at or after this time");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.reject_ratio_by";"param";"end_ts";"only consider requests at or before this time");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.reject_ratio_by";"param";"bucket_size";"a timespan to floor ts into buckets by (xbar) and group");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.reject_ratio_by";"param";"group_cols";"column names to group by in addition to any time bucket,");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.reject_ratio_by";"param";"mode";"`count (by number of requests) or `amount (weighted by size)");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.reject_ratio_by";"return";"";"a table, ts (if bucket_size isn't null) then group_cols columns");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.reject_ratio_by";"throws";"";"error if requests is missing a required column (ts, reject, size,");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.reject_ratio_by";"eg";"";".qexec.reject_ratio_by[requests;start_ts;end_ts;0D01:00:00;enlist `sym;`amount]");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.reject_ratio_by";"eg";"";".qexec.reject_ratio_by[requests;start_ts;end_ts;0Nn;`symbol$();`count]  -> one overall count-mode ratio");
.man.registerFunc (".qexec.vwap";".qexec";"Size-weighted average execution price across a set of fills.";".qexec.vwap";".qexec.vwap[1.1000 1.1010 1.1005;1000000 2000000 1000000]  -> 1.100625");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.vwap";"param";"prices";"list of fill prices");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.vwap";"param";"sizes";"list of fill sizes, same length as prices");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.vwap";"return";"";"the size-weighted average price");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.vwap";"eg";"";".qexec.vwap[1.1000 1.1010 1.1005;1000000 2000000 1000000]  -> 1.100625");
.man.registerFunc (".qexec.vwap_expanding";".qexec";"Size-weighted average execution price as of each fill, rather than for the whole set: element i is the VWAP of fills 0..i inclusive. The point of this over `vwap` is honesty about what was knowable when. A single full-window VWAP is a fine summary of fills that already happened, but used as a *benchmark* - comparing fill i against a VWAP computed over a window extending past it - it is look-ahead, and every \"beat VWAP\" figure built on it flatters or punishes by hindsight. The expanding form only ever sees fills up to and including the one being judged.";".qexec.vwap_expanding";".qexec.vwap_expanding[1.1000 1.1010 1.1005;1000000 2000000 1000000]  -> 1.1 1.100667 1.100625");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.vwap_expanding";"param";"prices";"list of fill prices, in execution order");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.vwap_expanding";"param";"sizes";"list of fill sizes, same length as prices, aligned to it");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.vwap_expanding";"return";"";"a vector the same length as prices - the VWAP as of each fill");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.vwap_expanding";"eg";"";".qexec.vwap_expanding[1.1000 1.1010 1.1005;1000000 2000000 1000000]  -> 1.1 1.100667 1.100625");
.man.registerFunc (".qexec.sweep_price";".qexec";"Walk a stack of order book levels to price a sweep of target_size: the blended price you'd get consuming best-to-worst levels until target_size is filled or the book runs out. Pass the ask side (best/lowest price first) to price a buy/sweep-the-offer, or the bid side (best/highest price first) to price a sell/sweep-the-bid - this function doesn't care which side it is, only that prices/sizes are already ordered best-first.";".qexec.sweep_price";".qexec.sweep_price[1.1000 1.1002 1.1005;1000000 1000000 2000000;3000000]  -> `avg_price`worst_price`filled_size`fully_filled!(1.100233;1.1005;3000000;1b)");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.sweep_price";"param";"prices";"level prices, best (most aggressive) first");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.sweep_price";"param";"sizes";"level sizes, same length as prices, aligned to the same levels");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.sweep_price";"param";"target_size";"the size you want to sweep");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.sweep_price";"return";"";"dict `avg_price`worst_price`filled_size`fully_filled - avg_price is");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.sweep_price";"throws";"";"error if target_size is not positive, or prices/sizes differ in length");
.man.registerArg (".qexec.sweep_price";"eg";"";".qexec.sweep_price[1.1000 1.1002 1.1005;1000000 1000000 2000000;3000000]  -> `avg_price`worst_price`filled_size`fully_filled!(1.100233;1.1005;3000000;1b)");

.man.registerFile ("ccy.q";"";".qccy";"ccy.q - currency pair symbol conventions: canonical CURCUR validation and normalization from looser input formats (lowercase, with separators). Canonical form used throughout this library is CURCUR - six uppercase letters, no separator, e.g. `EURUSD (not `eurusd, not \"EUR/USD\").");
.man.registerFunc (".qccy.ccy_to_str";".qccy";"Private: string-coerce x without exploding an already-string input into a list of 1-char strings - `string` on a char vector maps over each char instead of acting as the identity, which is a real q gotcha.";".qccy.ccy_to_str";".qccy.ccy_to_str `EURUSD  -> \"EURUSD\"");
.man.registerArg (".qccy.ccy_to_str";"param";"x";"a symbol or string");
.man.registerArg (".qccy.ccy_to_str";"return";"";"x as a plain string");
.man.registerArg (".qccy.ccy_to_str";"eg";"";".qccy.ccy_to_str `EURUSD  -> \"EURUSD\"");
.man.registerFunc (".qccy.is_ccy_pair";".qccy";"True if x is already in canonical CURCUR form: exactly 6 uppercase letters, no separator.";".qccy.is_ccy_pair";".qccy.is_ccy_pair `EURUSD  -> 1b");
.man.registerArg (".qccy.is_ccy_pair";"param";"x";"a symbol or string");
.man.registerArg (".qccy.is_ccy_pair";"return";"";"1b if x is a valid CURCUR pair, else 0b");
.man.registerArg (".qccy.is_ccy_pair";"eg";"";".qccy.is_ccy_pair `EURUSD  -> 1b");
.man.registerArg (".qccy.is_ccy_pair";"eg";"";".qccy.is_ccy_pair \"eur/usd\"  -> 0b");
.man.registerFunc (".qccy.normalize_ccy_pair";".qccy";"Normalize a currency pair into canonical CURCUR form: uppercases and strips common separators (/, -, _, space).";".qccy.normalize_ccy_pair";".qccy.normalize_ccy_pair \"eur/usd\"  -> `EURUSD");
.man.registerArg (".qccy.normalize_ccy_pair";"param";"x";"a symbol or string, e.g. `eurusd, \"EUR/USD\", \"eur-usd\"");
.man.registerArg (".qccy.normalize_ccy_pair";"return";"";"the canonical CURCUR symbol, e.g. `EURUSD");
.man.registerArg (".qccy.normalize_ccy_pair";"throws";"";"error if x cannot be normalized to a 6-letter CURCUR pair");
.man.registerArg (".qccy.normalize_ccy_pair";"eg";"";".qccy.normalize_ccy_pair \"eur/usd\"  -> `EURUSD");
.man.registerFunc (".qccy.ccy_pair_symbol";".qccy";"Build a canonical CURCUR pair symbol from a 3-letter base and quote currency code.";".qccy.ccy_pair_symbol";".qccy.ccy_pair_symbol[`EUR;`USD]  -> `EURUSD");
.man.registerArg (".qccy.ccy_pair_symbol";"param";"base";"3-letter base currency code, e.g. `EUR or \"eur\"");
.man.registerArg (".qccy.ccy_pair_symbol";"param";"quote";"3-letter quote currency code, e.g. `USD or \"usd\"");
.man.registerArg (".qccy.ccy_pair_symbol";"return";"";"the canonical CURCUR symbol, e.g. `EURUSD");
.man.registerArg (".qccy.ccy_pair_symbol";"throws";"";"error if base or quote is not a 3-letter alphabetic code");
.man.registerArg (".qccy.ccy_pair_symbol";"eg";"";".qccy.ccy_pair_symbol[`EUR;`USD]  -> `EURUSD");
.man.registerFunc (".qccy.ccy_pair_legs";".qccy";"Split a currency pair (any reasonable format - normalized first) into its base and quote 3-letter currency codes.";".qccy.ccy_pair_legs";".qccy.ccy_pair_legs `EURUSD  -> `base`quote!(`EUR;`USD)");
.man.registerArg (".qccy.ccy_pair_legs";"param";"pair";"a symbol or string, e.g. `EURUSD, \"eur/usd\"");
.man.registerArg (".qccy.ccy_pair_legs";"return";"";"dict `base`quote!(baseSym;quoteSym)");
.man.registerArg (".qccy.ccy_pair_legs";"throws";"";"error if pair cannot be normalized to a 6-letter CURCUR pair");
.man.registerArg (".qccy.ccy_pair_legs";"eg";"";".qccy.ccy_pair_legs `EURUSD  -> `base`quote!(`EUR;`USD)");

.man.registerFile ("daycount.q";"";".qdcf";"daycount.q - day count fraction conventions used to turn a pair of dates into the year fraction t consumed by rates.q / forwards.q / options.q. NOTE ON q ARITHMETIC: q has no operator precedence (strictly right to left evaluation), so every mixed +/-/* expression below is built from explicitly parenthesised, named intermediate terms rather than dense one-liners - see src/foundation/stats.q for the bug this avoids.");
.man.registerFunc (".qdcf.dcf_act_360";".qdcf";"Actual/360: (d2-d1) actual calendar days, annualised on a 360-day year. Common for USD, EUR money-market and most FX deposit/forward calcs.";".qdcf.dcf_act_360";".qdcf.dcf_act_360[2024.01.01;2025.01.01]  -> 1.016667 (2024 is a leap year)");
.man.registerArg (".qdcf.dcf_act_360";"param";"d1";"start date");
.man.registerArg (".qdcf.dcf_act_360";"param";"d2";"end date");
.man.registerArg (".qdcf.dcf_act_360";"return";"";"(d2-d1) actual days, divided by 360");
.man.registerArg (".qdcf.dcf_act_360";"eg";"";".qdcf.dcf_act_360[2024.01.01;2025.01.01]  -> 1.016667 (2024 is a leap year)");
.man.registerFunc (".qdcf.dcf_act_365";".qdcf";"Actual/365 (Fixed): (d2-d1) actual calendar days, annualised on a 365-day year. Common for GBP money markets.";".qdcf.dcf_act_365";".qdcf.dcf_act_365[2023.01.01;2024.01.01]  -> 1f");
.man.registerArg (".qdcf.dcf_act_365";"param";"d1";"start date");
.man.registerArg (".qdcf.dcf_act_365";"param";"d2";"end date");
.man.registerArg (".qdcf.dcf_act_365";"return";"";"(d2-d1) actual days, divided by 365");
.man.registerArg (".qdcf.dcf_act_365";"eg";"";".qdcf.dcf_act_365[2023.01.01;2024.01.01]  -> 1f");
.man.registerFunc (".qdcf.dcf_30e_360";".qdcf";"30E/360 (Eurobond basis): each month treated as having 30 days. Day-of-month is capped at 30 for both dates (the simple European variant - it does not carry the US/NASD end-of-February special case).";".qdcf.dcf_30e_360";".qdcf.dcf_30e_360[2024.01.15;2024.02.15]  -> 0.08333333 (30/360)");
.man.registerArg (".qdcf.dcf_30e_360";"param";"d1";"start date");
.man.registerArg (".qdcf.dcf_30e_360";"param";"d2";"end date");
.man.registerArg (".qdcf.dcf_30e_360";"return";"";"the 30E/360 year fraction between d1 and d2");
.man.registerArg (".qdcf.dcf_30e_360";"eg";"";".qdcf.dcf_30e_360[2024.01.15;2024.02.15]  -> 0.08333333 (30/360)");
.man.registerFunc (".qdcf.year_frac";".qdcf";"Dispatch to a day count convention by name.";".qdcf.year_frac";".qdcf.year_frac[`act360;2024.01.01;2025.01.01]  -> 1.016667");
.man.registerArg (".qdcf.year_frac";"param";"conv";"one of `act360`act365`30e360");
.man.registerArg (".qdcf.year_frac";"param";"d1";"start date");
.man.registerArg (".qdcf.year_frac";"param";"d2";"end date");
.man.registerArg (".qdcf.year_frac";"return";"";"the year fraction between d1 and d2 under the chosen convention");
.man.registerArg (".qdcf.year_frac";"throws";"";"error if conv is not one of the supported symbols");
.man.registerArg (".qdcf.year_frac";"eg";"";".qdcf.year_frac[`act360;2024.01.01;2025.01.01]  -> 1.016667");

.man.registerFile ("rates.q";"";".qrates";"rates.q - discount/growth factors and compounding conversions. t is a year fraction (see daycount.q), r a decimal annual rate (0.05 = 5%).");
.man.registerFunc (".qrates.growth_simple";".qrates";"Simple-compounding growth factor.";".qrates.growth_simple";".qrates.growth_simple[0.05;1]  -> 1.05");
.man.registerArg (".qrates.growth_simple";"param";"r";"decimal annual rate");
.man.registerArg (".qrates.growth_simple";"param";"t";"year fraction");
.man.registerArg (".qrates.growth_simple";"return";"";"1+r*t");
.man.registerArg (".qrates.growth_simple";"eg";"";".qrates.growth_simple[0.05;1]  -> 1.05");
.man.registerFunc (".qrates.growth_cont";".qrates";"Continuous-compounding growth factor.";".qrates.growth_cont";".qrates.growth_cont[0.05;1]  -> 1.051271");
.man.registerArg (".qrates.growth_cont";"param";"r";"decimal annual rate");
.man.registerArg (".qrates.growth_cont";"param";"t";"year fraction");
.man.registerArg (".qrates.growth_cont";"return";"";"exp(r*t)");
.man.registerArg (".qrates.growth_cont";"eg";"";".qrates.growth_cont[0.05;1]  -> 1.051271");
.man.registerFunc (".qrates.df_simple";".qrates";"Simple-compounding discount factor.";".qrates.df_simple";".qrates.df_simple[0.05;1]  -> 0.952381");
.man.registerArg (".qrates.df_simple";"param";"r";"decimal annual rate");
.man.registerArg (".qrates.df_simple";"param";"t";"year fraction");
.man.registerArg (".qrates.df_simple";"return";"";"1/(1+r*t)");
.man.registerArg (".qrates.df_simple";"eg";"";".qrates.df_simple[0.05;1]  -> 0.952381");
.man.registerFunc (".qrates.df_cont";".qrates";"Continuous-compounding discount factor.";".qrates.df_cont";".qrates.df_cont[0.05;1]  -> 0.9512294");
.man.registerArg (".qrates.df_cont";"param";"r";"decimal annual rate");
.man.registerArg (".qrates.df_cont";"param";"t";"year fraction");
.man.registerArg (".qrates.df_cont";"return";"";"exp(-r*t)");
.man.registerArg (".qrates.df_cont";"eg";"";".qrates.df_cont[0.05;1]  -> 0.9512294");
.man.registerFunc (".qrates.simple_to_cont";".qrates";"Convert a simple-compounded rate to the equivalent continuously compounded rate over the same period t.";".qrates.simple_to_cont";".qrates.simple_to_cont[0.05;1]  -> 0.04879016");
.man.registerArg (".qrates.simple_to_cont";"param";"r";"simple decimal annual rate");
.man.registerArg (".qrates.simple_to_cont";"param";"t";"year fraction");
.man.registerArg (".qrates.simple_to_cont";"return";"";"the continuously compounded rate with the same growth over t");
.man.registerArg (".qrates.simple_to_cont";"eg";"";".qrates.simple_to_cont[0.05;1]  -> 0.04879016");
.man.registerFunc (".qrates.cont_to_simple";".qrates";"Convert a continuously compounded rate to the equivalent simple rate over the same period t.";".qrates.cont_to_simple";".qrates.cont_to_simple[0.04879016;1]  -> 0.05");
.man.registerArg (".qrates.cont_to_simple";"param";"r";"continuously compounded decimal annual rate");
.man.registerArg (".qrates.cont_to_simple";"param";"t";"year fraction");
.man.registerArg (".qrates.cont_to_simple";"return";"";"the simple rate with the same growth over t");
.man.registerArg (".qrates.cont_to_simple";"eg";"";".qrates.cont_to_simple[0.04879016;1]  -> 0.05");

.man.registerFile ("stats.q";"";".qstats";"stats.q - normal distribution helpers used throughout uqf. ncdf/npdf use the Abramowitz & Stegun 7.1.26 rational approximation to the error function (max absolute error ~1.5e-7). inv_ncdf uses Peter Acklam's rational approximation to the inverse standard normal CDF (max relative error ~1.15e-9), released by its author into the public domain. See: https://web.archive.org/web/20151030215612/http://home.online.no/~pjacklam/notes/invnorm/");
.man.registerFunc (".qstats.PI";".qstats";"The constant pi, used by npdf.";".qstats.PI";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qstats.horner_eval";".qstats";"Evaluate a polynomial at x via Horner's method - the one place q's lack of operator precedence for hand-written Horner chains is worked out and tested, so no other formula in this repo has to re-derive it. See the kdb-q-conventions skill for why (`a*x+b` means `a*(x+b)` in q, not `(a*x)+b`, which silently broke early drafts of this function).";".qstats.horner_eval";".qstats.horner_eval[2 3 4;5]  -> 69 (i.e. 2*5*5+3*5+4)");
.man.registerArg (".qstats.horner_eval";"param";"coeffs";"polynomial coefficients, highest degree first, constant");
.man.registerArg (".qstats.horner_eval";"param";"x";"the point to evaluate the polynomial at");
.man.registerArg (".qstats.horner_eval";"return";"";"coeffs[0]*x^n + coeffs[1]*x^(n-1) + ... + coeffs[n]");
.man.registerArg (".qstats.horner_eval";"eg";"";".qstats.horner_eval[2 3 4;5]  -> 69 (i.e. 2*5*5+3*5+4)");
.man.registerFunc (".qstats.npdf";".qstats";"Standard normal probability density function n(x).";".qstats.npdf";".qstats.npdf 0f  -> 0.3989423");
.man.registerArg (".qstats.npdf";"param";"x";"point(s) to evaluate at (atom or vector)");
.man.registerArg (".qstats.npdf";"return";"";"the standard normal density at x");
.man.registerArg (".qstats.npdf";"eg";"";".qstats.npdf 0f  -> 0.3989423");
.man.registerFunc (".qstats.ncdf";".qstats";"Standard normal cumulative distribution function N(x).";".qstats.ncdf";".qstats.ncdf 1.96  -> 0.9750022");
.man.registerArg (".qstats.ncdf";"param";"x";"point(s) to evaluate at (atom or vector)");
.man.registerArg (".qstats.ncdf";"return";"";"P(Z is at most x) for a standard normal Z");
.man.registerArg (".qstats.ncdf";"eg";"";".qstats.ncdf 1.96  -> 0.9750022");
.man.registerFunc (".qstats.inv_ncdf";".qstats";"Inverse standard normal CDF (probit function).";".qstats.inv_ncdf";".qstats.inv_ncdf 0.95  -> 1.644854 (the one-tailed 95% z-score)");
.man.registerArg (".qstats.inv_ncdf";"param";"p";"probability, strictly within (0,1); an atom or a list of atoms");
.man.registerArg (".qstats.inv_ncdf";"return";"";"x such that ncdf[x]~p");
.man.registerArg (".qstats.inv_ncdf";"throws";"";"error if any element of p is not strictly between 0 and 1");
.man.registerArg (".qstats.inv_ncdf";"eg";"";".qstats.inv_ncdf 0.95  -> 1.644854 (the one-tailed 95% z-score)");

.man.registerFile ("data.q";"";".qdata";"data.q - optional Databento parquet data access, keyed by symbol and date. Not loaded by src/init.q: it needs a real kdb+/KDB-X interpreter (PeachQ has no `2:`, so it can't load KX's native pq module) and a local .env pointing at the data. Load explicitly with `\\l src/integrations/data.q`. Expects DATABENTO_DATA_DIR (from a repo-root .env file, or the OS environment) to point at a folder laid out as: <DATABENTO_DATA_DIR>/<SYMBOL>/<SYMBOL>_<YYYY-MM-DD>_raw_mbp-10_us_hours.parquet");
.man.registerFunc (".qdata.parseEnv";".qdata";"Parse a .env-style file (KEY=VALUE lines; blank lines and lines starting with `#` are ignored) into a dict. Missing file -> empty dict, so this is safe to call unconditionally at load time.";".qdata.parseEnv";".qdata.parseEnv[`:.env]");
.man.registerArg (".qdata.parseEnv";"param";"file";"the .env file to parse, e.g. `:.env`");
.man.registerArg (".qdata.parseEnv";"return";"";"a symbol!string dict of the parsed key/value pairs");
.man.registerArg (".qdata.parseEnv";"eg";"";".qdata.parseEnv[`:.env]");
.man.registerFunc (".qdata.env";".qdata";"Parsed contents of the repo-root .env file, if present - see parseEnv.";".qdata.env";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qdata.cfg";".qdata";"Look up a config value: the OS environment first, then the parsed .env dict, then the given default.";".qdata.cfg";".qdata.cfg[`DATABENTO_DATA_DIR;::]");
.man.registerArg (".qdata.cfg";"param";"k";"the variable name, e.g. `DATABENTO_DATA_DIR`");
.man.registerArg (".qdata.cfg";"param";"dflt";"fallback value, or generic null (::) to error if unset");
.man.registerArg (".qdata.cfg";"return";"";"the string value");
.man.registerArg (".qdata.cfg";"throws";"";"configMissing if k isn't set anywhere and no default was given");
.man.registerArg (".qdata.cfg";"eg";"";".qdata.cfg[`DATABENTO_DATA_DIR;::]");
.man.registerFunc (".qdata.databentoDir";".qdata";"Root folder for locally-available Databento parquet dumps (DATABENTO_DATA_DIR), one subfolder per symbol.";".qdata.databentoDir";".qdata.databentoDir[]");
.man.registerArg (".qdata.databentoDir";"return";"";"the configured folder path, as a string");
.man.registerArg (".qdata.databentoDir";"throws";"";"configMissing if DATABENTO_DATA_DIR isn't set anywhere");
.man.registerArg (".qdata.databentoDir";"eg";"";".qdata.databentoDir[]");
.man.registerFunc (".qdata.databentoFile";".qdata";"Expected path to one symbol/date's daily Databento MBP-10 parquet file.";".qdata.databentoFile";".qdata.databentoFile[`AAPL;2026.02.25]");
.man.registerArg (".qdata.databentoFile";"param";"sym";"ticker symbol, e.g. `AAPL`");
.man.registerArg (".qdata.databentoFile";"param";"date";"a q date, e.g. 2026.02.25");
.man.registerArg (".qdata.databentoFile";"return";"";"an hsym (file symbol) - the file need not exist yet");
.man.registerArg (".qdata.databentoFile";"eg";"";".qdata.databentoFile[`AAPL;2026.02.25]");
.man.registerFunc (".qdata.pqm";".qdata";"Private: the memo cell for pqModule below. Declared BEFORE that function's doc block rather than between it and the definition: a qDoc block belongs to whatever follows it, so sitting in the middle made the docs describe `pqm` while their own @eg named `pqModule`.";".qdata.pqm";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qdata.pqModule";".qdata";"KX's official pq module (github kx.com KDB-X distribution), loaded and memoized on first use rather than at file-load time, so simply loading data.q doesn't require a module-capable interpreter.";".qdata.pqModule";".qdata.pqModule[][`pq]");
.man.registerArg (".qdata.pqModule";"return";"";"the pq module namespace, exposing `.pq` (open file), `.op`/`.rd`");
.man.registerArg (".qdata.pqModule";"throws";"";"if `use` (KDB-X's module loader) is unavailable - real kdb+/KDB-X");
.man.registerArg (".qdata.pqModule";"eg";"";".qdata.pqModule[][`pq]");
.man.registerFunc (".qdata.getBySymbolDate";".qdata";"Open one symbol/date's Databento parquet file as a lazy, row-group-pruned virtual table (via pqModule) - select from it like a normal table, e.g. select avg price by 1 xbar ts_event.minute from .qdata.getBySymbolDate[`AAPL;2026.02.25]";".qdata.getBySymbolDate";".qdata.getBySymbolDate[`AAPL;2026.02.25]");
.man.registerArg (".qdata.getBySymbolDate";"param";"sym";"ticker symbol, e.g. `AAPL`");
.man.registerArg (".qdata.getBySymbolDate";"param";"date";"a q date, e.g. 2026.02.25");
.man.registerArg (".qdata.getBySymbolDate";"return";"";"a virtual table handle over the matching parquet file");
.man.registerArg (".qdata.getBySymbolDate";"throws";"";"fileNotFound if no file exists for that symbol/date");
.man.registerArg (".qdata.getBySymbolDate";"eg";"";".qdata.getBySymbolDate[`AAPL;2026.02.25]");

.man.registerFile ("book.q";"";".qbook";"book.q - reshape an incorrectly-ingested wide order book table (one column per depth level, and identifier columns stored as strings instead of symbols) into the vector-column book dict shape forwards.q's cross_book/cross_book_at_sizes/sweep_price expect: `bid_prices`bid_sizes`ask_prices`ask_sizes, each a level-0-first (best- first) list per row.");
.man.registerFunc (".qbook.fold_level_columns";".qbook";"Fold N per-level source columns into one vector-valued column, for each group in level_groups. Each row of the resulting column holds the N source values for that row, in the given column order - so level-0-first ordering is the caller's responsibility (derive_level_groups builds that order automatically from a naming convention; sort by hand otherwise). Columns not mentioned in any group pass through untouched.";".qbook.fold_level_columns";".qbook.fold_level_columns[tbl;(enlist `bid_prices)!(enlist `bid0`bid1)]");
.man.registerArg (".qbook.fold_level_columns";"param";"tbl";"the source table");
.man.registerArg (".qbook.fold_level_columns";"param";"level_groups";"dict target_col!ordered_source_cols - one entry per");
.man.registerArg (".qbook.fold_level_columns";"return";"";"tbl with each group's source columns replaced by one target_col");
.man.registerArg (".qbook.fold_level_columns";"eg";"";".qbook.fold_level_columns[tbl;(enlist `bid_prices)!(enlist `bid0`bid1)]");
.man.registerFunc (".qbook.starts_with";".qbook";"Private: true if s starts with prefix - a plain substring compare, not `like`, since `like`'s \"_\" wildcard would misfire on prefixes such as \"bid_px_\".";".qbook.starts_with";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qbook.all_digit_string";".qbook";"Private: true if s is non-empty and every character is a decimal digit.";".qbook.all_digit_string";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qbook.sorted_source_cols_for_prefix";".qbook";"Private: the ordered source columns for one (prefix;target_col) naming rule - see derive_level_groups.";".qbook.sorted_source_cols_for_prefix";"");
.man.registerArg (".qbook.sorted_source_cols_for_prefix";"throws";"";"error if the matched levels aren't a contiguous 0..N-1 run");
.man.registerFunc (".qbook.derive_level_groups";".qbook";"Derive a level_groups spec (target_col!ordered_source_cols, level-0-first) from a table's column names, given a naming convention: a column matches a (prefix;target_col) rule when its name starts with prefix and everything after the prefix is digits (that remainder is the level index) - so bid0..bid9 match prefix \"bid\", and Databento-style bid_px_00..bid_px_09 match prefix \"bid_px_\". Columns that don't match any rule this way are simply not part of that group; a name like \"bidSize0\" does not match prefix \"bid\" (its remainder \"Size0\" is not all digits), which is what keeps \"bid\" and \"bidSize\" groups from colliding.";".qbook.derive_level_groups";".qbook.derive_level_groups[`bid0`bid1`bidSize0`bidSize1;enlist (\"bid\";`bid_prices)]");
.man.registerArg (".qbook.derive_level_groups";"param";"col_names";"the source table's column names");
.man.registerArg (".qbook.derive_level_groups";"param";"prefix_targets";"list of (prefix;target_col) pairs, e.g.");
.man.registerArg (".qbook.derive_level_groups";"return";"";"dict target_col!ordered_source_cols, ready for fold_level_columns");
.man.registerArg (".qbook.derive_level_groups";"throws";"";"error if a group's parsed level indices aren't a contiguous");
.man.registerArg (".qbook.derive_level_groups";"eg";"";".qbook.derive_level_groups[`bid0`bid1`bidSize0`bidSize1;enlist (\"bid\";`bid_prices)]");
.man.registerArg (".qbook.derive_level_groups";"eg";"";".qbook.derive_level_groups[`bid_px_00`bid_px_01`ask_px_00`ask_px_01;((\"bid_px_\";`bid_prices);(\"ask_px_\";`ask_prices))]  -> Databento-style zero-padded prefixes, two rules at once");
.man.registerFunc (".qbook.symbolize_columns";".qbook";"Cast the named columns of tbl from string (cells are char vectors) to symbol. A column already type 11h is left untouched, so re-running this is idempotent. Uses `$` directly on the whole column (it maps over each char-vector cell automatically) rather than `string` first - see kdb-q-conventions' string-vs-symbol gotcha.";".qbook.symbolize_columns";".qbook.symbolize_columns[tbl;`sym`side]");
.man.registerArg (".qbook.symbolize_columns";"param";"tbl";"the table (already relevelled, if applicable)");
.man.registerArg (".qbook.symbolize_columns";"param";"sym_cols";"explicit list of column names to cast to symbol");
.man.registerArg (".qbook.symbolize_columns";"return";"";"tbl with sym_cols cast to symbol");
.man.registerArg (".qbook.symbolize_columns";"eg";"";".qbook.symbolize_columns[tbl;`sym`side]");
.man.registerFunc (".qbook.is_string_column";".qbook";"Private: true if column col of tbl is a \"string\" column - its cells are char vectors (type 10h each), not a symbol column. The column as a whole is type 0h (a general list of char vectors), not 10h itself - same gotcha as ccy_to_str.";".qbook.is_string_column";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qbook.candidate_symbol_columns";".qbook";"Candidate identifier-like string columns that are LIKELY mis-typed and should be symbols - advisory only, never applied automatically; run symbolize_columns yourself on whichever candidates you accept. A string column qualifies when either its name is in the caller-supplied allowlist, or its distinct-value cardinality is low relative to row count. Only run this on tables/columns you don't already know are large free text - `distinct` is pathologically slow on huge, high-cardinality vectors under the PeachQ interpreter used for local dev in this repo.";".qbook.candidate_symbol_columns";".qbook.candidate_symbol_columns[tbl;`sym`side;0.1]");
.man.registerArg (".qbook.candidate_symbol_columns";"param";"tbl";"the table to inspect");
.man.registerArg (".qbook.candidate_symbol_columns";"param";"allowlist";"column names always flagged when present and string-typed, e.g. `sym`side`exchange`venue`ccy");
.man.registerArg (".qbook.candidate_symbol_columns";"param";"cardinality_ratio";"flag a string column when (distinct count / row count) is below this ratio");
.man.registerArg (".qbook.candidate_symbol_columns";"return";"";"list of column names likely to be mis-typed symbol columns");
.man.registerArg (".qbook.candidate_symbol_columns";"eg";"";".qbook.candidate_symbol_columns[tbl;`sym`side;0.1]");
.man.registerFunc (".qbook.book_from_wide_levels";".qbook";"Fix an incorrectly-ingested wide order book table in one call: folds each group of per-level columns into a single vector column (fold_level_columns), then casts sym_cols from string to symbol (symbolize_columns). Does not guess/auto-apply candidate_symbol_columns - detection is a separate, explicitly-invoked helper the caller consults first.";".qbook.book_from_wide_levels";".qbook.book_from_wide_levels[tbl;.qbook.derive_level_groups[cols tbl;prefix_targets];`sym`side]");
.man.registerArg (".qbook.book_from_wide_levels";"param";"tbl";"the incorrectly-shaped source table");
.man.registerArg (".qbook.book_from_wide_levels";"param";"level_groups";"dict target_col!ordered_source_cols (already");
.man.registerArg (".qbook.book_from_wide_levels";"param";"sym_cols";"explicit list of column names to cast string->symbol");
.man.registerArg (".qbook.book_from_wide_levels";"return";"";"the corrected table");
.man.registerArg (".qbook.book_from_wide_levels";"eg";"";".qbook.book_from_wide_levels[tbl;.qbook.derive_level_groups[cols tbl;prefix_targets];`sym`side]");

.man.registerFile ("dqchecks.q";"";".qdqc";"dqchecks.q - data quality / risk limit checks that turn other modules' computations into an \"is this actually fine?\" report: one row per (entity, check), a status column, sorted so whatever needs attention sorts first. Mirrors positions.q's reconcile_trades - the same report-a-table-of-breaks shape, generalized beyond just book-vs-reference reconciliation to per-entity threshold checks (position/currency-exposure/ VaR limits) and quotes-table sanity (crossed books, outlier spreads, stale quotes). Deliberately doesn't overlap with what other modules already do: positions.q's reconcile_trades already covers \"does my book match a reference\" - this module never re-derives a position book itself, it only ever checks a caller-supplied metric/exposure/spread against a caller-supplied limit. Every src/*.q function elsewhere in this library throws immediately on a bad input (require_quotes_cols, ccy_pair_symbol's length checks, ...) - this module is the opposite: it never throws for a business-level problem (a breached limit, a stale quote), since the whole point is to surface many possible problems in one report rather than stopping at the first one. It still throws for genuinely malformed input (a quotes table missing required columns), same as everywhere else. Requires microstructure.q (spread_bps, for check_market_data_quality) and forwards.q (require_quotes_cols) to be loaded first.");
.man.registerFunc (".qdqc.status_for";".qdqc";"Private: metric vs. limit -> a 3-way status. `unmonitored (limit is null) is deliberately distinct from `ok - a limit nobody configured is itself an actionable gap, not a silent pass; surfacing it is the point of a dedicated status rather than defaulting an unset limit to \"no limit\" (= always ok) or \"zero limit\" (= always breached), either of which would hide the real issue (nobody decided a limit for this entity yet).";".qdqc.status_for";"");
.man.registerArg (".qdqc.status_for";"param";"metric";"the quantity being checked (already abs'd/signed however");
.man.registerArg (".qdqc.status_for";"param";"limit";"the configured limit for this entity, or 0Nf if unconfigured");
.man.registerArg (".qdqc.status_for";"return";"";"`ok, `breach, or `unmonitored");
.man.registerFunc (".qdqc.limit_for";".qdqc";"Private: the configured limit for one entity out of a limits table - 0Nf if that entity has no row in limits. Bracket-indexes limits by key_col (a column-name symbol, e.g. `sym or `ccy) rather than using a qSQL `where` clause, so the entity-id parameter can be named anything without the \"local variable shadows the table's own column of the same name\" hazard a `where col=col`-style clause would hit if they were named alike (see microstructure.q's quotes_for_sym, which sidesteps the same hazard by naming its parameter target_sym rather than sym).";".qdqc.limit_for";"");
.man.registerArg (".qdqc.limit_for";"param";"limits";"a table with an entity-id column (named per key_col) and a");
.man.registerArg (".qdqc.limit_for";"param";"key_col";"the entity-id column's name, e.g. `sym or `ccy");
.man.registerArg (".qdqc.limit_for";"param";"id";"the entity to look up");
.man.registerArg (".qdqc.limit_for";"return";"";"limits' configured limit for id, or 0Nf if id isn't in limits");
.man.registerFunc (".qdqc.check_limit";".qdqc";"Generic per-entity threshold check: is each row's metric within its configured limit? The shared engine every named check below (position notional, currency exposure, and - by direct use, no wrapper needed - VaR) is a thin wrapper over: build entity/metric, call this.";".qdqc.check_limit";".qdqc.check_limit[([] sym:`EURUSD`GBPUSD; metric:1200000 400000f); ([] sym:enlist `EURUSD; limit:enlist 1000000f); `sym]");
.man.registerArg (".qdqc.check_limit";"param";"metrics";"a table with an entity-id column (named per key_col) and");
.man.registerArg (".qdqc.check_limit";"param";"limits";"a table with the same entity-id column and a `limit`");
.man.registerArg (".qdqc.check_limit";"param";"key_col";"the shared entity-id column's name, e.g. `sym or `ccy");
.man.registerArg (".qdqc.check_limit";"return";"";"metrics with a `limit` and `status` column appended (`ok`,");
.man.registerArg (".qdqc.check_limit";"eg";"";".qdqc.check_limit[([] sym:`EURUSD`GBPUSD; metric:1200000 400000f); ([] sym:enlist `EURUSD; limit:enlist 1000000f); `sym]");
.man.registerFunc (".qdqc.check_position_notional_limits";".qdqc";"Position notional (abs qty, matching risk.q's pnl's own notional convention - see positions.q's unrealized_pnl) vs. a per-sym limit - check_limit specialized to a position book's own shape, so callers don't have to hand-build the entity/metric table themselves.";".qdqc.check_position_notional_limits";".qdqc.check_position_notional_limits[pos;([] sym:enlist `EURUSD; limit:enlist 1000000f)]");
.man.registerArg (".qdqc.check_position_notional_limits";"param";"pos";"a position book (see positions.q's empty_book)");
.man.registerArg (".qdqc.check_position_notional_limits";"param";"limits";"a table `sym`limit - the max notional (abs qty, base");
.man.registerArg (".qdqc.check_position_notional_limits";"return";"";"see check_limit - columns sym/metric/limit/status");
.man.registerArg (".qdqc.check_position_notional_limits";"eg";"";".qdqc.check_position_notional_limits[pos;([] sym:enlist `EURUSD; limit:enlist 1000000f)]");
.man.registerFunc (".qdqc.check_ccy_exposure_limits";".qdqc";"Net currency exposure (see positions.q's ccy_exposure_in, already revalued into one reporting currency - a limit only means anything once every currency's exposure is expressed the same way) vs. a per-currency limit, stated in that same reporting currency - check_limit specialized to ccy_exposure_in's own output shape.";".qdqc.check_ccy_exposure_limits";".qdqc.check_ccy_exposure_limits[.qpos.ccy_exposure_in[pos;quotes;`USD;.z.p];([] ccy:enlist `EUR; limit:enlist 5000000f)]");
.man.registerArg (".qdqc.check_ccy_exposure_limits";"param";"exposure";"ccy_exposure_in's output - a table with at least");
.man.registerArg (".qdqc.check_ccy_exposure_limits";"param";"limits";"a table `ccy`limit - the max net exposure (in the same");
.man.registerArg (".qdqc.check_ccy_exposure_limits";"return";"";"see check_limit - columns ccy/metric/limit/status");
.man.registerArg (".qdqc.check_ccy_exposure_limits";"eg";"";".qdqc.check_ccy_exposure_limits[.qpos.ccy_exposure_in[pos;quotes;`USD;.z.p];([] ccy:enlist `EUR; limit:enlist 5000000f)]");
.man.registerFunc (".qdqc.check_reject_ratio_limits";".qdqc";"Reject ratios (see execution.q's reject_ratio_by, already windowed and grouped - a limit only means anything against a stated window) vs. a per-entity limit - check_limit specialized to reject_ratio_by's own output shape, so a reject-rate breach lands in the same summarize_checks report as position, exposure and market-data breaches rather than being eyeballed out of raw ratios. key_col is whatever reject_ratio_by was grouped by - `sym for a per-pair limit, or a counterparty column if that is how the desk sets them. The limit is a ratio in [0,1], matching reject_ratio's own units, not a percentage.";".qdqc.check_reject_ratio_limits";".qdqc.check_reject_ratio_limits[rr;([] sym:enlist `EURUSD; limit:enlist 0.05);`sym]");
.man.registerArg (".qdqc.check_reject_ratio_limits";"param";"reject_ratios";"reject_ratio_by's output - a table with at least");
.man.registerArg (".qdqc.check_reject_ratio_limits";"param";"limits";"a table `key_col`limit - the max tolerated reject ratio per");
.man.registerArg (".qdqc.check_reject_ratio_limits";"param";"key_col";"the column naming the entity, e.g. `sym");
.man.registerArg (".qdqc.check_reject_ratio_limits";"return";"";"see check_limit - columns key_col/metric/limit/status");
.man.registerArg (".qdqc.check_reject_ratio_limits";"throws";"";"error if reject_ratios is missing key_col or reject_ratio");
.man.registerArg (".qdqc.check_reject_ratio_limits";"eg";"";".qdqc.check_reject_ratio_limits[rr;([] sym:enlist `EURUSD; limit:enlist 0.05);`sym]");
.man.registerFunc (".qdqc.check_market_data_quality";".qdqc";"Per-row market data sanity, built on microstructure.q's own spread_bps (no re-derivation of top-of-book/mid here) - a negative spread means the book is crossed (bid at or through the ask, a genuine data error, not just a quality concern), a spread wider than max_spread_bps is flagged separately (thin/stale liquidity or a bad print, not necessarily wrong, but worth a human's attention) so the two very different severities don't collapse into one generic \"bad\" bucket.";".qdqc.check_market_data_quality";".qdqc.check_market_data_quality[quotes;5]");
.man.registerArg (".qdqc.check_market_data_quality";"param";"quotes";"table `ts`sym`bid_prices`bid_sizes`ask_prices`ask_sizes");
.man.registerArg (".qdqc.check_market_data_quality";"param";"max_spread_bps";"a spread at or below this many bps is `ok; above");
.man.registerArg (".qdqc.check_market_data_quality";"return";"";"a table ts/sym/spread_bps/status (`ok`, `crossed`, or `wide`),");
.man.registerArg (".qdqc.check_market_data_quality";"throws";"";"error if quotes is missing a required column");
.man.registerArg (".qdqc.check_market_data_quality";"eg";"";".qdqc.check_market_data_quality[quotes;5]");
.man.registerFunc (".qdqc.check_stale_quotes";".qdqc";"Per-sym staleness: how long ago was the last quote at or before at_time, for every sym currently present in quotes? Scoped to syms quotes actually has at least one row for - detecting a sym that should be quoting but never ticks at all needs live process/feed monitoring (see lib/torq/code/dqc/tableticking.q for that different, complementary concern), which this pure-function library has no way to observe.";".qdqc.check_stale_quotes";".qdqc.check_stale_quotes[quotes;.z.p;0D00:00:05]");
.man.registerArg (".qdqc.check_stale_quotes";"param";"quotes";"table `ts`sym`bid_prices`bid_sizes`ask_prices`ask_sizes,");
.man.registerArg (".qdqc.check_stale_quotes";"param";"at_time";"only consider quotes at or before this time");
.man.registerArg (".qdqc.check_stale_quotes";"param";"max_age";"a gap at or below this is `ok; above it is `stale");
.man.registerArg (".qdqc.check_stale_quotes";"return";"";"a table sym/last_ts/age/status (`ok` or `stale`), sorted");
.man.registerArg (".qdqc.check_stale_quotes";"throws";"";"error if quotes is missing a required column");
.man.registerArg (".qdqc.check_stale_quotes";"eg";"";".qdqc.check_stale_quotes[quotes;.z.p;0D00:00:05]");
.man.registerFunc (".qdqc.summarize_checks";".qdqc";"Flattens a list of already-run check tables (any of the above, or a caller's own) into one \"what needs attention\" report - every row whose status isn't `ok, human-readable, across however many different checks were run. Deliberately doesn't try to normalize the different checks' columns into one shared shape (a position-limit breach's notional isn't comparable to a market-data check's spread_bps) - detail is a rendered string of the whole offending row instead, so nothing about why it triggered is lost.";".qdqc.summarize_checks";".qdqc.summarize_checks[((`position_limits;.qdqc.check_position_notional_limits[pos;limits]);(`spread;.qdqc.check_market_data_quality[quotes;5]))]");
.man.registerArg (".qdqc.summarize_checks";"param";"named_checks";"a list of (check_name;check_table) pairs - each");
.man.registerArg (".qdqc.summarize_checks";"return";"";"a table check/status/detail, one row per non-`ok row across");
.man.registerArg (".qdqc.summarize_checks";"eg";"";".qdqc.summarize_checks[((`position_limits;.qdqc.check_position_notional_limits[pos;limits]);(`spread;.qdqc.check_market_data_quality[quotes;5]))]");

.man.registerFile ("microstructure.q";"";".qmicro";"microstructure.q - order-book / liquidity microstructure signals: what the book looks like (imbalance, microprice, depth, slope, convexity) and how it's moving (order flow imbalance, velocity/acceleration, queue depletion) - built on top of execution.q's sweep_price/vwap and forwards.q's require_quotes_cols/quotes table shape. See docs/ROADMAP.md and docs/prompts/microstructure-features.md for the design this module implements. Unlike execution.q/forwards.q, Tier 1 functions here take a whole COLUMN from a quotes table - i.e. bid_prices/bid_sizes/ask_prices/ask_sizes are each a vector of vectors, one level-0-first level-vector per row/ snapshot - and return a vector aligned to those rows, the same generalization markout_at_horizons/cross_book_at_sizes already make elsewhere in this library. A single snapshot still works: wrap its 4 level vectors in `enlist` and read index 0 of the result.");
.man.registerFunc (".qmicro.level_at";".qmicro";"Private: the level-th element of each row's own vector in levels (a vector of vectors). Nulls out (0n) a row whose vector is shorter than level+1 - \"not enough depth at this row\" - rather than throwing an index error; distinct from a level that exists but is genuinely 0. Every extracted value is cast to float (\"f\"$), including real ones, so the returned vector is always uniformly typed even when levels holds long (integer) prices/sizes - a float null 0n mixed element-by-element into an otherwise-long vector produces a mixed-type general list under real kdb+/KDB-X (PeachQ tolerates it, real kdb+ then throws a 'type error on later arithmetic against it), so every element is normalized to float here, at the source, rather than only the null ones.";".qmicro.level_at";"");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.level_at";"param";"levels";"a vector of vectors, one level-0-first vector per row");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.level_at";"param";"level";"the level index to extract from every row");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.level_at";"return";"";"a vector, one value per row");
.man.registerFunc (".qmicro.sum_levels";".qmicro";"Private: sum of level_at[sizes;l] for l in til n_levels, per row.";".qmicro.sum_levels";"");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.sum_levels";"param";"sizes";"a vector of vectors, one level-0-first vector per row");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.sum_levels";"param";"n_levels";"how many levels (0..n_levels-1) to sum");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.sum_levels";"return";"";"a vector, one value per row");
.man.registerFunc (".qmicro.mid_price";".qmicro";"L0 mid price, per row - the natural companion to forwards.q's cross_ref_price_at \"mid\" concept, for a plain quotes column rather than a swept size.";".qmicro.mid_price";".qmicro.mid_price[enlist 1.1000 1.0998;enlist 1.1002 1.1004]  -> ,1.1001");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.mid_price";"param";"bid_prices";"a vector of vectors, one level-0-first vector per row");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.mid_price";"param";"ask_prices";"a vector of vectors, one level-0-first vector per row");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.mid_price";"return";"";"a vector, one mid price per row");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.mid_price";"eg";"";".qmicro.mid_price[enlist 1.1000 1.0998;enlist 1.1002 1.1004]  -> ,1.1001");
.man.registerFunc (".qmicro.book_pressure_at_level";".qmicro";"Book pressure at one level: (Vbid-Vask)/(Vbid+Vask), in [-1,1]. Exactly 0 when both sizes are 0 at a level that DOES exist for that row - distinct from level_at's null for a level that doesn't exist at all.";".qmicro.book_pressure_at_level";".qmicro.book_pressure_at_level[enlist 100 50;enlist 40 60;0]  -> ,0.4285714");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.book_pressure_at_level";"param";"bid_sizes";"a vector of vectors, one level-0-first vector per row");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.book_pressure_at_level";"param";"ask_sizes";"a vector of vectors, one level-0-first vector per row");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.book_pressure_at_level";"param";"level";"the level index (0 = top of book)");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.book_pressure_at_level";"return";"";"a vector, one pressure value per row");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.book_pressure_at_level";"eg";"";".qmicro.book_pressure_at_level[enlist 100 50;enlist 40 60;0]  -> ,0.4285714");
.man.registerFunc (".qmicro.order_book_imbalance";".qmicro";"Multi-level order book imbalance: total bid and total ask size are aggregated across levels 0..n_levels-1 FIRST, THEN one ratio is taken - (sumBid-sumAsk)/(sumBid+sumAsk) - NOT a sum of n_levels separate per-level book_pressure_at_level ratios (that would not be bounded in [-1,1] and contradicts the cited literature's own definition of OBI).";".qmicro.order_book_imbalance";".qmicro.order_book_imbalance[enlist 100 50;enlist 40 60;2]  -> ,0.2");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.order_book_imbalance";"param";"bid_sizes";"a vector of vectors, one level-0-first vector per row");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.order_book_imbalance";"param";"ask_sizes";"a vector of vectors, one level-0-first vector per row");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.order_book_imbalance";"param";"n_levels";"how many levels (0..n_levels-1) to aggregate over");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.order_book_imbalance";"return";"";"a vector, one imbalance value per row, in [-1,1]");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.order_book_imbalance";"eg";"";".qmicro.order_book_imbalance[enlist 100 50;enlist 40 60;2]  -> ,0.2");
.man.registerFunc (".qmicro.microprice";".qmicro";"Size-weighted L0 microprice: (Vask0*Pbid0+Vbid0*Pask0)/(Vbid0+Vask0), falling back to mid_price when both L0 sizes are 0.";".qmicro.microprice";".qmicro.microprice[enlist enlist 1.1000;enlist enlist 100;enlist enlist 1.1002;enlist enlist 300]  -> ,1.10005");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.microprice";"param";"bid_prices";"a vector of vectors, one level-0-first vector per row");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.microprice";"param";"bid_sizes";"a vector of vectors, one level-0-first vector per row");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.microprice";"param";"ask_prices";"a vector of vectors, one level-0-first vector per row");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.microprice";"param";"ask_sizes";"a vector of vectors, one level-0-first vector per row");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.microprice";"return";"";"a vector, one microprice per row");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.microprice";"eg";"";".qmicro.microprice[enlist enlist 1.1000;enlist enlist 100;enlist enlist 1.1002;enlist enlist 300]  -> ,1.10005");
.man.registerFunc (".qmicro.microprice_divergence";".qmicro";"microprice minus mid_price - how far size-weighting pulls the \"true\" price away from the naive top-of-book mid.";".qmicro.microprice_divergence";".qmicro.microprice_divergence[enlist enlist 1.1000;enlist enlist 100;enlist enlist 1.1002;enlist enlist 300]  -> ,-5e-05");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.microprice_divergence";"param";"bid_prices";"a vector of vectors, one level-0-first vector per row");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.microprice_divergence";"param";"bid_sizes";"a vector of vectors, one level-0-first vector per row");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.microprice_divergence";"param";"ask_prices";"a vector of vectors, one level-0-first vector per row");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.microprice_divergence";"param";"ask_sizes";"a vector of vectors, one level-0-first vector per row");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.microprice_divergence";"return";"";"a vector, microprice - mid_price per row");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.microprice_divergence";"eg";"";".qmicro.microprice_divergence[enlist enlist 1.1000;enlist enlist 100;enlist enlist 1.1002;enlist enlist 300]  -> ,-5e-05");
.man.registerFunc (".qmicro.spread_bps";".qmicro";"Quoted spread in basis points: 10000*(Pask0-Pbid0)/mid. This is always /10000 by definition of \"basis points\" - unlike eff_spread/slippage/ markout elsewhere in this library, it is NOT scaled by a caller-supplied pip_factor (a JPY cross's spread_bps is still bps, not pips).";".qmicro.spread_bps";".qmicro.spread_bps[enlist enlist 1.1000;enlist enlist 1.1002]  -> ,1.818017");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.spread_bps";"param";"bid_prices";"a vector of vectors, one level-0-first vector per row");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.spread_bps";"param";"ask_prices";"a vector of vectors, one level-0-first vector per row");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.spread_bps";"return";"";"a vector, spread in bps per row");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.spread_bps";"eg";"";".qmicro.spread_bps[enlist enlist 1.1000;enlist enlist 1.1002]  -> ,1.818017");
.man.registerFunc (".qmicro.depth_ratio";".qmicro";"Fraction of combined top-of-book size relative to the next 4 levels of depth: (Vbid0+Vask0) / sum(Vbid_i+Vask_i for i in 1..4). High means a fragile top of book (most size sits right at the touch); needs 5 levels present per row - a shallower row nulls out naturally (division by a null/zero deeper-level sum) rather than throwing.";".qmicro.depth_ratio";".qmicro.depth_ratio[enlist 100 20 20 20 20;enlist 100 20 20 20 20]  -> ,1.25");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.depth_ratio";"param";"bid_sizes";"a vector of vectors, one level-0-first vector per row");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.depth_ratio";"param";"ask_sizes";"a vector of vectors, one level-0-first vector per row");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.depth_ratio";"return";"";"a vector, depth ratio per row");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.depth_ratio";"eg";"";".qmicro.depth_ratio[enlist 100 20 20 20 20;enlist 100 20 20 20 20]  -> ,1.25");
.man.registerFunc (".qmicro.vwmp_skew_one";".qmicro";"Private: vwmp_skew for a single row - volume-weighted mid over the first n_levels (via execution.q's vwap, concatenating that row's bid and ask prices/sizes over til n_levels) minus the simple L0 mid, divided by the L0 spread.";".qmicro.vwmp_skew_one";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qmicro.vwmp_skew";".qmicro";"Volume-weighted mid (over n_levels, via execution.q's vwap) minus the simple L0 mid, divided by the L0 spread - how far deeper-book weighting skews the mid relative to the quoted spread.";".qmicro.vwmp_skew";".qmicro.vwmp_skew[enlist 1.1000 1.0998;enlist 100 100;enlist 1.1002 1.1004;enlist 100 100;2]  -> ,0f");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.vwmp_skew";"param";"bid_prices";"a vector of vectors, one level-0-first vector per row");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.vwmp_skew";"param";"bid_sizes";"a vector of vectors, one level-0-first vector per row");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.vwmp_skew";"param";"ask_prices";"a vector of vectors, one level-0-first vector per row");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.vwmp_skew";"param";"ask_sizes";"a vector of vectors, one level-0-first vector per row");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.vwmp_skew";"param";"n_levels";"how many levels (0..n_levels-1) to volume-weight over");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.vwmp_skew";"return";"";"a vector, skew per row");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.vwmp_skew";"eg";"";".qmicro.vwmp_skew[enlist 1.1000 1.0998;enlist 100 100;enlist 1.1002 1.1004;enlist 100 100;2]  -> ,0f");
.man.registerFunc (".qmicro.book_slope_one";".qmicro";"Private: book_slope for a single row - (P0-P_last)/sum(sizes).";".qmicro.book_slope_one";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qmicro.book_slope";".qmicro";"Book slope: (P0-P_last)/sum(sizes), called once per side - pass bid_prices/bid_sizes for the bid-side slope, ask_prices/ask_sizes for the ask-side slope. Argument shape deliberately mirrors execution.q's sweep_price (prices;sizes) - there is no target_size here to validate, so sweep_price's own validation isn't reused, just its argument order.";".qmicro.book_slope";".qmicro.book_slope[enlist 1.1000 1.0998 1.0996;enlist 100 100 100]  -> ,1.333333e-06");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.book_slope";"param";"prices";"a vector of vectors, one level-0-first vector per row");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.book_slope";"param";"sizes";"a vector of vectors, one level-0-first vector per row");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.book_slope";"return";"";"a vector, slope per row");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.book_slope";"eg";"";".qmicro.book_slope[enlist 1.1000 1.0998 1.0996;enlist 100 100 100]  -> ,1.333333e-06");
.man.registerFunc (".qmicro.book_convexity_one";".qmicro";"Private: book_convexity for a single row - (P0-P1)-(P1-P2), negated for side=`ask. Nulls out a row with fewer than 3 levels rather than indexing out of bounds.";".qmicro.book_convexity_one";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qmicro.book_convexity";".qmicro";"Book convexity: (P0-P1)-(P1-P2), measuring curvature of the price ladder away from the touch. Negated when side=`ask - bid prices decrease level-by-level while ask prices increase, so mirroring the ask-side sign keeps convexity comparably signed across both sides (a \"cheaper to go deeper than the first step implies\" book reads the same sign whichever side it's on). Needs >=3 levels; a shorter row is nulled, not an error.";".qmicro.book_convexity";".qmicro.book_convexity[enlist 1.1000 1.0998 1.0995;`bid]  -> ,-0.0001");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.book_convexity";"param";"prices";"a vector of vectors, one level-0-first vector per row");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.book_convexity";"param";"side";"`bid or `ask - which side prices belongs to");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.book_convexity";"return";"";"a vector, convexity per row");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.book_convexity";"eg";"";".qmicro.book_convexity[enlist 1.1000 1.0998 1.0995;`bid]  -> ,-0.0001");
.man.registerFunc (".qmicro.vamp_one";".qmicro";"Private: vamp for a single row - convert notional into a size via each side's own L0 price, sweep each side at that size (execution.q's sweep_price), and average the two avg_price results.";".qmicro.vamp_one";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qmicro.vamp";".qmicro";"VAMP (volume-adjusted mid price): convert notional into a size via each side's own L0 price, then sweep each side to that size with execution.q's sweep_price directly and average the two resulting avg_price legs - not a fresh notional-walking algorithm, exactly sweep_price reused twice.";".qmicro.vamp";".qmicro.vamp[enlist 1.1000 1.0998;enlist 1000000 1000000;enlist 1.1002 1.1004;enlist 1000000 1000000;500000]  -> ,1.1001");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.vamp";"param";"bid_prices";"a vector of vectors, one level-0-first vector per row");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.vamp";"param";"bid_sizes";"a vector of vectors, one level-0-first vector per row");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.vamp";"param";"ask_prices";"a vector of vectors, one level-0-first vector per row");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.vamp";"param";"ask_sizes";"a vector of vectors, one level-0-first vector per row");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.vamp";"param";"notional";"a single atom (broadcast to every row) or a vector");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.vamp";"return";"";"a vector, VAMP per row");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.vamp";"eg";"";".qmicro.vamp[enlist 1.1000 1.0998;enlist 1000000 1000000;enlist 1.1002 1.1004;enlist 1000000 1000000;500000]  -> ,1.1001");
.man.registerFunc (".qmicro.quotes_for_sym";".qmicro";"Private: rows of quotes for one sym, sorted `ts xasc, validated to have every column require_quotes_cols checks - the shared setup every Tier 2 rolling function needs. Named target_sym (not sym) to avoid colliding with the `sym` column inside the qSQL where-clause below (a param named the same as the column it's compared against would make the comparison compare the column to itself, always true) - forwards.q's leg_book_as_of hit this exact bug already and named around it the same way.";".qmicro.quotes_for_sym";"");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.quotes_for_sym";"throws";"";"error if quotes is missing a required column (see require_quotes_cols)");
.man.registerFunc (".qmicro.mid_price_velocity";".qmicro";"First difference of the L0 mid price for one sym's quotes, time-ordered. Index 0 is forced to 0n (no prior snapshot to diff against) - `deltas` keeps a vector's first element as-is rather than nulling it (unlike `prev`), so it's overridden explicitly here.";".qmicro.mid_price_velocity";".qmicro.mid_price_velocity[quotes;`EURUSD]");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.mid_price_velocity";"param";"quotes";"table `ts`sym`bid_prices`bid_sizes`ask_prices`ask_sizes");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.mid_price_velocity";"param";"target_sym";"the sym to compute velocity for");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.mid_price_velocity";"return";"";"a vector, one velocity value per quote row for target_sym, in ts order");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.mid_price_velocity";"throws";"";"error if quotes is missing a required column");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.mid_price_velocity";"eg";"";".qmicro.mid_price_velocity[quotes;`EURUSD]");
.man.registerFunc (".qmicro.mid_price_acceleration";".qmicro";"Second difference of the L0 mid price for one sym's quotes. Index 0 is already null from mid_price_velocity's own override (kept as-is by `deltas`); index 1 comes out null \"for free\" too, since deltas' real_value-0n arithmetic already propagates null - no manual override needed for either index here.";".qmicro.mid_price_acceleration";".qmicro.mid_price_acceleration[quotes;`EURUSD]");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.mid_price_acceleration";"param";"quotes";"table `ts`sym`bid_prices`bid_sizes`ask_prices`ask_sizes");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.mid_price_acceleration";"param";"target_sym";"the sym to compute acceleration for");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.mid_price_acceleration";"return";"";"a vector, one acceleration value per quote row for target_sym, in ts order");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.mid_price_acceleration";"throws";"";"error if quotes is missing a required column");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.mid_price_acceleration";"eg";"";".qmicro.mid_price_acceleration[quotes;`EURUSD]");
.man.registerFunc (".qmicro.queue_depletion_rate";".qmicro";"Queue depletion rate at L0 for one side: max(V_prev-V_cur,0)/V_prev - how much of the prior top-of-book size drained away, floored at 0 (a size increase is not \"negative depletion\"). Index 0 is forced to 0n (no prior snapshot).";".qmicro.queue_depletion_rate";".qmicro.queue_depletion_rate[quotes;`EURUSD;`bid]");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.queue_depletion_rate";"param";"quotes";"table `ts`sym`bid_prices`bid_sizes`ask_prices`ask_sizes");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.queue_depletion_rate";"param";"target_sym";"the sym to compute depletion for");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.queue_depletion_rate";"param";"side";"`bid or `ask");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.queue_depletion_rate";"return";"";"a vector, one depletion rate per quote row for target_sym, in ts order");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.queue_depletion_rate";"throws";"";"error if quotes is missing a required column, or side isn't `bid or `ask");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.queue_depletion_rate";"eg";"";".qmicro.queue_depletion_rate[quotes;`EURUSD;`bid]");
.man.registerFunc (".qmicro.ofi";".qmicro";"L0 order flow imbalance (Cont-Kukanov-Stoikov): per side, if the price improved vs the prior row that side's whole new size counts as \"new\" flow; if the price is unchanged it's the size delta; if the price worsened it's negative the prior size (that queue is gone). ofi is e_bid-e_ask (bid improves by price going UP, ask improves by price going DOWN - mind the sign). Index 0 is forced to 0n explicitly: the price comparisons against a null previous price do not themselves come out null (a boolean comparison against 0n is just false, not null), so without this override index 0 would silently pick a branch instead of nulling.";".qmicro.ofi";".qmicro.ofi[quotes;`EURUSD]");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.ofi";"param";"quotes";"table `ts`sym`bid_prices`bid_sizes`ask_prices`ask_sizes");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.ofi";"param";"target_sym";"the sym to compute OFI for");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.ofi";"return";"";"a vector, one OFI value per quote row for target_sym, in ts order");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.ofi";"throws";"";"error if quotes is missing a required column");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.ofi";"eg";"";".qmicro.ofi[quotes;`EURUSD]");
.man.registerFunc (".qmicro.ofi_at_level";".qmicro";"Private: ofi's e_bid-e_ask contribution at one specific level, across every row of sub. A level absent on a row (level_at's null) contributes 0 size (`0^` before use) rather than nulling that level's whole contribution - a level disappearing between rows shows up as negative flow (the old size draining away: its null price loses every price comparison against the prior row's real price, falling into the \"price worsened\" branch, -prev_size), matching genuine order-flow semantics rather than silently dropping the level.";".qmicro.ofi_at_level";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qmicro.ofi_multilevel";".qmicro";"Multi-level order flow imbalance: ofi's e_bid/e_ask logic applied at every level 0..n_levels-1 and summed, handling levels that appear or disappear between rows (see ofi_at_level). Index 0 is forced to 0n explicitly, same reason as plain ofi.";".qmicro.ofi_multilevel";".qmicro.ofi_multilevel[quotes;`EURUSD;3]");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.ofi_multilevel";"param";"quotes";"table `ts`sym`bid_prices`bid_sizes`ask_prices`ask_sizes");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.ofi_multilevel";"param";"target_sym";"the sym to compute multi-level OFI for");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.ofi_multilevel";"param";"n_levels";"how many levels (0..n_levels-1) to sum OFI over");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.ofi_multilevel";"return";"";"a vector, one multi-level OFI value per quote row for target_sym, in ts order");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.ofi_multilevel";"throws";"";"error if quotes is missing a required column");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.ofi_multilevel";"eg";"";".qmicro.ofi_multilevel[quotes;`EURUSD;3]");
.man.registerFunc (".qmicro.rolling_ofi";".qmicro";"Rolling (moving-window) sum of an OFI series - a thin wrapper around kdb+'s builtin msum, not a hand-rolled moving sum.";".qmicro.rolling_ofi";".qmicro.rolling_ofi[1 -1 2 0 -3;2]  -> 1 0 1 2 -3");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.rolling_ofi";"param";"ofi_series";"an OFI vector, e.g. from ofi or ofi_multilevel");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.rolling_ofi";"param";"window";"the moving-window size, in number of rows");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.rolling_ofi";"return";"";"a vector, msum[window;ofi_series]");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.rolling_ofi";"eg";"";".qmicro.rolling_ofi[1 -1 2 0 -3;2]  -> 1 0 1 2 -3");
.man.registerFunc (".qmicro.spread_ratio";".qmicro";"Ratio of the current quoted spread (spread_bps) to its own rolling mean (kdb+'s builtin mavg, not hand-rolled) over window - >1 means the spread is currently wider than its recent average.";".qmicro.spread_ratio";".qmicro.spread_ratio[quotes;`EURUSD;5]");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.spread_ratio";"param";"quotes";"table `ts`sym`bid_prices`bid_sizes`ask_prices`ask_sizes");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.spread_ratio";"param";"target_sym";"the sym to compute spread_ratio for");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.spread_ratio";"param";"window";"the moving-window size, in number of rows");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.spread_ratio";"return";"";"a vector, one spread_ratio value per quote row for target_sym, in ts order");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.spread_ratio";"throws";"";"error if quotes is missing a required column");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.spread_ratio";"eg";"";".qmicro.spread_ratio[quotes;`EURUSD;5]");
.man.registerFunc (".qmicro.inter_event_time";".qmicro";"Log-scaled inter-quote gap, in seconds: log(1+gap), where gap is the time since the sym's previous quote. Index 0 is null \"for free\": `prev` nulls the first element of a timestamp vector, and timestamp-minus-null- timestamp is already a null timespan - no manual override needed.";".qmicro.inter_event_time";".qmicro.inter_event_time[quotes;`EURUSD]");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.inter_event_time";"param";"quotes";"table `ts`sym`bid_prices`bid_sizes`ask_prices`ask_sizes");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.inter_event_time";"param";"target_sym";"the sym to compute inter-event time for");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.inter_event_time";"return";"";"a vector, one log(1+gap_seconds) value per quote row for target_sym, in ts order");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.inter_event_time";"throws";"";"error if quotes is missing a required column");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.inter_event_time";"eg";"";".qmicro.inter_event_time[quotes;`EURUSD]");
.man.registerFunc (".qmicro.ofi_autocorrelation";".qmicro";"Rolling lag-1 autocorrelation of an OFI series: for each window-sized trailing slice, correlates the slice's first window-1 values against its last window-1 values (a lag-1 pairing) with kdb+'s builtin cor, and writes the result at the index the window ends on. No builtin exists for a rolling correlation, unlike rolling_ofi/spread_ratio, so this is a genuine loop; every index before the first full window is left null (0n) - not enough history yet.";".qmicro.ofi_autocorrelation";".qmicro.ofi_autocorrelation[1 -1 2 0 -3 4;3]");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.ofi_autocorrelation";"param";"ofi_series";"an OFI vector, e.g. from ofi or ofi_multilevel");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.ofi_autocorrelation";"param";"window";"the moving-window size, in number of rows (needs >=2)");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.ofi_autocorrelation";"return";"";"a vector, same length as ofi_series, lag-1 autocorrelation per window ending at that index");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.ofi_autocorrelation";"eg";"";".qmicro.ofi_autocorrelation[1 -1 2 0 -3 4;3]");
.man.registerFunc (".qmicro.tape_actions";".qmicro";"The actions an event tape may carry.";".qmicro.tape_actions";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qmicro.tape_cols";".qmicro";"Required columns for any tape function. A subset of the full shape: these are what the counting and flow features read, and demanding the rest (price, order_id, pip_factor) would refuse a legitimately projected tape.";".qmicro.tape_cols";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qmicro.require_tape";".qmicro";"Refuse a tape that is missing a column, carries an unknown action, or is not sorted by time. ALL THREE are silent failures if unchecked, which is why this is a precondition rather than defensive normalisation: - a missing column reads as a null in most q code, so a windowed count over it returns 0 rather than erroring - an unknown action (a typo'd `trades`, say) is simply never matched by `action=`trade`, so every trade-based ratio silently reports on an empty set - an UNSORTED tape makes every rolling window compute over the wrong rows and return a plausible number. Rejecting beats sorting here for the same reason cross_book_at rejects unsorted quotes: the tape is sorted by construction, so sorting on every call pays O(n log n) to hide a caller's bug.";".qmicro.require_tape";".qmicro.require_tape[tape]");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.require_tape";"param";"tape";"an event tape table");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.require_tape";"return";"";"1b when the tape is usable");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.require_tape";"throws";"";"error naming what is wrong");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.require_tape";"eg";"";".qmicro.require_tape[tape]");
.man.registerFunc (".qmicro.signed_trade_flow";".qmicro";"Signed trade flow: the net direction of aggressive volume (ROADMAP #19). Only `trade` events count - an add or a cancel moves no volume. `side` on a trade is the AGGRESSOR's side (1 buy, -1 sell), which is what makes the result mean \"buying pressure\": summing the resting side instead would be the same number negated, and summing \"one party was a buyer\" would be the trade count. `size` is unsigned in the tape and signed here by multiplying, per the shape contract - a signed size column would make `sum size` meaningless.";".qmicro.signed_trade_flow";".qmicro.signed_trade_flow[tape]  ->  -1000000f");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.signed_trade_flow";"param";"tape";"an event tape table");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.signed_trade_flow";"return";"";"the net signed volume: positive when buyers were the aggressors");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.signed_trade_flow";"eg";"";".qmicro.signed_trade_flow[tape]  ->  -1000000f");
.man.registerFunc (".qmicro.cumulative_trade_flow";".qmicro";"Cumulative signed trade flow, one running total per trade event. The per-event series vpin and the flow-toxicity family build on, rather than the single scalar above. Returned aligned to the TRADE events only, with their times, because a cumulative delta indexed against add and cancel rows would repeat values at every non-trade row and invite a reader to treat those repeats as observations.";".qmicro.cumulative_trade_flow";"");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.cumulative_trade_flow";"return";"";"a table of time and cum_flow, one row per trade event");
.man.registerFunc (".qmicro.cancel_to_trade_ratio";".qmicro";"Cancel-to-trade ratio: cancels per trade (ROADMAP #23). A standard flow-toxicity proxy - a venue where orders are posted and pulled without trading has a high ratio, and a sharp rise in it is a classic quote-stuffing signature. Returns 0n when there are no trades, NOT infinity and not zero. Zero would read as \"no cancelling happening\", which is the opposite of the truth when the real situation is cancels with nothing trading; and 0w propagates into any average a caller takes. A null says \"undefined here\", which is what a ratio with an empty denominator is.";".qmicro.cancel_to_trade_ratio";".qmicro.cancel_to_trade_ratio[tape]  ->  1.5");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.cancel_to_trade_ratio";"param";"tape";"an event tape table");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.cancel_to_trade_ratio";"return";"";"cancels divided by trades, or 0n when no trade occurred");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.cancel_to_trade_ratio";"eg";"";".qmicro.cancel_to_trade_ratio[tape]  ->  1.5");
.man.registerFunc (".qmicro.cancel_to_trade_ratio_by";".qmicro";"Cancel-to-trade ratio per time bucket and grouping. Mirrors hit_ratio_by's windowed-groupby shape, which is what the ROADMAP asks these features to look like: xbar the time into buckets, group by those plus whatever columns the caller names, and report the ratio per group. A null bucket_size disables time-bucketing entirely. Buckets with no trade get 0n, for the reason cancel_to_trade_ratio does - and that matters more here, because a quiet bucket is common and a 0 in one would drag any average over buckets toward zero.";".qmicro.cancel_to_trade_ratio_by";".qmicro.cancel_to_trade_ratio_by[tape;0D01:00:00;enlist `sym]");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.cancel_to_trade_ratio_by";"param";"tape";"an event tape table");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.cancel_to_trade_ratio_by";"param";"bucket_size";"a timespan to floor time into, or 0Nn for no bucketing");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.cancel_to_trade_ratio_by";"param";"group_cols";"extra columns to group by, e.g. enlist `sym");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.cancel_to_trade_ratio_by";"eg";"";".qmicro.cancel_to_trade_ratio_by[tape;0D01:00:00;enlist `sym]");
.man.registerFunc (".qmicro.volume_buckets";".qmicro";"Split the trades in a tape into equal-VOLUME buckets (ROADMAP #25). The primitive vpin is built on, exposed separately because it is the interesting intermediate: a reader who distrusts a VPIN number wants to see the buckets it came from, and it is far easier to test on its own. WHY VOLUME BUCKETS AND NOT TIME BUCKETS This is the \"volume-synchronized\" in VPIN. Informed trading shows up as an imbalance per unit VOLUME, not per unit time: an hour with four trades and an hour with four thousand are not comparable observations, but two buckets of 10,000 lots each are. Time-bucketing a flow-toxicity metric makes it a measure of how busy the market was. A TRADE THAT STRADDLES A BOUNDARY IS SPLIT Its volume goes partly in one bucket and partly in the next, proportionally. That is not a nicety: VPIN divides by (n * bucket_volume), so every bucket must hold exactly bucket_volume or the denominator is wrong. Assigning whole trades to whichever bucket they end in would leave buckets of unequal volume and a VPIN that is systematically off by however lumpy the tape is. THE LAST, INCOMPLETE BUCKET IS DISCARDED A bucket holding less than bucket_volume has a smaller denominator, so its imbalance is not comparable with the others - and including it makes VPIN jump around as the final partial bucket fills. Only complete buckets are returned, which means a tape with less volume than one bucket returns none.";".qmicro.volume_buckets";".qmicro.volume_buckets[tape;1000000f]");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.volume_buckets";"param";"tape";"an event tape table");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.volume_buckets";"param";"bucket_volume";"the volume each bucket holds");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.volume_buckets";"return";"";"a table of bucket, end_time, buy_volume, sell_volume, imbalance");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.volume_buckets";"throws";"";"error when bucket_volume is not positive");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.volume_buckets";"eg";"";".qmicro.volume_buckets[tape;1000000f]");
.man.registerFunc (".qmicro.vpin";".qmicro";"VPIN - volume-synchronized probability of informed trading (ROADMAP #25). Easley, Lopez de Prado and O'Hara (2012). Over a trailing window of n_buckets equal-volume buckets: VPIN = sum |buy_volume - sell_volume| / (n_buckets * bucket_volume) which is the mean absolute order imbalance per bucket, normalised to a 0..1 fraction of bucket volume. 0 means every bucket was perfectly balanced; 1 means every bucket was entirely one-sided. ONE SIMPLIFICATION THIS TAPE PERMITS, AND IT IS WORTH KNOWING The paper classifies volume with BULK VOLUME CLASSIFICATION - a normal CDF over price changes - because most tapes do not say who was the aggressor, so buy and sell volume has to be INFERRED. This tape carries the aggressor side (docs/architecture/event-tape.md), so the classification is exact and BVC is not needed. That makes these numbers cleaner than a BVC-based VPIN, not comparable-but-different: same definition, better inputs. If a future source cannot supply the aggressor side, BVC is the thing to add, and it belongs here rather than in the tape - the tape should not invent a side it does not know. Returns one value per bucket, NULL until n_buckets have accumulated, which is ofi_autocorrelation's convention in this file: a window that has not filled yet has no answer, and 0 would be a wrong one.";".qmicro.vpin";".qmicro.vpin[tape;1000000f;50]");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.vpin";"param";"tape";"an event tape table");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.vpin";"param";"bucket_volume";"the volume each bucket holds");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.vpin";"param";"n_buckets";"how many trailing buckets each value averages over");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.vpin";"return";"";"a table of bucket, end_time and vpin");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.vpin";"throws";"";"error when n_buckets is not positive");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.vpin";"eg";"";".qmicro.vpin[tape;1000000f;50]");
.man.registerFunc (".qmicro.trade_arrival_rate";".qmicro";"Trade arrival rate: trades per second over the tape's span (ROADMAP #26). Only `trade` events count - an add or a cancel is not an arrival in the sense this measures, which is how fast executions are happening. Returns 0n when the span is zero, NOT infinity: one trade, or several at the same instant, gives no information about a rate. 0w would propagate into any average a caller takes, and 0 would read as \"no trading\", the opposite of the truth.";".qmicro.trade_arrival_rate";".qmicro.trade_arrival_rate[tape]  ->  0.2");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.trade_arrival_rate";"param";"tape";"an event tape table");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.trade_arrival_rate";"return";"";"trades per second, or 0n when the span is zero");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.trade_arrival_rate";"eg";"";".qmicro.trade_arrival_rate[tape]  ->  0.2");
.man.registerFunc (".qmicro.trade_arrival_rate_by";".qmicro";"Trade arrival rate per time bucket and grouping. Mirrors cancel_to_trade_ratio_by and hit_ratio_by. Here the rate is trades per bucket rather than per second, because the bucket IS the unit the caller chose - dividing again by the bucket's length would throw away the thing that makes buckets comparable.";".qmicro.trade_arrival_rate_by";".qmicro.trade_arrival_rate_by[tape;0D01:00:00;enlist `sym]");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.trade_arrival_rate_by";"param";"tape";"an event tape table");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.trade_arrival_rate_by";"param";"bucket_size";"a timespan to floor time into, or 0Nn for no bucketing");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.trade_arrival_rate_by";"param";"group_cols";"extra columns to group by, e.g. enlist `sym");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.trade_arrival_rate_by";"eg";"";".qmicro.trade_arrival_rate_by[tape;0D01:00:00;enlist `sym]");
.man.registerFunc (".qmicro.large_trade_threshold";".qmicro";"The size at or above which a trade counts as \"large\", taken from the tape's OWN distribution at the given quantile. The ROADMAP parked large_trade_ratio (#27) on the grounds that \"large\" is relative to a venue's typical clip and picking a number here would be inventing a market convention. That is right about the number and wrong about the blocker: the threshold does not have to be a constant. Asking for a QUANTILE makes it derived rather than invented, and \"relative to the venue's typical clip\" is exactly what a quantile of that venue's own trades computes. Quantile rather than a multiple of the mean, because trade sizes are heavy-tailed: a handful of very large clips drag a mean upward until \"twice the mean\" excludes trades that every desk would call large. A quantile is unmoved by how extreme the extremes are. Nearest-rank, and the rank is taken on the SORTED sizes with `ceiling` - so q=1.0 is the largest trade and q just above 0 is the smallest, with no interpolation inventing a size that never traded.";".qmicro.large_trade_threshold";".qmicro.large_trade_threshold[tape;0.9]");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.large_trade_threshold";"param";"tape";"an event tape table");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.large_trade_threshold";"param";"q";"the quantile in (0;1], e.g. 0.9 for the top decile");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.large_trade_threshold";"return";"";"the size at that quantile, or 0n when the tape holds no trades");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.large_trade_threshold";"throws";"";"error when the tape is malformed, or q is outside (0;1]");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.large_trade_threshold";"eg";"";".qmicro.large_trade_threshold[tape;0.9]");
.man.registerFunc (".qmicro.large_trade_ratio";".qmicro";"Share of trades at or above the quantile threshold, by COUNT. Note what this is not: it is not the share of VOLUME those trades carry. The count share is pinned near 1-q by construction, so the count form mostly restates the quantile it was given. The volume form below is the one that says something, and both are here so the distinction is explicit rather than left to a caller who assumed one and got the other. Near 1-q, not equal to it: the comparison is >=, and the nearest-rank threshold is itself a size that traded, so that trade counts as large. On ten trades of sizes 1..10 at q=0.9 the threshold is 9 and the ratio is 0.2, not 0.1. Using > instead would exclude a trade of exactly the ninetieth-percentile size, which is the wrong answer to \"how much trades at or above this size\".";".qmicro.large_trade_ratio";".qmicro.large_trade_ratio[tape;0.9]");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.large_trade_ratio";"param";"tape";"an event tape table");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.large_trade_ratio";"param";"q";"the quantile in (0;1]");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.large_trade_ratio";"return";"";"the fraction of trades at or above the threshold, 0n with no trades");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.large_trade_ratio";"eg";"";".qmicro.large_trade_ratio[tape;0.9]");
.man.registerFunc (".qmicro.large_trade_volume_share";".qmicro";"Share of traded VOLUME carried by trades at or above the threshold. This is the one that carries information. If the top decile of trades by count moves half the volume, that is a market where a few clips dominate - and a desk reads that very differently from one where volume is spread evenly, even though the COUNT ratio is 0.1 in both.";".qmicro.large_trade_volume_share";".qmicro.large_trade_volume_share[tape;0.9]");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.large_trade_volume_share";"param";"tape";"an event tape table");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.large_trade_volume_share";"param";"q";"the quantile in (0;1]");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.large_trade_volume_share";"return";"";"the fraction of traded volume in large trades, 0n with no trades");
.man.registerArg (".qmicro.large_trade_volume_share";"eg";"";".qmicro.large_trade_volume_share[tape;0.9]");

.man.registerFile ("positions.q";"";".qpos";"positions.q - a weighted-average-cost position tracker for an FX book, plus per-currency exposure decomposition. Requires risk.q (pnl), ccy.q (ccy_pair_legs/ccy_pair_symbol) and forwards.q (cross_book_at, for ccy_exposure_in's cross-currency revaluation) to be loaded first. Like every other module here, this is pure: the position book is an explicit keyed table threaded in and out of each function, never a hidden global - apply_fill returns the *updated* table rather than mutating one in place. A live caller (e.g. a TorQ process subscribing to a `trade` table) owns the actual mutable state itself, the same way torq_cross_etl.q/torq_vectorize_etl.q wrap other uqf pure functions with their own local state and glue.");
.man.registerFunc (".qpos.empty_book";".qpos";"An empty position book: one row per sym once opened, running signed qty, volume-weighted average entry price of the *currently open* position, and cumulative realized P&L (quote currency). Unrealized P&L is never stored - see unrealized_pnl, computed on demand against a current market price.";".qpos.empty_book";".qpos.empty_book[]");
.man.registerArg (".qpos.empty_book";"return";"";"an empty keyed table, ready for apply_fill");
.man.registerArg (".qpos.empty_book";"eg";"";".qpos.empty_book[]");
.man.registerFunc (".qpos.apply_fill";".qpos";"Apply one fill to a position book, weighted-average-cost style: - opening from flat, or adding to a position in the same direction: avg_price becomes the size-weighted average of the old position and this fill; no P&L realized. - reducing a position without flipping it: the closed slice realizes P&L (via risk.q's pnl, at the *old* avg_price) using notional slice = fill_qty; avg_price is unchanged for what remains open. - closing a position exactly flat, or flipping it past flat: the whole old position is realized at this fill's price; if the fill was large enough to flip, the excess opens a new position *at this same fill price* (the standard convention - a reversing trade closes the old side and opens the new one at one execution price).";".qpos.apply_fill";".qpos.apply_fill[.qpos.empty_book[];`EURUSD;1000000;1.1000;1] -> qty 1000000, avg_price 1.1, realized_pnl 0");
.man.registerArg (".qpos.apply_fill";"param";"pos";"a position book (see empty_book) - not mutated, the updated");
.man.registerArg (".qpos.apply_fill";"param";"sym";"the symbol filled");
.man.registerArg (".qpos.apply_fill";"param";"qty";"fill size, base currency units (unsigned - see side)");
.man.registerArg (".qpos.apply_fill";"param";"price";"the fill price");
.man.registerArg (".qpos.apply_fill";"param";"side";"1 for a buy, -1 for a sell");
.man.registerArg (".qpos.apply_fill";"return";"";"pos with sym's row updated (or added, if new)");
.man.registerArg (".qpos.apply_fill";"eg";"";".qpos.apply_fill[.qpos.empty_book[];`EURUSD;1000000;1.1000;1] -> qty 1000000, avg_price 1.1, realized_pnl 0");
.man.registerArg (".qpos.apply_fill";"eg";"";".qpos.apply_fill[b;`EURUSD;400000;1.1050;-1] (b = book from the previous example) -> qty 600000, avg_price 1.1 (unchanged), realized_pnl 2000 (400000 closed at a 50-pip gain)");
.man.registerFunc (".qpos.apply_fills";".qpos";"Fold a whole trades table through apply_fill, in time order, to build up a position book from history - the many-fills counterpart to apply_fill's single fill. trades only needs to have these four columns (by name, in any order, alongside whatever else the caller's trades table carries - e.g. execution.q's markout_at_horizons shape, or a superset of it like env/schemas.q's .envschema.trades); positions.q has no dependency on where the table actually comes from.";".qpos.apply_fills";".qpos.apply_fills[.qpos.empty_book[];trades] (trades: .envschema.trades-shaped)");
.man.registerArg (".qpos.apply_fills";"param";"pos";"a position book (see empty_book) to fold trades into - not");
.man.registerArg (".qpos.apply_fills";"param";"trades";"a table with at least sym (symbol), size (fill qty, base");
.man.registerArg (".qpos.apply_fills";"return";"";"pos with every trade in trades applied, oldest first");
.man.registerArg (".qpos.apply_fills";"eg";"";".qpos.apply_fills[.qpos.empty_book[];trades] (trades: .envschema.trades-shaped)");
.man.registerFunc (".qpos.reconcile_trades";".qpos";"Recompute a position book from a trades table (via apply_fills) and diff it, per sym, against an independent reference book - e.g. a broker/prime broker statement, or any other qty/avg_price snapshot not itself derived from trades. A break here means the two sources disagree about what's actually open: a missed or duplicated fill, a booking error on either side, or a fill this trades table simply doesn't have yet. avg_price is only compared when *both* sides show a nonzero position - avg_price is 0 by empty_book/apply_fill convention once flat, so comparing it while one side is flat would flag a meaningless \"break\" every time a position closes out on one side before the other.";".qpos.reconcile_trades";".qpos.reconcile_trades[broker_book;trades;0f;1e-6]");
.man.registerArg (".qpos.reconcile_trades";"param";"reference_book";"a position book (see empty_book) from a source");
.man.registerArg (".qpos.reconcile_trades";"param";"trades";"a table with at least sym/size/trade_price/side/time -");
.man.registerArg (".qpos.reconcile_trades";"param";"qty_tol";"qty difference at or below this (in absolute base");
.man.registerArg (".qpos.reconcile_trades";"param";"price_tol";"avg_price difference at or below this doesn't count");
.man.registerArg (".qpos.reconcile_trades";"return";"";"a table sym/computed_qty/reference_qty/qty_diff/");
.man.registerArg (".qpos.reconcile_trades";"eg";"";".qpos.reconcile_trades[broker_book;trades;0f;1e-6]");
.man.registerFunc (".qpos.unrealized_pnl";".qpos";"Mark-to-market unrealized P&L of sym's currently open position, in quote currency - built on risk.q's own pnl formula (side/notional decomposed from the book's signed qty) rather than reimplementing it.";".qpos.unrealized_pnl";".qpos.unrealized_pnl[b;`EURUSD;1.1100] (b: 600000 EURUSD @ 1.1000) -> 6000");
.man.registerArg (".qpos.unrealized_pnl";"param";"pos";"a position book (see empty_book)");
.man.registerArg (".qpos.unrealized_pnl";"param";"sym";"the symbol to mark");
.man.registerArg (".qpos.unrealized_pnl";"param";"mkt_price";"the current market price to mark against");
.man.registerArg (".qpos.unrealized_pnl";"return";"";"unrealized P&L, in quote currency (0 if sym isn't in pos or is flat)");
.man.registerArg (".qpos.unrealized_pnl";"eg";"";".qpos.unrealized_pnl[b;`EURUSD;1.1100] (b: 600000 EURUSD @ 1.1000) -> 6000");
.man.registerFunc (".qpos.total_pnl";".qpos";"Total P&L (realized + mark-to-market unrealized) of sym's position.";".qpos.total_pnl";".qpos.total_pnl[b;`EURUSD;1.1100]");
.man.registerArg (".qpos.total_pnl";"param";"pos";"a position book (see empty_book)");
.man.registerArg (".qpos.total_pnl";"param";"sym";"the symbol to mark");
.man.registerArg (".qpos.total_pnl";"param";"mkt_price";"the current market price to mark against");
.man.registerArg (".qpos.total_pnl";"return";"";"total P&L, in quote currency");
.man.registerArg (".qpos.total_pnl";"eg";"";".qpos.total_pnl[b;`EURUSD;1.1100]");
.man.registerFunc (".qpos.ccy_legs";".qpos";"Decompose one pair position into its two currency legs, in *fixed*, dealt-rate notional terms - not marked to current market. Spot FX legs settle at the rate they were actually dealt at, so avg_price (the book's own cost basis), not some current price, is what's economically real about the quote-currency amount - that's what makes this decomposition well-defined without needing a market price at all.";".qpos.ccy_legs";".qpos.ccy_legs[`EURAUD;1000000;1.6000] -> +`ccy`amount!(`EUR`AUD;(1000000;-1600000f))");
.man.registerArg (".qpos.ccy_legs";"param";"sym";"the pair traded (any format ccy.q's normalize_ccy_pair accepts)");
.man.registerArg (".qpos.ccy_legs";"param";"qty";"signed position size, in the pair's base currency units");
.man.registerArg (".qpos.ccy_legs";"param";"avg_price";"the position's average entry/cost-basis rate");
.man.registerArg (".qpos.ccy_legs";"return";"";"a 2-row table `ccy`amount - the base currency's leg is +qty,");
.man.registerArg (".qpos.ccy_legs";"eg";"";".qpos.ccy_legs[`EURAUD;1000000;1.6000] -> +`ccy`amount!(`EUR`AUD;(1000000;-1600000f))");
.man.registerFunc (".qpos.ccy_exposure";".qpos";"Net exposure per currency across an entire position book - decomposes every row via ccy_legs (fixed, dealt-rate terms) and sums by currency, so a currency touched by several different pairs (majors and crosses alike) nets correctly into one number. Raw currency-unit amounts, not comparable to each other across currencies - see ccy_exposure_in to revalue everything into one reporting currency.";".qpos.ccy_exposure";".qpos.ccy_exposure[b] (b: long 1mm EURAUD @ 1.60, long 500k AUDUSD @ 0.65) -> AUD: -1,600,000+500,000 = -1,100,000; EUR: 1,000,000; USD: -325,000");
.man.registerArg (".qpos.ccy_exposure";"param";"pos";"a position book (see empty_book)");
.man.registerArg (".qpos.ccy_exposure";"return";"";"a table `ccy`amount, one row per currency touched by the book");
.man.registerArg (".qpos.ccy_exposure";"eg";"";".qpos.ccy_exposure[b] (b: long 1mm EURAUD @ 1.60, long 500k AUDUSD @ 0.65) -> AUD: -1,600,000+500,000 = -1,100,000; EUR: 1,000,000; USD: -325,000");
.man.registerFunc (".qpos.ccy_exposure_in";".qpos";"Net exposure per currency (see ccy_exposure), revalued into one reporting currency via forwards.q's own cross-currency chaining (cross_book_at) - so a currency with no *direct* quote against the reporting currency (e.g. PLN needing PLN->EUR->USD, exactly the AUDPLN-needs-three-legs case cross_book_at itself already handles for pricing a single pair) still resolves, using whatever pairs are actually available in quotes. Uses mid, not bid/ask, since this is an exposure report, not an executable price.";".qpos.ccy_exposure_in";".qpos.ccy_exposure_in[b;quotes;`USD;.z.p]");
.man.registerArg (".qpos.ccy_exposure_in";"param";"pos";"a position book (see empty_book)");
.man.registerArg (".qpos.ccy_exposure_in";"param";"quotes";"a depth-aware quotes table - forwards.q's own");
.man.registerArg (".qpos.ccy_exposure_in";"param";"reporting_ccy";"the currency to convert every leg into, e.g. `USD");
.man.registerArg (".qpos.ccy_exposure_in";"param";"at_time";"only consider quotes at or before this time");
.man.registerArg (".qpos.ccy_exposure_in";"return";"";"a table `ccy`amount`reporting_amount - amount is the raw");
.man.registerArg (".qpos.ccy_exposure_in";"throws";"";"error (from cross_book_at) if some currency has no chain of");
.man.registerArg (".qpos.ccy_exposure_in";"eg";"";".qpos.ccy_exposure_in[b;quotes;`USD;.z.p]");

.man.registerFile ("risk.q";"";".qrisk";"risk.q - position-level P&L, carry and Value-at-Risk helpers for an FX book. Requires stats.q (inv_ncdf) to be loaded first for var_parametric.");
.man.registerFunc (".qrisk.pip_value";".qrisk";"Money value of a 1-pip move on a given base-currency notional, in quote currency.";".qrisk.pip_value";".qrisk.pip_value[1000000;10000]  -> 100f");
.man.registerArg (".qrisk.pip_value";"param";"notional";"position size in base currency units");
.man.registerArg (".qrisk.pip_value";"param";"pip_factor";"10000 for most pairs, 100 for JPY crosses");
.man.registerArg (".qrisk.pip_value";"return";"";"the value of a 1-pip move, in quote currency");
.man.registerArg (".qrisk.pip_value";"eg";"";".qrisk.pip_value[1000000;10000]  -> 100f");
.man.registerFunc (".qrisk.pnl";".qrisk";"Mark-to-market P&L of a spot position, in quote currency.";".qrisk.pnl";".qrisk.pnl[1000000;1.1000;1.1050;1]  -> 5000f");
.man.registerArg (".qrisk.pnl";"param";"notional";"position size in base currency units");
.man.registerArg (".qrisk.pnl";"param";"entry_rate";"the rate the position was opened at");
.man.registerArg (".qrisk.pnl";"param";"exit_rate";"the current/closing rate");
.man.registerArg (".qrisk.pnl";"param";"side";"1 for long base currency, -1 for short");
.man.registerArg (".qrisk.pnl";"return";"";"the P&L, in quote currency");
.man.registerArg (".qrisk.pnl";"eg";"";".qrisk.pnl[1000000;1.1000;1.1050;1]  -> 5000f");
.man.registerFunc (".qrisk.carry_return";".qrisk";"Forward premium/discount implied by spot vs. an outright, as a decimal return: (F-S)/S. Positive means the base currency trades forward at a premium (i.e. rf is below rd under CIRP).";".qrisk.carry_return";".qrisk.carry_return[1.10;1.1050]  -> 0.004545455");
.man.registerArg (".qrisk.carry_return";"param";"spot";"spot rate");
.man.registerArg (".qrisk.carry_return";"param";"fwd";"outright forward rate");
.man.registerArg (".qrisk.carry_return";"return";"";"the decimal forward premium/discount");
.man.registerArg (".qrisk.carry_return";"eg";"";".qrisk.carry_return[1.10;1.1050]  -> 0.004545455");
.man.registerFunc (".qrisk.carry_pnl";".qrisk";"Money value of that same forward premium/discount on a given notional.";".qrisk.carry_pnl";".qrisk.carry_pnl[1000000;1.10;1.1050]  -> 5000f");
.man.registerArg (".qrisk.carry_pnl";"param";"notional";"position size in base currency units");
.man.registerArg (".qrisk.carry_pnl";"param";"spot";"spot rate");
.man.registerArg (".qrisk.carry_pnl";"param";"fwd";"outright forward rate");
.man.registerArg (".qrisk.carry_pnl";"return";"";"the carry P&L, in quote currency");
.man.registerArg (".qrisk.carry_pnl";"eg";"";".qrisk.carry_pnl[1000000;1.10;1.1050]  -> 5000f");
.man.registerFunc (".qrisk.var_parametric";".qrisk";"One-sided parametric (variance-covariance) VaR for a position with annualised volatility vol over a horizon of t years, at a one-tailed confidence level (e.g. 0.95, 0.99).";".qrisk.var_parametric";".qrisk.var_parametric[1000000;0.10;1%252;0.95]  -> 10361.6 (1-day 95% VaR)");
.man.registerArg (".qrisk.var_parametric";"param";"notional";"position size (sign ignored - VaR is a loss magnitude)");
.man.registerArg (".qrisk.var_parametric";"param";"vol";"annualised volatility, decimal (0.10 = 10%)");
.man.registerArg (".qrisk.var_parametric";"param";"t";"horizon, year fraction (e.g. 1%252 for one trading day)");
.man.registerArg (".qrisk.var_parametric";"param";"confidence";"one-tailed confidence level, e.g. 0.95 or 0.99");
.man.registerArg (".qrisk.var_parametric";"return";"";"a positive loss estimate, in the notional's currency");
.man.registerArg (".qrisk.var_parametric";"eg";"";".qrisk.var_parametric[1000000;0.10;1%252;0.95]  -> 10361.6 (1-day 95% VaR)");
.man.registerFunc (".qrisk.var_historical";".qrisk";"Historical simulation VaR: the magnitude of the (1-confidence) empirical percentile of a series of historical P&L outcomes.";".qrisk.var_historical";".qrisk.var_historical[-100+til 200;0.95]  -> 90 (5th percentile of a 200-outcome series)");
.man.registerArg (".qrisk.var_historical";"param";"pnl_series";"a list of historical P&L outcomes");
.man.registerArg (".qrisk.var_historical";"param";"confidence";"one-tailed confidence level, e.g. 0.95 or 0.99");
.man.registerArg (".qrisk.var_historical";"return";"";"a positive loss estimate, in the same units as pnl_series");
.man.registerArg (".qrisk.var_historical";"eg";"";".qrisk.var_historical[-100+til 200;0.95]  -> 90 (5th percentile of a 200-outcome series)");

.man.registerFile ("forwards.q";"";".qfwd";"forwards.q - FX forward/swap points via covered interest rate parity (CIRP), and rate/cross-rate helpers used on an eFX forwards desk. Currency pair convention: a rate quoted BASE/QUOTE means 1 unit of BASE buys `rate` units of QUOTE (e.g. EURUSD 1.1000 -> 1 EUR = 1.10 USD). rd is the QUOTE currency's interest rate, rf the BASE currency's.");
.man.registerFunc (".qfwd.fwd_simple";".qfwd";"Outright forward rate under simple-interest CIRP: F = S*(1+rd*t)/(1+rf*t)";".qfwd.fwd_simple";".qfwd.fwd_simple[1.10;0.05;0.02;1]  -> 1.132353");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.fwd_simple";"param";"spot";"spot rate, BASE/QUOTE");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.fwd_simple";"param";"rd";"domestic (quote currency) decimal annual rate");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.fwd_simple";"param";"rf";"foreign (base currency) decimal annual rate");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.fwd_simple";"param";"t";"year fraction to the forward date");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.fwd_simple";"return";"";"the outright forward rate");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.fwd_simple";"eg";"";".qfwd.fwd_simple[1.10;0.05;0.02;1]  -> 1.132353");
.man.registerFunc (".qfwd.fwd_cont";".qfwd";"Outright forward rate under continuous-compounding CIRP: F = S*exp((rd-rf)*t)";".qfwd.fwd_cont";".qfwd.fwd_cont[1.10;0.05;0.02;1]  -> 1.1335");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.fwd_cont";"param";"spot";"spot rate, BASE/QUOTE");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.fwd_cont";"param";"rd";"domestic (quote currency) decimal annual rate");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.fwd_cont";"param";"rf";"foreign (base currency) decimal annual rate");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.fwd_cont";"param";"t";"year fraction to the forward date");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.fwd_cont";"return";"";"the outright forward rate");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.fwd_cont";"eg";"";".qfwd.fwd_cont[1.10;0.05;0.02;1]  -> 1.1335");
.man.registerFunc (".qfwd.fwd_points";".qfwd";"Forward points = (F-S) scaled into pips.";".qfwd.fwd_points";".qfwd.fwd_points[1.132353;1.10;10000]  -> 323.53");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.fwd_points";"param";"fwd";"outright forward rate");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.fwd_points";"param";"spot";"spot rate");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.fwd_points";"param";"pip_factor";"10000 for most pairs, 100 for JPY crosses");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.fwd_points";"return";"";"the forward points, in pips");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.fwd_points";"eg";"";".qfwd.fwd_points[1.132353;1.10;10000]  -> 323.53");
.man.registerFunc (".qfwd.points_to_outright";".qfwd";"Recover the outright from spot plus forward points.";".qfwd.points_to_outright";".qfwd.points_to_outright[1.10;323.53;10000]  -> 1.132353");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.points_to_outright";"param";"spot";"spot rate");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.points_to_outright";"param";"points";"forward points, in pips");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.points_to_outright";"param";"pip_factor";"10000 for most pairs, 100 for JPY crosses");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.points_to_outright";"return";"";"the outright forward rate");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.points_to_outright";"eg";"";".qfwd.points_to_outright[1.10;323.53;10000]  -> 1.132353");
.man.registerFunc (".qfwd.implied_foreign_rate";".qfwd";"Back out the implied foreign (base currency) rate from an observed outright, given the domestic (quote currency) rate - simple CIRP inverse.";".qfwd.implied_foreign_rate";".qfwd.implied_foreign_rate[1.10;1.132353;0.05;1]  -> 0.01999995 (0.02 to the eye; the residual is the rounding in the 1.132353 input, and documenting 0.02 overstated what the function returns)");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.implied_foreign_rate";"param";"spot";"spot rate");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.implied_foreign_rate";"param";"fwd";"observed outright forward rate");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.implied_foreign_rate";"param";"rd";"domestic (quote currency) decimal annual rate");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.implied_foreign_rate";"param";"t";"year fraction to the forward date");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.implied_foreign_rate";"return";"";"the implied foreign (base currency) decimal annual rate");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.implied_foreign_rate";"eg";"";".qfwd.implied_foreign_rate[1.10;1.132353;0.05;1]  -> 0.01999995 (0.02 to the eye; the residual is the rounding in the 1.132353 input, and documenting 0.02 overstated what the function returns)");
.man.registerFunc (".qfwd.implied_domestic_rate";".qfwd";"Back out the implied domestic (quote currency) rate from an observed outright, given the foreign (base currency) rate - simple CIRP inverse.";".qfwd.implied_domestic_rate";".qfwd.implied_domestic_rate[1.10;1.132353;0.02;1]  -> 0.05000005 (0.05 to the eye - same rounding residual as implied_foreign_rate)");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.implied_domestic_rate";"param";"spot";"spot rate");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.implied_domestic_rate";"param";"fwd";"observed outright forward rate");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.implied_domestic_rate";"param";"rf";"foreign (base currency) decimal annual rate");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.implied_domestic_rate";"param";"t";"year fraction to the forward date");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.implied_domestic_rate";"return";"";"the implied domestic (quote currency) decimal annual rate");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.implied_domestic_rate";"eg";"";".qfwd.implied_domestic_rate[1.10;1.132353;0.02;1]  -> 0.05000005 (0.05 to the eye - same rounding residual as implied_foreign_rate)");
.man.registerFunc (".qfwd.cross_rate";".qfwd";"Triangulate a cross rate: A/B * B/C = A/C.";".qfwd.cross_rate";".qfwd.cross_rate[1.10;150]  -> 165f (EURUSD * USDJPY -> EURJPY)");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_rate";"param";"ab_rate";"rate for A/B");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_rate";"param";"bc_rate";"rate for B/C");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_rate";"return";"";"the implied rate for A/C");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_rate";"eg";"";".qfwd.cross_rate[1.10;150]  -> 165f (EURUSD * USDJPY -> EURJPY)");
.man.registerFunc (".qfwd.invert_rate";".qfwd";"Invert a quote convention: BASE/QUOTE -> QUOTE/BASE.";".qfwd.invert_rate";".qfwd.invert_rate 2f  -> 0.5");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.invert_rate";"param";"rate";"a rate quoted BASE/QUOTE");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.invert_rate";"return";"";"the same rate quoted QUOTE/BASE");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.invert_rate";"eg";"";".qfwd.invert_rate 2f  -> 0.5");
.man.registerFunc (".qfwd.cross_rate_shared_base";".qfwd";"Cross rate via a shared base currency: given two pairs both quoted A/X and A/Y (e.g. EURPLN and EURUSD both quoted against EUR), returns the Y/X cross rate - the common eFX pattern of computing e.g. USDPLN from EURPLN and EURUSD as EURPLN * (1/EURUSD). Simply cross_rate composed with invert_rate; kept as its own named function because \"invert the second one, not the first\" is exactly the kind of thing that's easy to get backwards at the desk.";".qfwd.cross_rate_shared_base";".qfwd.cross_rate_shared_base[4.30;1.075]  -> 4f (EURPLN, EURUSD -> USDPLN)");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_rate_shared_base";"param";"rate_ax";"rate for A/X (the shared base A over the first quote currency X)");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_rate_shared_base";"param";"rate_ay";"rate for A/Y (the shared base A over the second quote currency Y)");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_rate_shared_base";"return";"";"the implied rate for Y/X");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_rate_shared_base";"eg";"";".qfwd.cross_rate_shared_base[4.30;1.075]  -> 4f (EURPLN, EURUSD -> USDPLN)");
.man.registerFunc (".qfwd.invert_book";".qfwd";"Invert an order book: BASE/QUOTE -> QUOTE/BASE. Inverting flips which side is more favourable, so the new bid comes from the old ask and vice versa.";".qfwd.invert_book";".qfwd.invert_book[`bid`ask!(1.1000;1.1002)]  -> `bid`ask!0.9089256 0.9090909");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.invert_book";"param";"book";"a dict `bid`ask!(bidPx;askPx) quoted BASE/QUOTE");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.invert_book";"return";"";"the same book quoted QUOTE/BASE");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.invert_book";"eg";"";".qfwd.invert_book[`bid`ask!(1.1000;1.1002)]  -> `bid`ask!0.9089256 0.9090909");
.man.registerFunc (".qfwd.combine_oriented_books";".qfwd";"Combine two order books that are already oriented A/B and B/C (i.e. the quote currency of the first leg matches the base currency of the second) into a top-of-book A/C book. Private building block for cross_book; call directly if you've already oriented the legs yourself.";".qfwd.combine_oriented_books";".qfwd.combine_oriented_books[`bid`ask!(1.1000;1.1002);`bid`ask!(150.00;150.02)]  -> `bid`ask!165 165.052");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.combine_oriented_books";"param";"book_ab";"dict `bid`ask!(bidPx;askPx) quoted A/B");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.combine_oriented_books";"param";"book_bc";"dict `bid`ask!(bidPx;askPx) quoted B/C");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.combine_oriented_books";"return";"";"dict `bid`ask!(bidPx;askPx) quoted A/C");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.combine_oriented_books";"eg";"";".qfwd.combine_oriented_books[`bid`ask!(1.1000;1.1002);`bid`ask!(150.00;150.02)]  -> `bid`ask!165 165.052");
.man.registerFunc (".qfwd.book_crossed";".qfwd";"True if a book is crossed (bid > ask) - a sanity check for synthetic books built from cross_book/combine_oriented_books.";".qfwd.book_crossed";".qfwd.book_crossed[`bid`ask!(1.1000;1.1002)]  -> 0b");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.book_crossed";"param";"book";"a dict `bid`ask!(bidPx;askPx)");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.book_crossed";"return";"";"1b if bid>ask, else 0b");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.book_crossed";"eg";"";".qfwd.book_crossed[`bid`ask!(1.1000;1.1002)]  -> 0b");
.man.registerFunc (".qfwd.ccy_orient_cross";".qfwd";"Private: work out how two currency pairs relate - which currency they share, what the resulting cross pair's symbol is, and whether either leg needs inverting before combining - without touching any prices. Shared by cross_book and cross_book_at_sizes so the two can never disagree about orientation.";".qfwd.ccy_orient_cross";".qfwd.ccy_orient_cross[`EURUSD;`USDJPY]  -> `cross_sym`invert1`invert2!(`EURJPY;0b;0b)");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.ccy_orient_cross";"param";"sym1";"currency pair for leg 1, any format ccy.q's normalize_ccy_pair accepts");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.ccy_orient_cross";"param";"sym2";"currency pair for leg 2, any format ccy.q's normalize_ccy_pair accepts");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.ccy_orient_cross";"return";"";"dict `cross_sym`invert1`invert2 - cross_sym is the resulting pair");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.ccy_orient_cross";"throws";"";"error if sym1 and sym2 share no common currency");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.ccy_orient_cross";"eg";"";".qfwd.ccy_orient_cross[`EURUSD;`USDJPY]  -> `cross_sym`invert1`invert2!(`EURJPY;0b;0b)");
.man.registerFunc (".qfwd.cross_book";".qfwd";"Build a synthetic top-of-book cross rate from two live order books on pairs that share a common currency, e.g. cross_book[`EURUSD;eurusd_book; `USDJPY;usdjpy_book] -> a synthetic EURJPY book. The shared currency is detected automatically and either leg is inverted as needed - the caller does not need to pre-orient anything. sym1/sym2 are normalized via ccy.q's ccy_pair_legs, so `eurusd, \"eur/usd\" etc. work too, not just canonical `EURUSD. Combines top-of-book only; it does not walk/net multiple depth levels of the underlying books - see cross_book_at_sizes for that.";".qfwd.cross_book";".qfwd.cross_book[`EURUSD;`bid`ask!(1.1000;1.1002);`USDJPY;`bid`ask!(150.00;150.02)]  -> `sym`bid`ask!(`EURJPY;165f;165.052)");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_book";"param";"sym1";"currency pair for book1, any format ccy.q's normalize_ccy_pair accepts");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_book";"param";"book1";"dict `bid`ask!(bidPx;askPx) quoted in sym1's own convention");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_book";"param";"sym2";"currency pair for book2, any format ccy.q's normalize_ccy_pair accepts");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_book";"param";"book2";"dict `bid`ask!(bidPx;askPx) quoted in sym2's own convention");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_book";"return";"";"dict `sym`bid`ask!(cross_sym;bidPx;askPx) for the synthetic cross");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_book";"throws";"";"error if sym1 and sym2 share no common currency");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_book";"eg";"";".qfwd.cross_book[`EURUSD;`bid`ask!(1.1000;1.1002);`USDJPY;`bid`ask!(150.00;150.02)]  -> `sym`bid`ask!(`EURJPY;165f;165.052)");
.man.registerFunc (".qfwd.invert_book_depth";".qfwd";"Invert a multi-level depth ladder: BASE/QUOTE -> QUOTE/BASE. Prices invert elementwise (order stays best-first automatically: inverting a monotonic ladder reverses its sense exactly the way flipping ask<->bid requires). Sizes rescale into the new base currency - a level of size BASE units at price QUOTE/BASE is worth size*price QUOTE units, which become the new base currency's size.";".qfwd.invert_book_depth";".qfwd.invert_book_depth[1.1000 1.1002;1000000 1000000]  -> (0.9090909 0.9089256;1100000 1100200f)");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.invert_book_depth";"param";"prices";"level prices, best-first");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.invert_book_depth";"param";"sizes";"level sizes in the ladder's own base currency, aligned to prices");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.invert_book_depth";"return";"";"(invertedPrices;rescaledSizes), still best-first");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.invert_book_depth";"eg";"";".qfwd.invert_book_depth[1.1000 1.1002;1000000 1000000]  -> (0.9090909 0.9089256;1100000 1100200f)");
.man.registerFunc (".qfwd.oriented_levels";".qfwd";"Private: the (prices;sizes) to sweep for one leg, for one side of the final cross. If this leg doesn't need inverting, that's just its own same-named side; if it does, it's the *other* original side, inverted (an inverted bid becomes an ask, and vice versa).";".qfwd.oriented_levels";"");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.oriented_levels";"param";"side";"`bid or `ask - the side of the final cross being priced");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.oriented_levels";"param";"book";"dict `bid_prices`bid_sizes`ask_prices`ask_sizes for this leg");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.oriented_levels";"param";"invert";"1b if this leg's convention needs flipping");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.oriented_levels";"return";"";"(prices;sizes) to pass to sweep_price");
.man.registerFunc (".qfwd.cross_sweep_side";".qfwd";"Private: sweep one side of a 2-leg cross at one size, converting the notional hop-by-hop - leg 2 is swept at the amount of the shared/bridge currency that leg 1's sweep actually produced, not at the raw input size.";".qfwd.cross_sweep_side";"");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_sweep_side";"param";"book1";"dict `bid_prices`bid_sizes`ask_prices`ask_sizes for leg 1");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_sweep_side";"param";"book2";"dict `bid_prices`bid_sizes`ask_prices`ask_sizes for leg 2");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_sweep_side";"param";"side";"`bid or `ask - the side of the final cross being priced");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_sweep_side";"param";"size";"the size to sweep, in leg 1's relevant currency");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_sweep_side";"param";"invert1";"1b if leg 1 needs its convention flipped");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_sweep_side";"param";"invert2";"1b if leg 2 needs its convention flipped");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_sweep_side";"return";"";"dict `price`filled_size`fully_filled for this side of the cross");
.man.registerFunc (".qfwd.cross_book_at_one_size";".qfwd";"Private: bid, ask and mid for a 2-leg cross at a single size. mid is the average of the swept cross bid and the swept cross ask at that size (not a separate sweep of its own).";".qfwd.cross_book_at_one_size";"");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_book_at_one_size";"param";"sym1";"currency pair for leg 1");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_book_at_one_size";"param";"book1";"dict `bid_prices`bid_sizes`ask_prices`ask_sizes for leg 1");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_book_at_one_size";"param";"sym2";"currency pair for leg 2");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_book_at_one_size";"param";"book2";"dict `bid_prices`bid_sizes`ask_prices`ask_sizes for leg 2");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_book_at_one_size";"param";"size";"the size to sweep, in leg 1's relevant currency");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_book_at_one_size";"return";"";"one row: dict `size`sym`bid`bid_filled_size`bid_fully_filled`ask`ask_filled_size`ask_fully_filled`mid");
.man.registerFunc (".qfwd.side_cols";".qfwd";"Private: the result columns contributed by one requested side.";".qfwd.side_cols";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qfwd.cross_book_at_sizes";".qfwd";"Depth-aware synthetic cross rate: like cross_book, but walks multi-level order book depth on both legs for each requested size, converting the notional hop-by-hop (leg 2 is swept at the bridge-currency amount leg 1's sweep actually produced), and returns only the sides you ask for. Each leg's book must supply real depth, not just top-of-book - see sweep_price's book shape.";".qfwd.cross_book_at_sizes";".qfwd.cross_book_at_sizes[`EURUSD;eurusd_book;`USDJPY;usdjpy_book;1000000 3000000;`bid`ask`mid]");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_book_at_sizes";"param";"sym1";"currency pair for leg 1, any format ccy.q's normalize_ccy_pair accepts");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_book_at_sizes";"param";"book1";"dict `bid_prices`bid_sizes`ask_prices`ask_sizes for leg 1, each level best-first");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_book_at_sizes";"param";"sym2";"currency pair for leg 2, any format ccy.q's normalize_ccy_pair accepts");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_book_at_sizes";"param";"book2";"dict `bid_prices`bid_sizes`ask_prices`ask_sizes for leg 2, each level best-first");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_book_at_sizes";"param";"sizes";"list of sizes to price, e.g. 1000000 2000000 5000000");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_book_at_sizes";"param";"sides";"subset of `bid`ask`mid to include in the result");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_book_at_sizes";"return";"";"a table, one row per size, columns `size`sym plus whichever of");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_book_at_sizes";"throws";"";"error if sym1 and sym2 share no common currency, or sides has");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_book_at_sizes";"eg";"";".qfwd.cross_book_at_sizes[`EURUSD;eurusd_book;`USDJPY;usdjpy_book;1000000 3000000;`bid`ask`mid]");
.man.registerFunc (".qfwd.ccy_orient_chain";".qfwd";"Private: like ccy_orient_cross, but resolves orientation across an arbitrary chain of N>=2 currency pairs instead of just two - walks the legs in order, threading the running cross symbol forward via repeated calls to ccy_orient_cross. A running cross symbol is always already correctly oriented forward (that's what ccy_orient_cross guarantees for its own cross_sym), so it never needs inverting against the next leg - only that next leg does.";".qfwd.ccy_orient_chain";".qfwd.ccy_orient_chain[`EURUSD`USDJPY`JPYCHF]  -> `cross_sym`inverts!(`EURCHF;000b)");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.ccy_orient_chain";"param";"syms";"list of currency pair syms, one per leg, in traversal");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.ccy_orient_chain";"return";"";"dict `cross_sym`inverts - cross_sym is the resulting end-to-end");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.ccy_orient_chain";"throws";"";"error if syms has fewer than 2 legs, or if two consecutive legs");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.ccy_orient_chain";"eg";"";".qfwd.ccy_orient_chain[`EURUSD`USDJPY`JPYCHF]  -> `cross_sym`inverts!(`EURCHF;000b)");
.man.registerFunc (".qfwd.cross_sweep_chain";".qfwd";"Private: sweep one side of an N-leg cross at one size, folding the notional hop-by-hop across every leg - leg i+1 is swept at the bridge notional leg i's sweep actually produced, exactly generalizing cross_sweep_side's 2-leg logic to an arbitrary chain length. The reported filled_size is always leg 1's filled_size (the constraint is expressed in leg 1's units, same as the 2-leg version); fully_filled is the AND of every leg's fully_filled, so a shortfall on any leg - including a middle leg - shows up even though leg 1 itself filled completely.";".qfwd.cross_sweep_chain";"");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_sweep_chain";"param";"books";"list of dicts `bid_prices`bid_sizes`ask_prices`ask_sizes,");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_sweep_chain";"param";"side";"`bid or `ask - the side of the final cross being priced");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_sweep_chain";"param";"size";"the size to sweep, in leg 1's relevant currency");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_sweep_chain";"param";"inverts";"boolean list, one per leg, from ccy_orient_chain");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_sweep_chain";"return";"";"dict `price`filled_size`fully_filled for this side of the cross");
.man.registerFunc (".qfwd.cross_book_chain_at_one_size";".qfwd";"Private: bid, ask and mid for an N-leg cross at a single size. mid is the average of the swept cross bid and the swept cross ask at that size (not a separate sweep of its own) - same convention as cross_book_at_one_size.";".qfwd.cross_book_chain_at_one_size";"");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_book_chain_at_one_size";"param";"syms";"list of currency pair syms, one per leg, in traversal order");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_book_chain_at_one_size";"param";"books";"list of dicts `bid_prices`bid_sizes`ask_prices`ask_sizes,");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_book_chain_at_one_size";"param";"size";"the size to sweep, in leg 1's relevant currency");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_book_chain_at_one_size";"return";"";"one row: dict `size`sym`bid`bid_filled_size`bid_fully_filled`ask`ask_filled_size`ask_fully_filled`mid");
.man.registerFunc (".qfwd.cross_book_chain_at_sizes";".qfwd";"Depth-aware synthetic cross rate across an arbitrary chain of N>=2 legs: generalizes cross_book_at_sizes from exactly 2 legs to N, e.g. EURUSD -> USDJPY -> JPYCHF -> EURCHF. Walks multi-level order book depth on every leg for each requested size, converting the notional hop-by-hop (leg i+1 is swept at the bridge-currency amount leg i's sweep actually produced), and returns only the sides you ask for. Each leg's book must supply real depth, not just top-of-book - see sweep_price's book shape. Consecutive legs must share a currency (in either position); syms/books are matched by position, not re-sorted or re-oriented for you. Note: since every leg's book dict shares the same keys, passing books as (book1;book2;...) commonly auto-flips into a kdb+ table (type 98h) rather than staying a generic list - this is harmless here, since positional indexing (books i) returns the same dict either way.";".qfwd.cross_book_chain_at_sizes";".qfwd.cross_book_chain_at_sizes[`EURUSD`USDJPY`JPYCHF;(eurusd_book;usdjpy_book;jpychf_book);1000000 3000000;`bid`ask`mid]");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_book_chain_at_sizes";"param";"syms";"list of currency pair syms, one per leg, in traversal");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_book_chain_at_sizes";"param";"books";"list of dicts `bid_prices`bid_sizes`ask_prices`ask_sizes,");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_book_chain_at_sizes";"param";"sizes";"list of sizes to price, e.g. 1000000 2000000 5000000");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_book_chain_at_sizes";"param";"sides";"subset of `bid`ask`mid to include in the result");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_book_chain_at_sizes";"return";"";"a table, one row per size, columns `size`sym plus whichever of");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_book_chain_at_sizes";"throws";"";"error if syms has fewer than 2 legs, if syms and books aren't");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_book_chain_at_sizes";"eg";"";".qfwd.cross_book_chain_at_sizes[`EURUSD`USDJPY`JPYCHF;(eurusd_book;usdjpy_book;jpychf_book);1000000 3000000;`bid`ask`mid]");
.man.registerFunc (".qfwd.ccy_graph_edges";".qfwd";"Private: an undirected currency graph, one edge per direction per available quoted pair - e.g. `EURUSD contributes both EUR->USD and USD->EUR, both tagged with the symbol `EURUSD (the direction it needs inverting, if any, is worked out later by ccy_orient_chain, not here).";".qfwd.ccy_graph_edges";"");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.ccy_graph_edges";"param";"avail_syms";"currency pair symbols known to be quotable");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.ccy_graph_edges";"return";"";"table `src`dst`via, one row per direction per pair");
.man.registerFunc (".qfwd.ccy_shortest_path";".qfwd";"Breadth-first search for the shortest chain of available quoted pairs connecting two currencies - e.g. given `AUDUSD`EURUSD`EURPLN available, finds that AUD->PLN needs `AUDUSD`EURUSD`EURPLN (via the shared USD and EUR legs), while USD->PLN only needs `EURUSD`EURPLN. Returned symbols are in their own original (unoriented) form - pass the result straight to ccy_orient_chain/cross_book_chain_at_sizes, which work out which legs need inverting.";".qfwd.ccy_shortest_path";".qfwd.ccy_shortest_path[`AUDUSD`EURUSD`EURPLN;`AUD;`PLN]  -> `AUDUSD`EURUSD`EURPLN");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.ccy_shortest_path";"param";"avail_syms";"currency pair symbols known to be quotable");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.ccy_shortest_path";"param";"start_ccy";"the starting 3-letter currency code");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.ccy_shortest_path";"param";"goal_ccy";"the target 3-letter currency code");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.ccy_shortest_path";"return";"";"ordered list of pair symbols to chain, or an empty symbol list");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.ccy_shortest_path";"eg";"";".qfwd.ccy_shortest_path[`AUDUSD`EURUSD`EURPLN;`AUD;`PLN]  -> `AUDUSD`EURUSD`EURPLN");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.ccy_shortest_path";"eg";"";".qfwd.ccy_shortest_path[`AUDUSD`EURUSD;`AUD;`JPY]  -> `symbol$() (JPY isn't reachable from the available pairs)");
.man.registerFunc (".qfwd.cross_decomp";".qfwd";"Work out which quoted pairs are needed to build a synthetic cross rate for sym, and in what order - the \"recipe\" cross_book_at follows automatically, exposed on its own for inspection (or for feeding into something other than a depth-aware book, e.g. ccy_orient_chain directly). A thin wrapper around ccy_shortest_path: parses sym into its two currencies and BFS-searches avail_syms for the shortest chain connecting them.";".qfwd.cross_decomp";".qfwd.cross_decomp[`AUDUSD`EURUSD`EURPLN;`AUDPLN]  -> `AUDUSD`EURUSD`EURPLN");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_decomp";"param";"avail_syms";"currency pair symbols known to be quotable");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_decomp";"param";"sym";"the pair to decompose, any format ccy.q's normalize_ccy_pair accepts");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_decomp";"return";"";"ordered list of pair symbols to chain (their own original,");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_decomp";"eg";"";".qfwd.cross_decomp[`AUDUSD`EURUSD`EURPLN;`AUDPLN]  -> `AUDUSD`EURUSD`EURPLN");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_decomp";"eg";"";".qfwd.cross_decomp[`EURUSD`USDRUB;`EURRUB]  -> `EURUSD`USDRUB");
.man.registerFunc (".qfwd.leg_book_as_of";".qfwd";"Private: one symbol's book, as of a given time, pulled out of a quotes table via an as-of join (aj) - the most recent row at or before at_time. Requires quotes already sorted `sym`ts xasc - cross_book_at checks that once up front (aj on unsorted data doesn't error, it silently returns wrong rows), not repeated here on every leg lookup.";".qfwd.leg_book_as_of";"");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.leg_book_as_of";"throws";"";"error if quotes has no row for target_sym at or before at_time");
.man.registerFunc (".qfwd.single_leg_at_one_size";".qfwd";"Private: bid, ask and mid for a single already-available leg at one size - the 1-leg-chain analogue of cross_book_at_one_size, used by cross_book_at when the requested pair (or its inverse) is quoted directly, with no chaining needed.";".qfwd.single_leg_at_one_size";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qfwd.single_leg_at_sizes";".qfwd";"Private: like cross_book_chain_at_sizes, but for exactly one leg.";".qfwd.single_leg_at_sizes";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qfwd.require_quotes_cols";".qfwd";"Private: throws a clear, single error naming every column missing from quotes - shared by every function here that takes a quotes table, so a malformed one (wrong or misspelled column, e.g. ask_price instead of ask_prices) fails loudly and immediately here, rather than either erroring confusingly deep inside leg_book_as_of/aj, or - worse - being silently swallowed into a null by cross_ref_price_at's own protective error handling, which exists for the different, genuinely expected case of \"no quote yet at this time\", not a malformed table.";".qfwd.require_quotes_cols";"");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.require_quotes_cols";"throws";"";"error naming every column in `ts`sym`bid_prices`bid_sizes`ask_prices`ask_sizes missing from quotes");
.man.registerFunc (".qfwd.cross_book_at";".qfwd";"Depth-aware synthetic book for any pair, found automatically by chaining together whatever quoted pairs are available in `quotes` - unlike cross_book_chain_at_sizes, you don't need to know or supply the leg chain yourself. Finds the shortest currency-graph path (ccy_shortest_path) from sym's base to its quote currency using quotes' own distinct `sym` column as the available quoted pairs, looks up each leg's most recent quote at or before at_time (leg_book_as_of), then delegates the actual depth-aware pricing to cross_book_chain_at_sizes - or, if sym (or its inverse) is quoted directly and no chaining is needed at all, prices that single leg directly.";".qfwd.cross_book_at";".qfwd.cross_book_at[`sym`ts xasc quotes;`AUDPLN;.z.p;1000000 3000000;`bid`ask`mid]");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_book_at";"param";"quotes";"table `ts`sym`bid_prices`bid_sizes`ask_prices`ask_sizes,");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_book_at";"param";"sym";"the pair to price, any format ccy.q's normalize_ccy_pair accepts");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_book_at";"param";"at_time";"only consider quotes at or before this time");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_book_at";"param";"sizes";"list of sizes to price, e.g. 1000000 3000000");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_book_at";"param";"sides";"subset of `bid`ask`mid to include in the result");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_book_at";"return";"";"a table, one row per size - see cross_book_chain_at_sizes");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_book_at";"throws";"";"error if quotes is missing a required column, isn't sorted");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_book_at";"eg";"";".qfwd.cross_book_at[`sym`ts xasc quotes;`AUDPLN;.z.p;1000000 3000000;`bid`ask`mid]");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_book_at";"eg";"";".qfwd.cross_book_at[`sym`ts xasc quotes;`EURUSD;.z.p;enlist 1000000;enlist `mid]  -> EURUSD is quoted directly in this quotes table, so no chaining is needed");
.man.registerFunc (".qfwd.cross_price_ok_at_size";".qfwd";"Private: true if sweeping `size` on `side` (via cross_book_at) still lands at an average price at least as good as price_limit, and the sweep is fully filled. `bid` side: good means avg_price>=price_limit (selling at proceeds no worse than wanted); `ask` side: good means avg_price<=price_limit (buying at cost no worse than wanted).";".qfwd.cross_price_ok_at_size";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qfwd.CROSS_SIZE_MAX_DOUBLINGS";".qfwd";"Configurable search tuning for cross_size_at_price's two-phase binary search - max_doublings bounds the initial upper-bound search (a hi of 2^60 base-currency units is past any realistic tradeable size, so this is a worst-case cap, not an expected one); rel_tol/max_halvings bound the bisection phase once a bracket is found (relative, not absolute, since \"close enough\" scales with the size itself - 2.5mm needs a much coarser absolute tolerance than 2.5). Override before calling if your instrument universe needs a coarser/finer size resolution, e.g. .qfwd.CROSS_SIZE_REL_TOL:1e-4 for faster, coarser sizing.";".qfwd.CROSS_SIZE_MAX_DOUBLINGS";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qfwd.CROSS_SIZE_REL_TOL";".qfwd";"Configurable search tuning for cross_size_at_price's two-phase binary search - max_doublings bounds the initial upper-bound search (a hi of 2^60 base-currency units is past any realistic tradeable size, so this is a worst-case cap, not an expected one); rel_tol/max_halvings bound the bisection phase once a bracket is found (relative, not absolute, since \"close enough\" scales with the size itself - 2.5mm needs a much coarser absolute tolerance than 2.5). Override before calling if your instrument universe needs a coarser/finer size resolution, e.g. .qfwd.CROSS_SIZE_REL_TOL:1e-4 for faster, coarser sizing.";".qfwd.CROSS_SIZE_REL_TOL";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qfwd.CROSS_SIZE_MAX_HALVINGS";".qfwd";"Configurable search tuning for cross_size_at_price's two-phase binary search - max_doublings bounds the initial upper-bound search (a hi of 2^60 base-currency units is past any realistic tradeable size, so this is a worst-case cap, not an expected one); rel_tol/max_halvings bound the bisection phase once a bracket is found (relative, not absolute, since \"close enough\" scales with the size itself - 2.5mm needs a much coarser absolute tolerance than 2.5). Override before calling if your instrument universe needs a coarser/finer size resolution, e.g. .qfwd.CROSS_SIZE_REL_TOL:1e-4 for faster, coarser sizing.";".qfwd.CROSS_SIZE_MAX_HALVINGS";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qfwd.cross_size_at_price";".qfwd";"Largest size (in sym's base currency) tradeable on one side without the average swept price crossing price_limit - the inverse question to cross_book_at's \"at this size, what's the price\". There is no closed form for this: the swept average price compounds across every leg's own depth (see cross_sweep_chain), so it can only be evaluated forward, size -> price, not inverted directly. This binary-searches size instead, using cross_book_at itself as the price oracle at each candidate (first doubling to find an upper bound, since sym's total tradeable depth isn't known up front either).";".qfwd.cross_size_at_price";".qfwd.cross_size_at_price[quotes;`AUDPLN;.z.p;`bid;2.5650]");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_size_at_price";"param";"quotes";"table `ts`sym`bid_prices`bid_sizes`ask_prices`ask_sizes, sorted `sym`ts xasc");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_size_at_price";"param";"sym";"the pair to price, any format ccy.q's normalize_ccy_pair accepts");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_size_at_price";"param";"at_time";"only consider quotes at or before this time");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_size_at_price";"param";"side";"`bid (how much can be SOLD at avg price at least price_limit) or");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_size_at_price";"param";"price_limit";"the price boundary");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_size_at_price";"return";"";"the largest size tradeable without the average price crossing");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_size_at_price";"throws";"";"error if side isn't `bid or `ask, or anything cross_book_at itself throws");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_size_at_price";"eg";"";".qfwd.cross_size_at_price[quotes;`AUDPLN;.z.p;`bid;2.5650]");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_size_at_price";"eg";"";".qfwd.cross_size_at_price[quotes;`AUDPLN;.z.p;`ask;2.5700]  -> the ask-side (buy) boundary at a different price limit");
.man.registerFunc (".qfwd.cross_ref_price_at";".qfwd";"Private: cross_book_at's mid for sym at at_time, at a caller-chosen (typically negligible, top-of-book-ish) size - used wherever a \"price at a point in time\" is needed for a synthetic pair with no quoted mid of its own. Nulls out rather than throwing if no quote exists yet for some required leg at or before at_time, so a caller sweeping many timestamps (cross_markout_at_horizons, cross_markout_decomp) can null one bad lookup instead of failing the whole batch.";".qfwd.cross_ref_price_at";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qfwd.ts_col";".qfwd";"Configurable output column name for the \"point in time\" a row in cross_markout_at_horizons/cross_impact_at_horizons refers to - defaults to `ts to match the quotes table's own timestamp column convention used throughout this file (cross_book_at, leg_book_as_of, ...). Override before calling if some downstream consumer expects a different name, e.g. .qfwd.ts_col:`timestamp.";".qfwd.ts_col";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qfwd.col_precedence";".qfwd";"Configurable column-ordering \"precedence\" for markout-family output tables that have both a timestamp column (named per ts_col) and a sym column: those lead, in this order, whenever BOTH are present - `ts then `sym by default. A table missing either one (e.g. cross_book_chain_at_sizes's `size`sym`... shape, which has no timestamp column at all) is left in its existing column order - apply_col_precedence only ever reorders when every precedence column is actually there, never a partial reorder. This is independent from ts_col, not derived from it - update both together if you rename the timestamp column, or reordering will silently stop matching.";".qfwd.col_precedence";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qfwd.apply_col_precedence";".qfwd";"Private: move col_precedence's columns to the front of tbl, in that order, if every one of them is present in tbl - otherwise returns tbl unchanged.";".qfwd.apply_col_precedence";"");
.man.registerFunc (".qfwd.cross_markout_at_horizons";".qfwd";"Markout at one or more horizons around a single trade on a synthetic cross pair - the cross_book_at-based analogue of execution.q's markout_at_horizons, for pairs with no quoted mid of their own to as-of join against (a synthetic AUDPLN, priced by chaining whatever's in `quotes`, rather than a plain pair already sitting in a `sym`time`mid quote table). Horizons may be negative (looking backward from the trade, e.g. -500 for \"500ms before\") the same way markout_at_horizons' do; markout sign convention matches execution.q's markout.";".qfwd.cross_markout_at_horizons";".qfwd.cross_markout_at_horizons[quotes;`AUDPLN;trade_time;1;2.5650;10000;-500 -300 0 100 300;1]");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_markout_at_horizons";"param";"quotes";"table `ts`sym`bid_prices`bid_sizes`ask_prices`ask_sizes, sorted `sym`ts xasc");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_markout_at_horizons";"param";"sym";"the pair traded, any format ccy.q's normalize_ccy_pair accepts");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_markout_at_horizons";"param";"trade_time";"the trade's own timestamp");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_markout_at_horizons";"param";"side";"1 for a buy, -1 for a sell");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_markout_at_horizons";"param";"trade_price";"the execution price");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_markout_at_horizons";"param";"pip_factor";"10000 for most pairs, 100 for JPY crosses");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_markout_at_horizons";"param";"horizons_ms";"one or more offsets from trade_time, in");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_markout_at_horizons";"param";"ref_size";"the (typically negligible) size to sweep for the");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_markout_at_horizons";"return";"";"a table, one row per horizon, columns reordered by");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_markout_at_horizons";"throws";"";"error if quotes is missing a required column, isn't sorted");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_markout_at_horizons";"eg";"";".qfwd.cross_markout_at_horizons[quotes;`AUDPLN;trade_time;1;2.5650;10000;-500 -300 0 100 300;1]");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_markout_at_horizons";"eg";"";".qfwd.cross_markout_at_horizons[quotes;`AUDPLN;trade_time;1;2.5650;10000;enlist -500;1]  -> a single backward-looking horizon, 500ms before the trade");
.man.registerFunc (".qfwd.cross_markout_decomp";".qfwd";"Decompose a synthetic cross pair's price move between two times into exact per-leg contributions, by revaluing one leg at a time - in the chain's own order (cross_decomp) - from its t0 price to its t1 price, and attributing each step's resulting price change to that leg. This is exact (contribution_pips sums exactly to pip_factor*(cross_mid[t1]-cross_mid[t0]), not an approximation), but it is ORDER-DEPENDENT: attributing leg 2's move happens with leg 1 already held at its t1 price, so which leg \"gets credit\" for a move that happens to coincide with another leg's move depends on chain order - a well-known property of any sequential/waterfall-style attribution, not a bug.";".qfwd.cross_markout_decomp";".qfwd.cross_markout_decomp[quotes;`AUDPLN;t0;t1;10000;1]");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_markout_decomp";"param";"quotes";"table `ts`sym`bid_prices`bid_sizes`ask_prices`ask_sizes, sorted `sym`ts xasc");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_markout_decomp";"param";"sym";"the pair, any format ccy.q's normalize_ccy_pair accepts");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_markout_decomp";"param";"t0";"the earlier reference time");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_markout_decomp";"param";"t1";"the later reference time");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_markout_decomp";"param";"pip_factor";"10000 for most pairs, 100 for JPY crosses");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_markout_decomp";"param";"ref_size";"the (typically negligible) size to sweep for each");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_markout_decomp";"return";"";"a table, one row per leg in chain order: `leg`invert`price_t0`price_t1`contribution_pips");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_markout_decomp";"throws";"";"error if quotes is missing a required column, isn't sorted");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_markout_decomp";"eg";"";".qfwd.cross_markout_decomp[quotes;`AUDPLN;t0;t1;10000;1]");
.man.registerFunc (".qfwd.cross_impact_at_horizons";".qfwd";"Market-impact check: did a trade in traded_sym coincide with a price move in a DIFFERENT, related pair (impact_sym) around the same time? Unlike cross_markout_at_horizons (which measures the traded pair's own price drift after its own trade), this measures a sibling pair's price drift instead - signed using the traded pair's own side, so a positive markout_pips means impact_sym moved the way you'd expect if the traded pair's flow spilled over into it (e.g. buying EURPLN weakens PLN; a positive number here means CZKPLN moved the same way, i.e. PLN weakened against CZK too). There's no real trade in impact_sym, so its \"trade_price\" is its own reference price at trade_time (cross_ref_price_at), not a supplied execution price - this is a thin wrapper around cross_markout_at_horizons using that as the baseline.";".qfwd.cross_impact_at_horizons";".qfwd.cross_impact_at_horizons[quotes;`EURPLN;`CZKPLN;trade_time;1;10000;-500 -300 0 100 300;1]");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_impact_at_horizons";"param";"quotes";"table `ts`sym`bid_prices`bid_sizes`ask_prices`ask_sizes, sorted `sym`ts xasc");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_impact_at_horizons";"param";"traded_sym";"the pair actually traded, any format ccy.q's normalize_ccy_pair accepts - context only, not priced");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_impact_at_horizons";"param";"impact_sym";"the different pair to check for impact, same format rules");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_impact_at_horizons";"param";"trade_time";"the traded pair's own trade timestamp");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_impact_at_horizons";"param";"side";"1 for a buy, -1 for a sell of traded_sym - reused as impact_sym's markout sign convention");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_impact_at_horizons";"param";"pip_factor";"10000 for most pairs, 100 for JPY crosses - applies to impact_sym");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_impact_at_horizons";"param";"horizons_ms";"one or more offsets from trade_time, in");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_impact_at_horizons";"param";"ref_size";"the (typically negligible) size to sweep for");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_impact_at_horizons";"return";"";"a table, one row per horizon, columns reordered by");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_impact_at_horizons";"throws";"";"error if impact_sym normalizes to the same pair as traded_sym");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_impact_at_horizons";"eg";"";".qfwd.cross_impact_at_horizons[quotes;`EURPLN;`CZKPLN;trade_time;1;10000;-500 -300 0 100 300;1]");
.man.registerArg (".qfwd.cross_impact_at_horizons";"eg";"";".qfwd.cross_impact_at_horizons[quotes;`EURPLN;`CZKPLN;trade_time;-1;10000;enlist 300;1]  -> a sell reports the impact pair's own drift with the opposite sign");

.man.registerFile ("options.q";"";".qopt";"options.q - Garman-Kohlhagen pricing for European FX vanilla options. s = spot, k = strike, rd = domestic (quote-currency) risk-free rate, rf = foreign (base-currency) risk-free rate, sigma = volatility (decimal, e.g. 0.10 = 10%), t = year fraction to expiry. Setting rf=0 reduces the model to plain Black-Scholes. Requires stats.q (ncdf/npdf) and rates.q (df_cont) to be loaded first.");
.man.registerFunc (".qopt.d1_d2";".qopt";"Private: (d1;d2) computed together so callers never duplicate the underlying arithmetic (see src/foundation/stats.q for why that matters in q).";".qopt.d1_d2";".qopt.d1_d2[1.10;1.12;0.045;0.02;0.10;0.75]  -> 0.05174784 -0.0348547");
.man.registerArg (".qopt.d1_d2";"param";"s";"spot rate");
.man.registerArg (".qopt.d1_d2";"param";"k";"strike");
.man.registerArg (".qopt.d1_d2";"param";"rd";"domestic (quote currency) decimal annual rate");
.man.registerArg (".qopt.d1_d2";"param";"rf";"foreign (base currency) decimal annual rate");
.man.registerArg (".qopt.d1_d2";"param";"sigma";"volatility, decimal (0.10 = 10%)");
.man.registerArg (".qopt.d1_d2";"param";"t";"year fraction to expiry");
.man.registerArg (".qopt.d1_d2";"return";"";"(d1;d2)");
.man.registerArg (".qopt.d1_d2";"eg";"";".qopt.d1_d2[1.10;1.12;0.045;0.02;0.10;0.75]  -> 0.05174784 -0.0348547");
.man.registerFunc (".qopt.d1";".qopt";"The Garman-Kohlhagen d1 term.";".qopt.d1";".qopt.d1[1.10;1.12;0.045;0.02;0.10;0.75]  -> 0.05174784");
.man.registerArg (".qopt.d1";"param";"s";"spot rate");
